      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0004" n="art0004">
        <head>Vrospertus.</head>
        <p>Vrospertus.</p>
        <p>THE METROPOLITAN BANK OF ENGLAND AND WALES, LIMITED, and its Branches, are authorised to receive Subscriptions for the Five per cent. First Mortgage Debenture Stcck at par. The Subscription List tor the Debenture Stock will be opened on Saturday, Ma 7 19th. 1900. THE HOME COUNTIES POTTERIES AND BRICK WORKS, LIMITED. (Incorporated under the Companies' Acts. 1862 to 1893.) API CAL, 15,00) Ordinary £1 Shares £l5,COO 700 First Mortgage Debentures of £5O each £50,000</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>ISSUE OF</head>
        <p>ISSUE OF</p>
        <p>700 FIVE PER CENT. FIRST MORTGAGE DEBENTURE</p>
        <p>BONDS of £5) each at par.</p>
        <p>Payable— £25 on Application, and £5 on Allotment. The Debenture Stock will be a first charge on the under- taking, and be secured by a mortgage of the property now purchased by the Company. and a floating charge on ail the other assets of the Company in favour of the Debenture Swckholders, and interest will be pald half-yearly thereon on the Ist day of January end the Ist day of July, the first pay ments being apportioned up to the Ist day of January next,- Out of this issue £5,000 will be set aside for Working Capital. The articles of Association provid e that an annual sum sufficient to redeem the Debentures within 30 years shall be set aside before any dividend is payable on the Ordinary Shares. The Vendors take the whole of the Ordinary Shares in part payment of the purchase price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>DIRECTORS.</head>
        <p>DIRECTORS.</p>
        <p>Herbert Alexander, Esq. (Managing Director of Hig.her Antley Brick and Tile Works, Limited, Accrington), Rock- wood, liirkstall. near Leeds. Major John M. Gray, 21, Queen's Square, London, W.C. *Herbert Carpenter. Esq., Nyetimbr, New Malden, Surrey. *Will join the Board after Allotment. BANKERS.</p>
        <p>The Metropolitan Bank of England and Wales, Limited, Gracechurch Street, London, B.C. SOLICITOR. W. D. Fisher, Esq., 46, Queen Street, London, N.C. AUDITORS. Messrs. J. H. Byrne &amp; Co., Chartered Accountants, 81. Gra2echurch btreet, London, B.C. SECRETARY AND OFFICES (pro tern.) P. C. Vernon, 16, Great Winchester Street, B C. PEO.-3PECTUS. _</p>
        <p>- . This Company has been formed for the purpose of acquiring the valuable Freeh ,ld Property known as the Durdans Erick and Tile Works, at Southall. Middlesex, and the buildings. plant, machinery, aria equipment thereof, and of further developing the Estate by the laying down of additional plant, and by the erection of the necessary buildings to largely increase the present output. The property consists of about 35 acres o• Freehold Land, at present fitted with modern, machinery, plant, drying sheds and kilns. There are also on the freehold nine cottages, with coach-house, or waggoa shed, and stabling. The whole of this property consists entirely of valuable brick clay to a depth of 100 feet, this giving a practically inexhaustible supply of clay. The first or top 10 feet is composed of a quality of clay suit- able to the manufacture of the first-class pottery and moulded goods. The clay below this depth is a particularly good bed of strong marley clay. The yard and works have a frontage of over a quarter of a mile on the Grand Junction Canal, available tor full-sized canal barges. and a dock in the centre of the property cap- able of accommodating six barges, together with boating stages for loading and unloa Aug the same direct to and from the kilns or clamps. The value of the facilities thus alforded for dealing with the Bricks produced at the yard and the cargoes of fuel, 80„ cannot be overestimated. The property is thus in direct water communication with the West and North-Western districts and suburbs of London, and also with the whole of the riverside suburbs from Kingston downwards. Another important consideration is that the coal required, being entirely watcr-'oorne and delivered direct from the barges to the kilns, can be ol.tained at a very reduced rate. The Bricks turned out on this property command an immediate sale both on the London and Local Markets,being in constant demand owing to their superior quality. The Estate is exceptionally well prodded with water,there being a large reservoir on the property. The Estate is at present equipped with plant of a very com- plete character, capable of pro iucing 10 million Bricks per annum, and it is proposed, by the addition of further plant, to increase the capacity of the yard to 509,000 Bricks weekly, which. allowing for stoppages, bad weather, and holidays, may be reasonably caleatatad at 29 millions per annum. The whole of the propellty has been examined by Mr. Herbert Alexander, of Leeds, the well-known Brick manu- facturing Engineer and Valuer, who reports as follows : Queen's Engineering Works. Leeds, 14th February. 19CO. To the Directors of THE HOME COUNTIES POTTERIES AND BRICK WORKS, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>Gentlemen. In accordance with your instructions, I have carefully inspected the Durdans'Brick Works at Southall, Middlesex, The property is situated on the Paddington Canal (to which it has a frontage of over a quarter of a mile). about 10 miles frem London, and is also about one mile from the main line of the Great Western Railway Station at Soutkaa. It is within easy carting distance from Baling, and is in the heart of a rapidly growi,g district, extensive building operaticns being in progress all round. ' The area of the property which I understand is freehold, comprises about 35 acres. There is 10 feet of good top obey, which is suitable for making first-claes pottery and moulded , goods, and form this stratum is produced an excellent red ' moulded facing brick, which commands a ready sale at high prices, Underlying this pottery clay there is a particularly ' good bed of strong marley clay, which has been tested to a depth of 100 feet. I consider this bed of clay at being ef exceptional value, as after a careful teat. I am cpilte satisfied that it could advan- tageously be treated on the latest methods of brick making. In my opinion this clay can be treated ill the most economical and modern process of brick-making, viz .—The Stiff Plastic Process, whereby a Plastic Pressed Brick is produced, which can be set direct in the kiln, thus saving the cost of intermediate ,rying and drying sheds, which will effect a great economy. There is a brickmaking plant with a capacity of about 35,000 wire cot bricks per day, driven by an excellent and powerful engine and boiler. There is a large reservoir on the property, which conserves an ample supply of water for all purposes. The present cost of pr 's rtion is about 18s per 1,000 Whilst the supply has never the local demand. Calculating upon the known depth of the clay, and the depth at which it can be profitably worked, I estimate that the property will yield over 35,0(0,030 of bricks per acre, so that even with the pros, .secl increase of the output, there are over 40 years of work in w. The buildings covering ,he machinery are of a very sub- stantial character, and exceedingly well built., There is an excellent run of drying sheds, nineteen of which are heated by hot air, and two by steam. There are five kilns with a capacity of 45.000 to 50,000 each. The engines main and auxiliary, are all in good order, as are the brick machines ballast and mortar milL. and the works are well equipped with all the necessaries 01. a brick yard. There is on the propeet. 'also a terrace of nine cottages in excellent order and *pa,: and a good four-stall stable and coach or waggon shed, with living rooms over. There is also a dock leading direct into the Paddington branch of the Grand Juncsion Canal, about. a mile from the junction with the main canal. The dock which is r quipped with two loading boats, will accommodate six barges, and thus give direct access toy the cheaper system of carriage,not only direct to the helot of London at the Paddington Doeke, but also to all the riverside suburbs from Kingston down- wards.</p>
        <p>It is difficult to over-estimate the value of this dock and the frontage on the canal in dealing with the output which this yard should produce, and I regard it as a most important fa itor.</p>
        <p>Valuation.—l estimate the value of the clays on this property at I value the whole of the buildings, kilns, drying sheds, cottages, stthles, offices, dock, machinery and plant, itc.</p>
        <p>Total £47,700</p>
        <p>Your. faithfully. (Signed) HERBERT ALEXA.NDER.</p>
        <p>Contracts are in hand an I in negotiation which practically take the entire output of resent plant for the rising year. With tne rapid increase ,1 building now going on in and</p>
      </div>
