      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE, / am certainly of the opinion that the artificia flowers used in millinery this spring are unusuall3 beautiful and artistic. Some nasturtiums in rick al-ivies of orange-red, on a hat of ecru satin stram the other day, were wonderfully realistic in botk form and hue. The hat was a very pretty one, with a rather high crown and moderately wide brim, veiled with folds of ecru tulle, on which the ziasturtiums were laid; three flat folds of orange. red velvet were sewn round the underside of the brim, which was slightly tilted to show a nastur- tium or two and a rosette of tulle nestling beneath it. Grapes, cherries,, and currants are much in request as hat-trimmings, and look their best on the picturesque shapes of coarsely-woven ecru, biscuit, and cream straw which are so fashionable at present. Velvet cherries have nothing realistic about them, but are much worn in hats and toques at present. Currants, red and green, are also in favour, as well as plums of every possible shade of purple and claret colour. Ivy berries and variegated ivy leaves are the materials of one of the pretty foliage toques now so much in vogue. Mignonette, which seems always half foliage, half flower, it be Cf.... 1 dand for these toques.</p>
        <p>It is as well always to keep in mind that the front of the cloth part of the bodice should be slightly full, and finished with a narrow band of jetted trimming. If a smart tucked shirt of glace silk in colour, either plain turquoise or violet, is bought or made, a pretty costume will result. The glace shirt should be finely tucked and finished with a collar of the silk turned over at the top of a high neckband and edged with a tiny frill. Boleros and zouaves are likely to be much worn over the bodices of house gowns this summer. They are very small indeed, reaching only just below the bust, but their diminutive size is made tip for by the elaboration of their trimmings. For the foundation material silk, satin and velvet are all popular, but so profusely are these tiny coats ornamented with lace and embg9idery that not much of this is rirdbla</p>
        <p>Sprigged muslin or sprigged net seems to be a favourite material. Nothing could look fresher or tlaintier than spotted net, whether worn over silk or satin, or over chiffon or muslin under-skirts. A. very pretty dress I saw had small bows of inch- wide-silk ribbon &amp;Andy tied, and then sewn rather olosely over the whole of the skirt, each bow being placed at a different angle from that at which its immediate neighbours were set. The ribbon ruch- ings that border many skirts are an old fashion revived. Such ruchings trimmed the short, tight gowns that were worn years ago; but elaborate tasekings and pipings and stitchings are out of piace. Of a lady's dress I saw yesterday the cor- sage and petticoat were of white poult de soie, with a swathed belt of white chiffon, and a train of mousse green velvet hanging in soft folds from the dicolleta.ge behind, with begoaia leaves laid on the edge at intervals ald on the. shoulders.</p>
        <p>Bailor hat are higher in the crown and narrower in the brim than of yore, and a new idea is to re. place the familiar ribbon band with one of leither, with straps and buckles at the side. The loather is in any shade desired, and has rather a smart effect with tailor gowns. A lady wearing a hat of blue straw, with hydrangeas nestling amongst the pinkish chiffon, had a dress in pastel pink voile, the corsage swathed prettily across the figure, with an old lace jabot, which was caught down by buckles de l'art nouveau. The same buckles catch he guipure on the sleeves and the knots of lace on the skirt, a black ribbon running through them all round the skirt.</p>
        <p>Your loving,</p>
        <p>JENNIE</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
