      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>HALF A CENTU</head>
        <p>HALF A CENTU</p>
        <p>ESTAI3 LISI-tED</p>
        <p>I,A.F:_aawr PIANO Eie ORGAN</p>
        <p>FIRM I</p>
        <p>readers of The Warrington Examiner.</p>
        <p>Organs and Harmoniums, 5/- per month.</p>
        <p>CRANE AND SONS, LIMITE</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Dowager Duchess of Wellington, who is lying very seriously ill at hsr seat in Surrey, was at one time Mis- tress of the Robes and the Queen with her customary thoughtfulness and concern for all who bave sersel her has caused freq :eat ,i,quiries to be made troce the com- mencement of the Dueneta'o indaspeaition. Dunne the whole of her long riden, cut Sovereign has only had ten Mistresses of the Robes. The firgit wemen appointed to this, the highest post one of our sex can hold in the Royal flousehld, was Harriet, Dechess of Satherlin I, who was three times Mistress of the Itches. In 1335, the Duchess of Buecteugh wee made llarsneeis of the Rebel; and she agaiu. at the prevent trme. fills this important office. The Duchess is quite the moor, ox- elusive woman in London. and her eeteiteinmente are earned out in truly magnificent style. Montagu House, , Whitehall, contains a very fine hall and staircase, but its chief glory is the unique ganery of pictures, male from the brash of Lely, Vendyck and Cenoleatte. The Prince of Wales never nais!s an opportunity of supporting any proposal for the alteration of the lacy with regard to marriage with a deceased wile's sister. This question has aeain been raited in the House of ! Lords, from a Colonial standpoint, when LrJ Strath- , cona's Bill was read a second time the other day, by a ! majority of 85, which included the Prince of Wales, the Duke of Connaught, and the Duke of York. • The Bill seeks to remove a crying grievance by making valid in this country those marriages which are lawfully con- tracted in the Colonies with a deceased wite's sister. Such unions have long been legalised in the self-govern- ing Colonies, with the assent of the green in Council and Imperial Government, and they are so recognised all over the world,except, strange to say,in this the mother- country. Surely,thie is the strangest of all anomalies, espocially when, just now we are all tal'zing so much of a United &amp;moire. It is really time that Parliament was compelled by the preFsure of public opinion to deal once and for all with the deceased wife's sister. Tee circumstarces under which such unions aver° made illegitimate in 1835, were nothing better than a scandal and disgrace to the Government of the time, and, as long as most people can remember, the question has been bandied about hetween the two Henses of Parliament, though it is cer• tam n that public opinion is overwhelmingly in favour of an alteration of the law. The Colonial griever es has also been in the same boat. It is infamous that the law of this country should not recognise as valid, these marriages which it has declared to be legal in the Colin:dm Yet for twenty years or more repeated attempts have ben made in Parliament to alter this state of tables without success. Two years ago, the House of Lords unanimously passed a similar measure to Lord Strathconane but it came to the ground between the two Hoeses of Parliament, and in all proba- bility a similar fate awaits the present Bid. The representation of the Passion Play, now being performed at Ober Aminergau, is supposed to dat3 back as far as the 12th century, though ita origin is a little obscure and doubtful. It is known to have been to. vived at the time of the "Black Death" which devastated Europe in 1634, when the terrified people promised to perform the play regularly, in future if they were pre- served from the plague. Until fifty or sixty years ago the audience eonaisted mainly of Blearier. peasants from , the neighbourine %Wages. It is still chiefly of German ! origin, but people now flock to Ober Ammergau from all ! parts of the world. The new theatre, with the vast ttage partly open to wind and weather, which has just been completed, will hold several thousand people, the price of admission varying from two to ten marks. From a dramatic point A view the performance has been brought to the highest State of perfection, though to many people's thinking it is open to objection upon religious grounds. The play represents the period from the entry of Christ into Jerusalem to His Crucifixion and death, and with inter- vals for rest and refreshment, it oe,cupies from eiglit to nine hours. The National Bazaar succeeded in raising the larger part of the expected £50,000, and, though it did not reach the total. it has established a record as the biggest effort of the kind ever made in London. Mrs. and Miss Baden-Powell took ma active part among the stall-holders in selling for the regiments with which the hero of Mafeking was formerly connected. Much of the takings was of course given for charity's sake. In the American Bar, the price of a " Beer Paralyser" or " Badea-Poweil Smile "was net excessive, but there was the important legend " Please tip the waitress." The extra day,whiclo the bazaar was kept open, was the most amusing, as it was then that the unsold articles were disposed of by auction. The amateur auctioneers rivalled one auother in prefeosional attainments but in spite of their undoubted skill in this direction, many articles went for comparatively next to nething, and no doubt feeling that enough had been done for charity, many people seemed proud of the bargains they had secured- Black and white was undoubtedly the wear at the National Bazaar. Gowns were generally of white muslin, or soft silk, insertioned with lace, built with a train,and transparent yoke aad sleeves: With these gowns, hugs black picture hats prevailed, providbog an excellent foil to the daiuty white frocks. Of neck ruff! le there was quite an epidemic—tbe majority of these confections being arranged of net muslin or tulle, edged with ribbon loops of chenille,or flowers—:he violet evidently standing first favourite. The yoke is a groat: feature of Vie newest bleuseeand there is some sign of the reappearance of the box, pleat up the front. This latter is an item calculated to rejoice</p>
        <p>21 %. 1/6 2/, 86 at Lengths</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>~ LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>~ LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>the heaes of all povessors of choice or antiq as hu'- tens. Onyx specimens have enjoyed a considerable vogue of late, also rocc)co buttons, made in curious pat- terns of twisted gold jewelled with turqulises or garnets. Old paste buttons are very much en evidence too, and many seaman have lately been giving large prices for antique buttons called from old Georgian coats. For many years Gaelic services bas been held'An Crowa-coart Church, for the henefi; of Flighlandus who live in London. The services are held once every three months and are conducted alternately by ministers of the Eatabished and Free Churches of Scotand. They are always well attended, but it seems the services scarcely pay their way, and an appeal for help in made to all who are interesed in their continuance. Mr. Dahald Murray who has been chiefly instrumental in organising the services, wit gladly acknowledge any eontributions addressed to him at 15 Crown-Court, Covent Garden, London.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WARRINGTON HARRIERS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>Annual Amateur Athletic Sports.</p>
        <p>Beautiful weather prevaile 1 on Saturday afternoon when the first annual amateur athletic sports in connec- tion with the Werrineton Harriers were held on the Warrington football ground, Wilderspool-roeal. There was however, only a small attendance of the public. The entries numbered over 250, the compstitore includ- ing reveral well-known athletes from different part; of the country. The proeramme consisted of seven events which were well contested. There were three successful local competitors, in the open evente—J. Merrier of the Sankey Harriers, whe came in first in the 100 yards flat handicap, R Righarn, el Warrington, who carried off first prize in the one mile novice bicycle handicap, and W Bolt, Warrington Harriers, who came in second in the one mile flat race, P.C. Robinson, a local runner of repute entered several of the races, but was heavily handicapped, and tbis no doubt prevented him from achieving success. The tus of war between the War- rineton Police and Fleming's resulted in an easy vietory for the police. Tee prizes were hand- some and valuable, and were supplied by I Messrs. E. A. Eustance sad Company. They were presented to the winners at the conclusion of the racing by Councillor Tinnion. In doing so he said he regretted that the sports nad not been better patronised by the puttlie. He had always taken considerable interest in the Warrington Harriers and he was sure they wore a credit to the tewn. The sports heel boon very enjeyable and reflected credit oe the cernenittee.—The prlzes were then distributed, the successful competit,ors being loudly cheered by the spectatore. The following were the officials : Judges, Messrs. W Dillon and T Bowers ; marksmen. Wears. W. Price, H Melbourne, T Smith, T Billinghem. W Hewitt and J Chambers; Proreseional starter, Mr. Thomas Wain- wright, handicapper Mr. Walt?r Platt, lion treasurer, Mr G Anderton, referee, Councillor D Tinnioo hornsecia- tary, Mr. W B Smith. Appended is the list of successful competitere :- 220 Yards Members Flat Race.—J Craeidock.l6 yards 1; A Ford, 16 yards, 2 ; F Brutal, 5 yards, 3. 100 Yards Flat Handicap (epen).—J Mercer, Sankey Harriers, 9 yards, 1; W Baroett, Altrinebtien, 9 yards, 2 ; R McCracken, Liverpool, 61 yards, 3. Elalf Mile Members Flat Race. Haneicep.—J Mather. 30 yards, 1 ; R Melbourne, 33 yards, 2 ; R Price, 50 yards, 3. Works Tug of War (Werringten Police v• Messrs. Flernine'a).—Police won easily. 440 Yards Open Flat flandicap.—J McCracken. Liver- pool, 29 yards, 1 ; R Waning, Widnes, 34 yards, 2 ; W Barnett, Altrincham, 31 cards. 3. One Mile Novice Bicycle Hennicap (eperi).—R Sieben) Warrington, 105 yarde, 1 ; El Washineton, Middlewich, 110 yards. 2 ; 0 Atherton, Present, 115 yards. 3. One Mile Flat Race Handicap (epen).---F W Webber, Sutton Harriers, 90 y iris, 1 ; W Het, Warrington, 120 yards, 2 ; J Morris, Crewe, 97 yards, 3.</p>
        <p>Shopbreaking. At Liverpool C':upt:" teuions, on Tueslay, William Harris (21). labourer, and Elizabeth Cun- liffe (21), tailoress, w,re indicted for feloniously breaking and ente7ing the shoo of Peter Urundy, at St. Helene, on the 18th of April, and stealing two alarm clocks, two gentlemen's geld rings, twelve ladies' gold rings, two silver alberts, and a quantity of other goods.—Cunliffe pleaded guilty, and the other prisoner not gutlty.—Mr. Rigby Swift'prose- cuted, and in hi A op,tning statement, said that on the night of the 17th of April, the prisonera were under the observation or the police, and about twelve o'clock, the sergeant who was watching them, lost sight of them. He saw them again later, and found articles of jewellery upon them, which were afterwards founl to have been stolen from the shop.—Cunliffe was sent to gall for six months with hard labour, she having a long tecord of °rime against her. The other prisoner, wits had not been previously convicted, was sent to priton for three months.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 1 1900</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>(Copyright.) STEVE THE OUTLANDER.</head>
        <p>(Copyright.) STEVE THE OUTLANDER.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR LAYCOCK, Author of "The Young Gaffer," "Seaside Sketches," "Lancashire Sketches," &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>CHAPTER XXIX,</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXIX,</p>
        <p>Ralph Cartleigh was conducted by the Secret Service man to the office of the local Field Cornet, and, having taken the oath of allegiance to the Transvaal Republic before this official, he was furnished with a Mauser rifle and a well-stocked bandolier.</p>
        <p>A few days later he proceeded by rail to the Natal border in company with the Irish Brigade and the rest of the Johannesburg contingent.</p>
        <p>At Volksrust a halt was 'nada, arid the raw recruits were put through a severe course of drill by the German officers. The hotel near the station where Ralph Cartleigh had lost all his money several months previously, was besieged by the Johannesburgers, but the wily Boers bad closed the place before their allies arrived. The baffled troopers, especially the 'Frisco Bar section, were loud in their complainings, and assumed a most threatening demeanour. _</p>
        <p>A few wcirds from the -Boer- commandant soon appeased them, however. "Men," he said, "we wish to do all that we can for your comfort and well-being so long as you give us your valuable aid, but we cannot allow you to go into action drunk. We burghers have never done it, nor can you do it and hold your own against our enemy. They are a strong, a powerful enemy, the British, and we must face them soberly. When the fighting is over, when we have won the victory, you can do as you wish." "Hooray for the victory ! Let us have our fling at the loot and We'll win your battles!" sthouted the men.</p>
        <p>The force continued on its journey, crossed the Drackensburg mountains, and on the following afternoon approached Dundee. Here, as the Johannesburgers detrained, they heard the booming of cannon, the rat-tat of musketry, and the rattle of Maxims. The battle of Glencoe was in progress, and the Boers were being worsted in the combat. But the reverse did not wholly demoralise the burghers, for that night they rallied anew, and, encouraged by the constant addition to- their numbers, they strongly entrenched themselves on the heights above Elands Laagte and awaited the dawn of another day of terrible carnage. After a long and weary tramp over hilly country the Johannesburg troop reached the Boer laager, and having rested and partaken of food they whiled away the small hours of the morning in singing ribald songs. The solemn burghers of the Standerton Commando, who were quartered near, frowned at the levity of their Outlander companions. "They don't know what war is," said a burly, brown-bearded fellow. "They don't know what it is or they'd be praying to-night instead of blaspheming and singing wild songs." "They'll know soon enough, Paul," said another burgher, who was working at the entrenchments. "We'll have the English to fight again in a few hours, then these men will know what war is." "I don't think they will, Jan Schultz. They'll desert us, you'll see, at the last moment. I want none of your Englishmen or your English-speaking men, call them Irish, American, what you will. They are no good. They are cowards; they'll desert ns. Brave men don't sing the devil's songs, Jan Schultz, when they go to battle; they humbly pray to God Almighty." As day broke and the burghers rested from their labours the rowdy, irreverent Johannesburgers gazed in astonishment as the veld-farmers fell upon their knees and the predicant of the com- mando raised his hands to Heaven.</p>
        <p>"I didn't know that I was coming to a prayer- meeting, Percy," a 'Frisco Bar man whispered' in Cartleigh's ear. "No, nor I, old man. I thought it was the Boer army we had. joined, but it's the Salvation Army seemingly." "All but the drum and the tamboutine. We only want the 'boom' and the clang 'to complete the thing." The "boom" and "clang" were instantly forth- coming as the Nordenfeldt guns to the left of the camp belched forth in thunderous roar. Instantly the laageewas alive with excitement and bustle.</p>
        <p>"That's our salvation salute to the enemy," said Cartleigh's companion. "See how it's spoilt the prayer-meeting." Along the mountain ranges the scouts came flying in. "The British are coming 1 The rooineks are coming!" Again the big guns bayed hoarsely, and the screaming shells hurtled through the air for miles, dropping perilously near the advancing forces in the valley below. Commandants and Field Cornets galloped hither and thither giving orders. From the valley came the answering roar of the British artillery. Battle was certain. _Elands Laagte would become historic. Above, the sun shone radiant through fleecy clouds. Peace above, war below. The hiss and shriek of shrapnel now alternated with rifle reports as the outposts and sharpshooters on the ridges exchanged shots with the British advanced forces. Then on the long ridge across the valley appeared solid, moving columns, advancing steadily, compactly forward. _ _</p>
        <p>Cartleigh and his comrades were located at the foot of a sugar-loaf kopje in the centre of the Boer laager, hemmed in on each side by burgher com- mandos, the Standerton troop being on the left. As the British army came well into view on the opposite ridge four thousand yards distant, and the terrrible shells from their artillery fell all over the hill-face, the Johannesburg contingent became rest- less with fear, and several men hastily retreated. "Stand to your positions?" roared the Com- mandants, forcing them back. "Stand to your positions or we'll shoot you I There's no retreating slow," Cowering behind the rocks and boulders the cowed men tremblingly awaited their fate, many scarce daring to look out at the ever advancing foe. .Acroßs the wide plain the khaki-coloured masses moved on, their artillery momentarily becoming more destructive, silencing for a time one of the big Nordenfeldt guns to the left of the Boer camp. The German gunners were repeatedly driven from their positions, but doggedly returned to the attack, though their ranks grew thinner each time, and the mounds of dead and wounded about them grew higher. The afternoon was passing; the opposing armies were gradually nearing each other. Soon the final tussle must come. The Boers stubbornly held their ground, but their Outlander allies wavered, and again sought to retreat, but were kept to their positions by the threats and acts of the Field Cornets, who drew their revolvers and shot certain of the flying fugitives. , Looking out from behind his huge boulder Ralph Cartleigh swept the plain below and saw dotted all over its surface masses of the enemy—his country- men. For a moment a feeling of shame came upon him as be realised that he was fighting on the wrong side, fighting against his Queen and country. He was sober enough to-day, and could think calmly and collectedly despite the distracting whistle and shriek of bullets and shells which were</p>
        <p>whirling about him. "My country right or wrong, I should be fighting with it, not against it," he reflected. "But," as a piercing shriek rang on his ear, "I have sworn to fight for the Boers, and, by Heaven! I'll keep my word!" The wounded Outlander at his right hand shrieked aloud again, then fell forward dead, with his face to the advancing fv. Again panic seized the Outlander contingent as the terrible fire of the British guns and musketry pitted the hill-face with wounded and dead men. "You have given your word, keep it now. You might as well die like men as dogs," Cartleigh shouted to his comrades. "Bravo, Percy! Bravo Fighting Percy I " cried the more courageous 'of the men. "Bravo, Englishman, bravo!" bellowed Paul Schultz. The scared fellows thus rallied, returned reluctantly to their positions, and, fixing their Mausers on ant-hills or rocks, aimed as best they could at the dreaded brown figures ever approach- ing nearer and nearer. Then came a fearful deluge of rain, stabbing, piercing drops, blotting out enemy, friends, hill- side, rocky ridge, everything. Through it the guns thundered, and the Maxims rattled incessantly. A murderous hail of lead and iron followed, and groaning aoacl shrieking was heard on all sides. "The British are on us! They're here 0 God! Fly!" To their horses flei the burghers, scampering headlong, heeding not the shouts and curses of their leaders and their few brayer comrades.</p>
        <p>—dtanct t otana to your grouna, cowards " roared Paul Schultz. "Jan! Pretorius Where ~re you ? "</p>
        <p>For answer came a terrible. roar of artillery, and the big Boer, who had been waving his arms aloft, fell back as a shrapnel shell burst in his face, shattering his right hand. He was up again in a moment, rifle in his left hand, waiting the onslaught of the hated enemy. "Courage, Paul 1 " came a voice through the blackness. "I'm here and little Pretorius. We will stand! Ah! he is down!"</p>
        <p>- _ The voice was lost in the deafening roar, as the Boer force was overwhelmed by the hacking, slash- ing Gordons, the Dragoons, and the Light Horse. For half-an-hour the hand-to-hand fight was desperately waged oi7er the heights of Elands Laagte, then the main body of the Boers fell back utterly routed. At the foot of the little kopje, a small band held out stubbornly, contesting every yard with the invincible foe.</p>
        <p>Stephen Hart, with the dismounted Light Horse, rushed madly on this position defended so obstinately by a mere handful of men. "Charge " cried the officers, and, as the stirring bugles rang out, Manchesters, Gordons, and Light Horse rose up as one man and stormed the kopje. A moment of awful slaughter, a moment of shrieks, yells, curses, and groans, and not one Boer was left standing. The ground was strewn with dead and dying. Stephen Hart stood dazed in the midst of the carnage. As yet he could not fully comprehend what had taken place. One minute before he had dashed headlong into the storm of fire and lead with fixed bayonet, and he dimly recollected im- paling a man with a white face. Oh such a white face. It would haunt him for ever. The man fell with a curse and—he remembered no more, only that now that white face was gazing at him through the dark shadows. Already the ambulance men were at work amongst the Boers. Their lamps lighted up the gruesome scene. Stephen Hart closed his eyes to blot out the horrible sight. But he could not close his ears. "Paul Paul!" cried a voice at his side. A faint answering moan came from a big Boer doubled up, still clutching his Mauser. "Paul t The Englishman kept his word 1 " and Jan Schultz inclined his head backward where, by a huge boulder, lay the man whose face still haunted Steve. Steve stepped forward to the rock, and, as a lantern was brought up, peered into the face of the Englishman, who still grasped his Boer weapon. "Ralph Cartleigh! " he gasped. "Yes, that was him. That was his name,", said Jan Schultz, overhearing the exclamation. "He was an Englishman, and he kept his word. Tell Paul; tell Paul there that the Englishman kept his word," and Jan Schultz indicated the big Boer to the right. But Paul was dead. fight. "My little Pretorius! Ah, where is he ? " cried Jan Schultz, staring wildly about him. "Ah, where is he? Bring him to me; I cannot move. Englishman, bring him to me. You will. We are all friends now. Englishman, you are kind, good fellows. Bring my little Pretorius to me. He is but fourteen; he is but a child! Oh, bring my boy to me! " Steve groped around among the prostrate bodies, and was bending over the dead form of a boy when a bullet shot by a wounded Boer, penetrated his right shoulder, and he fell with a groan. Then through the gloom came the' searchers and ambulance men again, and a tall, gaunt figure stood over him. "Bearers here!" A stretcher was brought and Steve was quickly placed upon it. "No," he said, valiantly, "not me; it's not my turn. lam not hurt much. Stop the bleeding and I can walk. Take him! Take him!" The stretcher-bearers were passing Jan Schultz. "Take him! " cried Steve. "He is badly wounded." The men halted a moment. The tall man came up coughing gutturally. • "Go forward! Time is precious." "Yes. Leave me 1; said Jan, "I shall not live, I shall die. Only let me see my little Pretorius before I die. He fell, over there!" Steve was conveyed to the little hospital at the foot of the hill. The tall man sought amongst the dead and wounded for the Boer's son. "He is here, I think," he said to one of his fellow-searchers. The man took up the lad's body and conveyed it to the fast-expiring burgher. "Ah, yes, it -is he. Pretorius! My little Pretorius l'He is dead; he is dead!" and, hugging the loved form to his bosom, Jan Schultz expired. "And this is war!" exclaimed Barnard, the con- sumptive, for such was the tall man. "0 God! I can stand no more of it! It is too terrible. It is too terrible! " He sank upon a rock, burying his face in his hands and sobbing aloud. "Get down to the hospital, manl" cried the doctor, sternly. "You are not It to be-here. This work will kill you before the night is out. You aught never to have come!" Barnard rose wearily and his sad eyes surveyed the bloody hillside, made more terrible by the fitful light of the flickering lanterns. "It was madness for you to come with us on such an errand," said the doctor. "Get down to the hospital at once, Barnard." But the gaunt consumptive braced himself and again set out on his mission of mercy, giving water to the fainting sufferers who could not for a time be conveyed to shelter. Anon he bound up a shattered limb and gave temporary relief to a grateful soul. Boer and Briton were alike to him. He was the friend, the brother of all. But nature gave way at last, and the brave spirit which had so miraculously sustained the frail consumptive throughout this night of horrors, flickered feebly and the gaunt form fell to the sodden, bloody earth. When Barnard awoke to consciousness, he was lying on a rude bed alongside a wounded trooper whose face was turned towards him. The two men gazed at each other in silence for a considerable time. At length Stephen Hart spoke. "Aren't you the ambulance man who found me out ? " "Yes—l do not know. Ilhave seen many faces these last few dreadful hours. I know your voice, though I cannot see your face; the light is dim. Speak again, I know your. voice, you are—" "Stephen Hart. And you are--?" "Barnard," answered the other, feebly. "Ah! I knew it. I felt it from the first, but I could not see you, and the Boer cried so pitifully. Then they brought me down here and dressed my wound, and I slept and—dreamt, yes, I dreamt, Barnard, that it was you. And sure it is, and4you, are lying here by me. You are wounded ? " "No, only the old wound. But it is mortal, not _like yours. You w 1 'iv, I shall die." "No, no, not id. -.ton must not. Oh, I ,want to shake your hand, old man." Steve groaned in pain as he strove to raise his; arm. "No, I sa ;it is useless. I must look at you, I must look at you long, Barnard." "Not for long, old man. I'm going:soon." "Oh, what have you done? Why have you come here to be amongst this awful bloodshed?" "It was my choice," answered Barnard, his voice becoming fainter each moment. "I knew I had not long to live, but I resolved to fight ray only enemy, death, to the last. I would fight him ere he stole the life-blood from the wounded, such as you. I knew that I could be of some service to the ambulance. I have some knowledge of surgery. I joined the Free State Red Cross band. The doctor refused"to take me at first, but I insisted on accompanying the forces. Last night word was want to our camp that there would be urgent need of ambulance men here to-day. I volunteered to come, but was again refused a place on the waggon. But I got through, followed our m9n on horse-back and arrived • just when yotir company with the Gordons and Devons had swept through the Boer camp. Afid how all is over. I have fought death as best I could. But it is terrible I It is terrible!" The consumptive fell back on•his pillow com- pletely exhausted. The doctor came along and looked gravely into his face as he held the thin, bony wrist. Barnard opened his eyes and a smile of' peaceful resignation overspread his ghastly visage. "Never mind me, doctor. Look to the other poor fellows," he gasped. "They'll—be—worth—some- thing in—the future. My—work—is—done. Thank God ! " And the eyes closed for 'ever; and the smile was still upon the noble face.</p>
        <p>He had fought his last</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CITAPTER XXX.</head>
        <p>CITAPTER XXX.</p>
        <p>Henry Cartleigh, the English missionary at ITinhlana, stood on the Elands Laagte heights gazing sunward where the mighty orb was lifting its blood- red face above the ridges to look on a bloodier battlefield, the scene of a night of horrors.</p>
        <p>ane pale, trouotect race, tne sorrowtut eyes, the tremulous mouth, which ever and anon murmured a broken prayer, betokened overpowering grief. Throughout the long, dreadful night Henry Cart- leigh had laboured with the doctors and ambulfmee men, and the harrowing sights he had witnessed bad well-nigh prostrated him. Now for a brief space he had left the wounded living and stood amongst the dead, who lay about him on every hand, wrapped in eternal peace. "A few hours ago they rose in strength and might to answer the last bugle they would hear on earth, now they will rest until the last trump shall sound and call them to Heaven." _</p>
        <p>So mused the missionary as, standing with bare head and folded hands, he looked upon the slain, friend and foe side by side, all quiet and at rest. A burying-party on the side of the kopje attracted his attention. He slowly walked towards the men. They were a detachment of the Gordons '7ing slain comrades. uietly and solemnly they bent to their task, Gigging a deep trench and tenderly laying therein the stiff, stark forms. The grave being full the men turned involuntarily to the sad-eyed missionary who offered up a heart- felt prayer, then tearfully turned away as the last clods were thrown on the gallant soldiers' grave. " 'Tis the last, best service we can render to the poor fellows," he said, "to give them decent burial, and have the graves dug by those nearest and dearest to them. . _ _ _</p>
        <p>He came to the spot where the Boers had made their last endeavour to combat the overpowering foe. Here young, stalwart lads, sons of the veld, lay by their long-bearded sires, stretched at full length, others were half crouched behind boulders, some in a sitting or kneeling posture, all with the terrible death look on their livid faces. Beyond were a few allies, Outlanders who had fought with the Bows. Horror, intense fear, was depicted on their features. Surely some of these men must have died from fright ere the slashing, hacking enemy dashed upon them Henry Cartleigh looked with pity into these scared faces. What dreadful torture these poor fellows must have endured ere death closed their eyes and numbed their senses! How different were those faces from this one—he stood before the bent form of Paul Schultz. Here was the face of a brave man, victorious even in death, meeting his fate heroically. And this other face, too, this of :le Boer who is clasping the dead fbrm of his son; pain and harrowing grief are on those ruggeil features, yet resignation is there also. That man died believing he ha:d done his duty. Ah ! And here is yet another Outlander face, not of a craven either—a proud face, the white lips curling with a sneer. This man laughed at death, laughed at the gleaming blade which pierced his heart! And this is an Englishman, but fighting with a Boer weapon I Who can he be, this man who met death so lightly? Henry Cartleigh bent over the prostrate figure' and, pushinc, back the broad-brimmed hat, peered into the proud, sardonic face. "Ralph! Ralph! My brother " he cried. "0 Good I And this-is the end!"</p>
        <p>. _ Long he remained kneeling before the stricken form, overpowered with grief. Once a Highlander from the burying party approached him, but per- ceiving his condition withdrew quietly and joined his comrades, who covered up the bodies without a prayer being offered over them. The sun was high in heaven ere the missionary rose to his feet again. He walked over to the Highlanders and borrowed a spade, then proceeded to dig a grave near to the spot where his brother had fallen.</p>
        <p>A number of Boers bearing a Red Cross flag came near to him and commenced digging trenches wherein to bury their dead. They stared wonderingly as they saw the English predicant wield his spade, and one of their number came forward and volunteered to assist Henry in his task. _ _</p>
        <p>"Thank you," he said, gratefully. "I prefer to dig the grave myself. He was my brother," and he pointed to the body at his side. ".And these were our brothers," answered the Boer, indicating the many dead forms around. "Yes, to be sure," said the missionary, tremu- lously; "they were our brothers, too. We are all brothers, Boers and Britons. We are all God's children. Why did we fight ? " "I do not know." "Nor I." "I will help you then ? " "Yes, and I will help you. We will bury each other's dead and pray for each other' dead. "And for the living," said the Boer. "And for the living," said Henry Cartleigh. The graves were completed, the large one wherein the slain burghers were placed, and the small one in which the slain Englishman was entombed. The Boer assisted Henry to lift Ralph's body into the little trench, then, standing midway between the two graves, the missionary clasped his hands in prayer, the Boers gathered round, and a group of stalwart Highlanders reverently stepped forward. and joined the little throng. An impressive, touching prayer it was, coming from the inmost depths of a loving, forgiving, tender nature. At its close Britons and Boers filled up the graves, then shaking hands these brothers in sorrow each departed their various ways. From the battle- field Henry Cartleigh proceeded to the field hospitals where the Red Cross flags were flying. Here he discovered that the wounded were being removed as speedily as possible to Ladysmith. He therefore jounieyed to the little town with the hospital train and learnt on his arrival there that Sir George White was advising all non-combatants to proceed at once to Pietermaritzburg. The General had already given instructions .for the wounded to be conveyed thither. "If Ladysmith is likely to be besieged by the Boers," said Henry Cartleigh, "I shall remain here, as there will be much work for me to do. There will be sick and wounded soldiers who will require attention. I will help your hospital staff. The duty of a minister of the Gospel it appears to me is to remain here." So he remained behind. On the station platform a crowd of townsfolk, with many refugees from Johannesburg, who had arrived in the town a few days previously, awaited the train which should convey them beyond the theatre of war. A few carriages containinc, wounded soldiers from the Elands'Laagte field hospitals were drawn up near the platform, and certain of the assembled people conversed with the suffering patients. Stephen Hart, reclining on a Fairly comfortable bed, was intently watching a man who was chatting with a comrade in the next bed. "Seems to me I've seen that man somewhere before," mused Steve, as he took stock of the man's aniform. "Ah, to be sure, I remember. He was the Captain of the Salvation Army at Johannesburg. Queer it is how we meet folks in this country.. I wonder how that girl went on who was struck by Cartleigh." Then, as he still gazed at the Salvationist, he recalled his first meeting with his old enemy in Johannesburg, and his still later meeting, not twenty hours ago, on the heights of Elands Laagte. This last recollection was too painful to dwell upon, and he again reverted to the Johannesburg incident. What had become of the girl who had been so brutally struck that night? He had looked for her in the Salvationist ring, but never had she appeared again. He had been tempted more than once to ask the Captain about her; then he had rebuked himself for a fool. Why should he trouble to ask about the girl? What was she to him? What was any woman to him? But ever again he fell a-wondering about the Salvation lass. And now that the Captain was so near to him he resolved to speak tc. him about her. "Do you want me ? " the man asked, as Steve beckoned him. • "Yes, I wish to have a few words when you have done with my friend." "Well, I guess I'm at liberty now, my lad. I musn't talk too much to your pal. He's very ask yet." --Ah, we're a weak lot altogether, I think," said Steve, glancing round at the pain-drawn faces about him. "Well, what is it, my lad ? " asked the Salvationist, as he came to the bedside. "What became of the young lady who was struck by a fellow one night at ono of your open-air meetings ? " "When was that ? " asked the Captain. "You remember, don't you? The man objected to the Hottentot girl who--" Ah, yes, I remember. He struck at the poor creature and Sister Ella stepped between and received • the blow. Hello! You're looking quite sick, my lad." Steve had blanched suddenly on hearing the " Sister' s " name. "Oh, I'm all right," he said, controlling himself with a i effort. "Go on." "Well," continued the Salvationist, "she wasn't much worse for the blow, but she got a bad fright that night and we couldn't induce her to come out again. "But, praise the Lord!" cried the man, enthusiastically, "Sister Ella didn't desert us. Oh, Go she was WO good a soul for that. She rgmained</p>
        <p>in our nursing-home in Johannesburg attenaing the sick and afflicted, sacrificing herself wholly for others, until this terrible war broke out. Then she came down to Natal here to nurse our country's soldiers; for though, as she says, we are all God's children. and no distinction should be made as to race or country, yet she believes that her country- men have the first claim upon her care. Ah A good, valiant soul is Sister Ella; one of the best and bravest of God's soldiers. She has successfully combated sickness and disease in the past; in the future she will be equally successful, for the Lord fights on her side." - The man spoke with all the earnest fervour of the street preacher, but there was firm conviction behind his impassioned words. "Sister Ella is now at 'lliaritzburg," he went on; "and if you, my lad, happen to get into her hands' she'll pull you through safely. Have her for nurse, my lad, and all will be well with yoo* Good-day, brother. God bless you!" The passenger train drew up, the hospital coaches were attached, and presently refugees and woundea soldiers were being speedily borne from ill-fated Ladv-APkith.</p>
        <p>(To be concluded.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>OFF TO THE FEI E.</head>
        <p>OFF TO THE FEI E.</p>
        <p>Duriag the months of May and June t'ae tI hiSnoured custom of holding Festivals in connection Witt; Rural Sick Benefit Soc!eties still prevails in many par'd of this country. The event of the annual !Ate is awal'co with considerable interest by the villagers, and sane wonder that this ie marked as a red-letter day in tns, annals of an uneventful Arcadian life. Last year 3 r`a patter of the "Dorset Stardarci" was deputed to LAND,: the 16,!e• at Bradford Abbas, a Dorsetshire village flea; Shertiorne, and in the course of his peregrinations also% the holiday makers he had occaaion to seek out a 371:41°,.; lady, Miss Bessie Tuck by narae,reniding near the Farm. He found her, a girl of seventeen, with glow cheeks and dancing eyes, preparing to proceed to the, We field, but, as be put a leading question, a melre3 tray cloud passed o'er her bright face. " Yes," he remarked, " for two or three yearsl have suffered greatly from deficiency of blood and hea'; comslaint. In February I felt very ill,and as I had 10" the opportunity of rest to enable me to battle nail!: the affliction, I got rapidly worso and unat le to fto my household duties, My bead got so weak tiO,tifg could hear it thump day and night; my appetite leg me, and, besides thas, a dreadful sore appeared on • le.a A doctor attended me, but I did not recover. Iw, si in a wretched state, and kept my bed for weeks. I iciaa, the use of my leg, then, and when I got downstairs, an effort, I had to use a stick to cross the room. I li:a aelplees,and so distressed that I lay ,by the fireplace, 5' said many a time I whhed I could dire". " That's qutte true," said Mra. Tuck. "I have heel; mother of thirteen, and she is the yonngeet, but be father and I thought we should lase her." Miss Tuck then continued, in a more cheerful Vei.ll; "I had been told a great deal about Dr. Williams' Pissi pills for pale people by a friend in Yeovil, and at laPd made up my mind to give the pills a trial. I ensnmencE, by taitng one after each meal, and, as I elt they cy.er, doing me good, I gradually increased the uumber,feellit still better by doing so. When I had taken the first he' I was astonished at the improvement,and so was mettlf,r; I took two more bcxee, and, besides my leg being healed and strong, I am able to eat and eejoy my 100 awl feel better than I have sver been before." " Oh, yes, alie's quite cured," said Mrs. Thet‘si "Really, it's wonderful. Why, she's quite a diffetea, girl. She'll join in a game or a temp with any of us'. The above is no unusual experience. Such cases s't this—cases where sickly, languid girls, without c010u.,, or energy, and weak, nervous men looking as if thf,', would never be very strong, have been transformed Dr. Williams' pink pills—may be heard of in el'es's's neighbourhood. New blood in every dose—that is TiT', they give ; and with this new blood, which is new coursing through the veins, icfluenza and coughs, w", might have been the beginning of consumptionew- appear ; the fortified system throws off rheumatielr; sc'atica, St. Vittlte dance, fits, and other nervous dslije eases ; threatened paralysis is kept at bay; and t's system learns to utilise its food so that liver einggishofeb" indigestion, backache, and the ailments of ladies van' To avoid substitutes, which can do no good, bear in on that it is Dr. Williams' pink pills you want ; anusli dishonest people try to push substitutes upon you, ee's direct, enclosing price, two shillings and ninepencalt box, er thirteen and ninepeece for six boxes, tO Williams' Medicine Company, Holborn Viaduct Lendoet- These pills are a tonic, the only tonic that does 0° derange digestion.</p>
