      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>LORD HAWKE AND THE YORKSHIRE PROFESSIONALS.</head>
        <p>LORD HAWKE AND THE YORKSHIRE PROFESSIONALS.</p>
        <p>RMr. Randal Roterte has an article on "Loid Hawke 21; Home" in "Cassell's Magazine" for June Lo.ro Hawke preceeded to give ma some very interestingfr formation about the pay and status of the present-as/ Y'orkshire professional. "The regular members'of th team'. he vaid,'receive a fixed salary during the su'inglen. and winter pay besides. Then they have, in additiom certain perquisites. For instance, as of course, 1,(311 know every member of the tam has a chance of earlll "talent motley," which means that any batsman SIMMS fifty man innings gets a sovereign,lhirty if he makes i3eventy-five, two sovereigns if he scor.ell_, hundred, and so on. Theie is no fixed feat which entitl_s; a bowler to talent money, hut I am empowered by Or County Committee to give a sovereign to any of9tio bowlers for what I consider a sufficiently meritorlo 0 performance. And then, every member of the team 11.502 his benefit to look forward to as something to help k 3,1 a towards a position of independence when his pIaYIIIr days are over.'"</p>
