      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>RICHARD AND RUDYARD MR. LE GALLIENNE'S DEPRECIA-</head>
        <p>RICHARD AND RUDYARD MR. LE GALLIENNE'S DEPRECIA-</p>
        <p>TION OF KIPLING.</p>
        <p>Rudyard Kipling, a criticism, by Richard Le tallienne," suggests a remarkaole study in contrast. It i 8 Scarcely to be expected that the apostle of the Pretty, and the naughty, and the dainty, especially the I, dainty, should take kindly to the' blood and tobacco,, the" broiled bones and brandy "of Mr. 'Pang's muse. One might almost as well expect tatulvaney or Ortheris to read with any toleration :,..The Quest of the Golden Girl." That singular ;York, indeed, represents a literary type so amus- ,,`l3glY opposed to the whole spirit of Mr. Kipling, s eroduteta that it is impossible to resist the tempta- Mon of quoting a few passages. Here is one at "heeth from the aforesaid rhapsody: advancing to me eagerly along the causeway the very spirit of Alastor himaelf. There was :A Star.l upon his forehead, and around his young face there 'lsWed a aureole of gold and roses—to sp-_.ak figuratively, for the star upon his brAw was hop., and the gold and It"ee encircling his he3d. a miniarare rainbow, were youth and health. His longish golden hair had no doubt Moan in the effect, as likewise the soft yellow silk ',;!ei that fluttered like a flame in the speed of his going. 8 blue eyes were tragically fresh and clear—as though .C.eY had as yet been little used. There were little Ini:gB of haste upon his feet, and he came straight to 17Ith the air of the Angel Gabriel about to make his G°llle announcement. For a moment I thought that ti;wee an apparition of prophecy charged to announce viLnlaiden of the Lord for whom I was seeking [and Don" Petticoat and stockivga our hero carried iu his Illanteau]. However, his brief ,flushed question was these things. He desired first to ask the time of tho'n',.4,nd next—here, after a bump to the earth one's R;Zuts: ballooned again heavenwards—" 114 d I seen A.loo:eopy of Shelley lying anywhere along the road?" tion 5a414e this effusion let us place for our recrea- reserv- ibit of Kipling which Air, Ls Gallienne 'ejacui" !,8, for special disapproval. The opening lOU, has, of course' no reference to the above eXCerpt ' BlTumph., said the machine-gun through all its five noses as the anbalt ,rn drew the lever home. The empty cartridges clashed on the floor, and the amokellew back through the truck. There were indiscriminate firing at the rear of the train, a return fire from the darkness throughout, and unlimited howling. Dick stretched biniself on the floor, wild with delight at the sounds and the smells. " God is very good. I never thought I'd gear this again. Give 'ern hell, men! Oh give 'am he cried. TWO extremes, it will be said; but which appeals hlest to.the healthy nerve of a man? corresponding verse contrast is perhaps not lair, t°r,t're believe Mr. Le Gallienne does fiat pretend to be cpoet ; but again the temptation is too strong, Queen Aphrodite's .Daughters are we, the that was born of the moon And the sea; White are our limbs As the foam on the wave, Wild are our hymns, And our lovers are brave. Queen Aphrodite, Born of the sea, Beautiful, dutiful daughters Are we !</p>
        <p>You that would follow Fear not to come, For love is for love And dove is for dove. The harp of Apolla Shall lull you to rest, And your head find its home On this beautiful breast. Queen Aphrodite, &amp;c. 141:'et lie open Kipling at ramdom. -s'adl had guns (as I bad goo.ls) to work my 3bristian harm. oad run him up from his quarter-deck to trade with his own yard-arm; "ad naiwi .h;s ears to my capstan-head, and ripped A them off with a saw, soused them in the bilge-water, and served thorn to raw:; h 4 hina a acl gulag him blind in a rudderless boat, to rot to the / 1, rocking dark, "ad towed him aft of his own craft, a bait for .his , brother sharka nad tapped .him round with cesoa-hui.k and drenched A him with the oil. lashed him fast to his own mast to blaze above my Spoil. ti (" The Three Captains." 3 44-3 for the rhyme of ' The Three Captains,' I may, ,".8 at fault" says Mr. Le Gallienne, "but I have i4lever been allie to read it to the end ;but so far as I gone its story struck me as dull, its nautical 14eanicalities• choking it like seaweed, more than pbt.lallY tiresome, and its metre open to the same Jeetion." &amp;C atallaving illustrated the divergenci.es!between these Men, to show wily Mr. La Uallienne cannot be itlnected fully to appreciate Mr. Kipling, let, us give pi"? instances where he does not. 4` The English 4:11,R '' is generally regarded as one of the finest rl'i3nal lyrics ever written—a pure product•of the Qraethean fire at its whitest heat, Hear what reiseus thinks: the'a first eight lines are spirited. They stir one. Were rest of the poem equal to them the plein had been a glii,epa- But they are not. Recondite geography—Mr. ta f,s4,ag'a fatal geography—and astronomy are called in its—we the place of inspiration, and, as nothing can take elace, they fail. gellarely the Kurile Isles, and Singapore, and Bet- verb' and Kowloon, and the Horn and Lizard are not i,.reCondite geography. and the most enuclented 'id might stand the intellectual strain of the lier Inborn Croi,s " and the " Northern Liglre." theici at any rate, are no Alastors with stars upon r foreheads. efter this casual notice of "The English /flag a Ina„stquent proceedings in Mr Le Gallienne's book interest-, the lever of KiPling- lie may I:l4;',Aer, however, what the critic thinks of "The ym, 1118 of the Fore and Aft," perhaps the best short vvrtover spun. Here we have it on page 134: ther ere in that vivid picture of a butcher's shop is hinte a hint of pity far the Afghan ? . . . Not a hreal(:ifaanY civilized feeling tor a noble race inevitably tillerei..gg, not before supetior courage or physique, but ' iazel superior cievernes3 and totter machines. True, ie To° a firat-clas9 fighting man." But that's all. Such atleapjl:lllY's simple view, and Mr. Kipling's is no less Tuttunyl4 is sheer primitive ignorance. Mr I—what • 'llrPriiiing that Mr, Le Gallienne did not</p>
        <p>stumble upon the right answer even as he wrote, for he was distinctly " hot." Of course, Mr. Kip- ling's view, for the purpose of the short story, was Tommy's own, as nearly and literally as possible. But perhaps Mr. Kipling, in his next edition, will insert the philosophic meditations suggested by his critic. We might give other instances of what we will not call Mr. be Gallienne's " sheer primitive ignor- ance," for it is worse than that—it is bad criti- cism." Of Mr. Kipling's" The White Man's Burden" and "Recessional "we read : "They are reactionary in the direction of sentimental superstition, and, however sincerely Mr. Kipling meant them, are eerious reinforcements of British national hypacrisy." And here, leaving Mr. Richard be Gallienne to accuse Mr. Rudyard Kipling of senti- mentality, we might end; but we notice that Mr. be Gallionne draws out an indictment against the whole British people:: 'Whatever the reason may he, there is unmistakably at the moment a general indifference to human suffering." A trio of para- graphs in this morning's paper seems scarcely to bear out this sweeping generalization. They are as folio vs : The Transvaal Fond at the Mansion House amounted last even,ng to £9C7,400. The Indian Famine Fund at the Mansion House amounted !wit night to £240,300. The Ottawa Fire Fond at the Mansion House amoun ted last night to •E 49,000. This Rea!'cly looks like "a general indifference to human suff -ring." We must not forget to acknow- ledge the merits of a most useful bibliography which Mr. Lane hal appande(i to this little volume, and which gives it value.—Daily News.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>OPENING OF THE "WINMARLEIGH"</head>
        <p>OPENING OF THE "WINMARLEIGH"</p>
        <p>CAFE.</p>
        <p>The" Winmarleigh " Café in Sankey-street was opened to the public last Wednesday, and from the p itronage it at once secured it may be confidently predicted that this first-class house of refreshment will have a successful future. The proprietors are a recently-formed local company known as the Warringtoa Caf33 Limited, and the first directors are Mr J W Hayward (chairman),Mr Henry Milling, Hr te It Roberts, Mr John Hesketh and Mr Walter Brough. 'I he duties of secretary will devolve upon Mr William Roberts. The company have acquired by purchase the re- freshment and catering business successfully carried on for several years by Mrs. Hayward, and which has now been transferred to "The Winmarleigh " at the new and commodious premises, 70, Sankey- street, formerly occupied by Messrs. Hesketh and Dutton. The business will still be under the personal supervision of Mrs. J W Hayward whe has already gained a high reputation for the excellency of her confectionery and cuisine. No expense has been spared in fitting up and decorating the establish- ment which for luxuriance and completeness of appointment is unapproached locally and has few equals anywhere. The fittings have been elaborately designed by the architects. Messrs Fraser and War- burton, of Lyme-street. The front portion of the shop is set apart-for the purposes of a retail trade and is divided from the dining room by an orna- mental screen. The shop is provided with a beauti- ful counter with marble top and wall fixtures of an improved design. The ceiling and friezes have been artistieally decorated and the ornamental woodwork has been treated in white and gold. An interesting feature of the dining-room is the provision of five cubicles fitted with comfortable -cushioned seats and lined with art tapestry. Proceeding upstairs the visitor is agree- ably surprised to find himself in a richly furnished drawing room where it is proposed to serve teas. The floor is covered with soft-treading Turkish car- pets, and arranged about the room are about a dozen afternoon tea tables at which a tete-A-tete conver- sation can be enjoyed. Near to are ladies' and gentlemen's retiring rooms and lavatories, fitted up on the most modern and sanitary principles. The second storey is devoted to billiards and room is well-lighted and elegantly furnished. The well-known firm of 'Orme and Sons, billiard table builders, of Manchester. have supplied the table. In the basement which is approached by a stairca-e from the dining-room an- other billiard table is provided and both billiard rooms will no doubt be a pleasant rendezvous for members of the sterner sex. The kitchen and ser- very have been fitted up with the latest cooking and heating apnlionces. It should be added that the furniebing was entrusted to Mr. R. Johnson, of Bridge-street; the cabinet work in tbe billiard room has been supplied by Messrs Joseph Mounfield and Sons, and the whole of the decorative work line been carried out by the firm of Messrs James Hes- ket h anti Sons, under the personal supervision of Mr John Hesketh. The visitor comes away with ' the impression that here is a step in advance on anything hitherto attempted in Warriugton and the publie will no doubt show their appreciation of toe enterprise which has prompted it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Great Sankey School Board.</head>
        <p>Great Sankey School Board.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Great Sankey School Board was held in the Board Schools on Tuesday evening, Mr T Halton presiding. There were also present—Messrs J Fulton-Smith, Thomas Perks, Joseph liiishaw, Wright,W D Jeans Ann. (clerk),and (-4 Clark (headmaster).—The Headmaster's report was presented and was as follows :—Week ending May 12,h, on book 5,28; present, 270; average,244.2; fees, lie 61; May 19th, on books, 294; present, 280; averago, 251; fees, us 10:1 ; May 26th,on b00k5,293; present, 277; average, 240 9 ; fees, 12s 10d; average for the month, 213.9; total fees, £29 21. An esti- mate of the expenditure for the next half year was produced and a precept for the sum of £l6O was issued.</p>
      </div>
