      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 1, ,1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 1, ,1900.</p>
        <p>at the front four dukes, and 32 members of Parliament so that they would see that the highs: to the loweet of the people had shown a wo derful bravery and deter- mlnation to fight for the honour of the old country. (Applause.) He was sure they were:all delighted to see Colored Walker present and they only hoped they might see in the future very me ch more of the members of the Walker family than they had done in the past. Be was cert tin they would always get a ni3it heate:y welcome. (Applause.) Colonel Warmso, ia respo adine, said at this time hero was no douot that such a toast as had been just proposed must naturally be received with very great enthusiasm. It was a time in the history of our good Queen's reign where the efficacy of our navy and army had been thoroughly Proved. As regarded the navy, one would have thoueht that in this war there would not have been mon for it to do, but strange to' say, they had been called upon to render most invalu- able service. (Rear, hear.) They had finished their work and they had received already an ovation upon their vial:en', son their return home. It was a wonder- ful sight to see a sailor on horseback riding through the streets of Londoa in the person of Captain Lambtoo, accompanied by those gall ant men of his belonging to Her Majesty's ship" Powerful." As regarded the army,the work they had bad to perform hid been no easy task. It had been conducted under an entirely new phase of fighting and new methods had had to be employed and bitter lessons learnt. We had paid dearly for those lessons ty losing many valuable lives but at the same time those lessons must naturally prove of the greatest possible value in the future. We ' m'gist congratulate ourselves that in this long reign of peace and prosperity, never before had we cause to call into active service the men of the reserve forces, and yet upon the eve of our good Queen's reign it seemed only right that these forces ought to have had one opportunity of proving themselves thor- oughly worthy of their postion. It was for the people of toe Bfitish Empire to judge whether they had ful- filled their promise ; it was for them to say whether they had realised expectations. (Applause.) He. had noticed that our generals had made even mere use of the volunteers than they bad of the regulars, and ne wish-d to be distinctly understood that in mentioning the volunteers he included the whole of the celonial forces. (Hoar, hear, and applause.) He had not the slightest doubt but that a new era would take place in regard to the treatment which the v dunteers of this coun- tra would receive in the future. (Hoar, hear.) We were all proud ot our countrymen ; we were all proud of our cousins from Canada and Australasia and he ventured to say in aeknovvledging this toast, that so long as our Queen reigeed we should over find men willing to give their services, voluntarily and willingly and if necessary to sacrifice their lives to uphold the best traditions of those vast dominions over which our goad Queen reigned. (Appfau,e.) It would be invidious for him if he did not tike that opportunity of mentioning one or two names of men who had been foremost in pushing the Volunteers to the front in this war. The name he would mention first was that of a friend of his and whom he was proud to say was Major General Baden-Powell. (Applause.) He was a man who had made the most of his opportunities vf studying his profession. Ho was a good friend, a' good sportsman and in the true sense of the word, a thorough anliier. (Hear, hear.) In the early part of this war he organized a diminutive army of irregulars numbering 500 men, and with these he had kept at a distance no fewer than 5,000 Boers for the beet Part of seven months. (Applause) He was happy to say that the good old British flag had been kept floating over Mafeking. (Applause.) Another offi -or whom he would mention was Maclaren of the ' 13th Hussars. He was one of the first to go round to the South of Rhodesia and organise volunteers with the objet of rendering assistance to Baden-Powell. Ho was one of those who met with a serious wound and ' almost sacrificed his life in the work. When our braye soldiers returned they would help to brush away the tears that dimmed our eyes at the loss of our true friends and at the same time they would bring to our cheeks the blush of pride—the pride and joy of our ' soldiers (L3ud applause.) Sir Peter Walker.</p>
        <p>The Mevon said he had pleasure in proposing the toast " of the thy, that or Sir Peter Walker. ( ipplause.) Sir Peter represented ttie third generation of a family who had been very intimately and honourably asseciated with the interests of Warrington. He found that as far back as 1854 Sir Peter's grandfather first became a mern her of their corporation and for nine years he served the townin that capacity. (Applause.) One generous and noble gift of his was perhaps one of the best they had over received as a Corporation. (Hear, hear.) He did not know of a thousand pounds which was ever made to go se far as the gift of the late Sir Peter Walker many years ago (Laughter.) Whilst perhaps the late Sir Andrew Walker was not so intimately associated with them as his father was, they had a great deal of personal pride that the neighbouring city of Liverpool benefited a great deal by his generosity, and in some smell degree I:, had been reflected .upon them in Warrington. One of the most beautiful ortiaments .1:1 that splendid city was the gift of a War- rington man, the late Sir Andrew Walker, and he (the Mayor) knew that the University, and more particularly the engineering section, had been of great benefit to many youths from Warrington. (Applause.) Toelay they had with them the representative of the third geaeration, and he could assure Sir Peter—and he said it in no formal sense—that they were heartily and sincerely gratified to have him and his excellent brother with them at that festive board. (Hear, hear). They hoped it was the precursor of many, many visits. In regard to Sir Peter and his family they wished him every prosperity and happiness. (Applause.) They hoped the future might have in store for Sir Peter hap piness and joy, and that he might have the satisfaction as years went by and as he got older to know that he was doing somchine to benefit his friends and neighbours. (Hear, hear.) They thanked him sine ,rely for the gift which he had bestowed upon them. As in ornament to the town it had great artistic writ and Warrington was not one of those towns over-burdened mementoes of this character. Tney not only thanked Sir Peter for the gift but still more for the very kindly and generous motive which prompted it. .(Applause.) The toast was drunk wieh enhusiesrn and the singing of "He's a Jolly Good Fellow." ' Sir PETER WALKER who was cordially received on rising to reply,said it gave him much pleasure to respond to the toast and acknowledge the kind remarks which had accompanied it and the hearty manner with which it had been received. He could assure them that his brothers and himself were very glad to have had the opportunity of cemmernorating their grandfather's memory. (Applause) They would readily believe that it was an opportunity which was very dear to them. It was so long ago as 46 years that his grandfather came to Warrington and founded the original firm of Peter Walker and Sons which had grown almost as rapidly as the town itself. (Hear, hear.) His grandfather formed many friendships in War- rington and did not shrink from performing his duty in a public capacity for he entered the Caned just one month before he (Sir Peter) was born. (Laughter.) He believed they were all aware that when he retired from the Council he presented the Corperation with the sum of money named by the Mayor. How the Council managed to make the money last the lese,th of time it did he was somewhat surprised at but at any rate it redounded to the credit of the Mayor and Corporation that they were able to make it go so far. (Hear, hear.) His brothers and himself hoped that the renewed find of one thousand pounds would be as useful as the late . fund proved to he,and he was delighted to know that the first use to which it had been put was to equip the local</p>
        <p>I'Vollinteers who had gone out to fieht for our country. .(Applause ) The money would; no doubt he spout in time, but the fountain, he hoped would be some sme_ll memento of a permanent kind to his gra,' i (Applause.) Lady Walker gave him special instructions to thank them for having in- vited her to perform the ceremony that day, and she hoped ar some future time to visit Warrington to make their n(cpaintance and see the town where he(Sir Peter) worked as a young man. (Rear, hear.) He thanked zhe:n for the cordial maaaer they had received his name. (applause.) Alderman Surrox proposed the health of the Mayor. '1 He observed that Alderman Roberts had title] the mayoral office for six months and he thought they would all agree with him that ha had performed hie duties in a very creditable and satisfact,-,ry manner. (Rear, hear.) In fact he thought he might go so far as 'n say that next to himself (Alderman Sutton) he had been one of the best Mayors. (Laughter.) They were all aware that the Ms,yoral duties were becoming more and more onerous every year. Ha had no doubt that Alderman Roberts would find that the present was no exception to previous years. They mi:fht cmgratulate themselves on _</p>
        <p>- the fact that he had ever manifested his. willingness to perform public duties and had shown that he was pea iPsaed of all the ability necessary to cope with any difficuty which might arise in the administration of the affairs of this towri. (applause ) He wichel Alderman Roberts the best health it was posaible to have during his year of office, and that he might also have the assistance of his wife—(applause)—who seemed to take an interest in everything connected with the Mayoral duties so far as she could and assisted him in every possible way. (Applause). After the toast had been drunk the company sang "For he's a jolly good fellow." The MAYOR, in responding, said he was extremely grateful to them for that handsome testimony and the good opinions which had been espressed of the work he had tried to do during the past six months. hie admitted that it would he impossible for a man of his temperament to do other than play second fiddle to his very modest friend who pronoried the toast. (Langhter.) Joking apart, there was no doubt about this, that theft, was in the Council a more thorough spirit of co-opera- tion than he ha I ever seen during the sixteen ye are he lead had the pleasure of sitting in the council chamber. (Hear, hear.) They seem( d to have put on one side—and he hoped for ever—everything that was antagonistic, to the one main otij,ct,— to do the best they could with fhe knowledge they had, to promote the prosperity of the people of the town in which they lived and the</p>
        <p>people who surrounded them. It was quite true that the duties attaching to the offie.e of Mayor were becom- Lig more and more onerous. Parliament was devolving greater respousibilities and larger duties upon municipal bodies alinosz every year and it was of the greatest im- por.ance that the men who constituted the governing bodies should be men who were willing and able to devote their energies to the administration of affairs in the district in which they resided. Ile thought it to be great honour to be a member of the Town Council and the greatest honour he had ever received had been a elected to the position of chief magistrate. If anything could possibly add to the gratification and pleasure he had felt it had been the knowledge that he had the united and loyal support of every member of the Coun- cil. The Mayor then ref2red to the exceptional duties which had devolved upon him since he took office and the spirit af loyalty and devotion which had been shown by the townspeop!e. The interesting function concluded with the singing of the NAtio3al Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WALKER FOUNTAIN.</head>
        <p>THE WALKER FOUNTAIN.</p>
        <p>Turning-on of the Water. Interesting Ceremony. On Friday witnessed the interesting ceremony of turning-on the water of the Walker Fountain in front of the Town Hall. The weather was beautifully fine and a large concourse of people witnessed the proceedings. Tee object of Sir Peter Carlaw Walker in presenting the fountain to the town is given in the following letter in which the offer of the gift was made : "Ormaston Manor,Derby,lst December, 1893. Dear Mr. Mayor, It has been brought to my notice that the gift of my late grandfather, • Pater Walker, of £l,OOO for the benefit of the town of Warrington has now been exhaustiod aad having, by the courtesy of yourself and the Town Clerk had an opportunity of perus- ing the accounts relating thereto, I and other members of our family should for the sake of old and present associations be much gratified if the town of Warrington could do us the honour of accepting a second gift of £l,OOO for the same general purposes. Will you kindly bring this matter before the Council, and also ask their accept- ance from the same source of a farther stun of £l,OOO for a fountain to be ereced in the space in front of the Town Hall or any other site to be ap- proved by yourself and the conned in memory of my grandfather, the late Pater Walker. To his Worshipful the Ilayor, Alterman J. E. Wright, J.P., Town Hall, Warrington." The fountain is classic in style, and harmonizes with the architecture of the Town Hall, in front of which it is placed. Fears were entertained that the erection might,to some slight extent obscure the view of the hall,but any disadvantages of this kind have been fully compensated tor by the beauty of the fountain. It is a cast iron spray erec- tion, with a ground basin 40 feet in diameter enriched by bronze portrait medallions of the donor and members of the family. On the centre portion there are four seated figures over life size, modelled by Nlr„ D. W. Stevenson, RSA., and representing art, science, literature, and commerce, which by the way are in themselves triumphs of the ironfouuders' craft. The upper basin is sup- ported by a centre pedestal having twelve panels decorated with the Zaiiacal signs, (men sign being surrounded with the flora suitable to the month represented. Around the rim of this ornamental basin are eight sea urchins spouting water from horns,whilst over thisitonr entwined dolphins carry the terminal basin to a heignt 37 feet from the ground. the fountain has been designed and cast by Messrs Walter Macfarlane and Company, art founders of Fossil Park, Glasgow. lO3nri Hand S Chandley were entrusted with the paint- ing of it and in its coat of pale green and gold it pre- sents a beautiful appearance. In the basin itself rojpk stone has been placed and it is probable that fi n may vet find a home in the clear water which gushes from the innumerable jets of the fountain. The Opening Ceremony. Sir Peter Ctrlaw W tlker, Bart., performed the formal opening at noon yesterday, in the presence of the Mayor kAlderrnan Roberts), the members of the Corporation, several ladies, including the Mayoress and a large assembly of the public. Sir Peter was accompanieu by his brother, Colonel William Hall Walker. The MAYOR said : Sir Peter Walker, E have very great pleasure, on behalf of my fellow-townmen, in the first place to offer you a hearty and sincere welcome amongst us to-day, and, secondly, to ask yen to be good enough to turn on the water of the fountain you have been so kind to present to the town. It will always remind us' apart from its artistic beauty, of the generous thought which prompted the noble act • (Hear, hear.) I have much pleasure in asking you to perform Ulm ceremony of turning on the water. (Applause). Sir PETER WALKER said: It has been a very great pleasure to myself and my brother to have been given Permission to erect a fountain in your town to the memory of our grandfather. He always took a very great interest in the town of Warrington, and we felt we should Ike, if it would not to unwelcome to you, to erect some little memento t 3 his memory. I thank you, Mr Mayor, for your kind remarks, and without further words proceed with the ceremony. (Applause.) Sir Peter then turned oa the water, and loud cheers were given. Cheers were afterwards given for the generous donor.</p>
        <p>Lunch at the Town Hall. After the ceremony, an adjournment was made to the Town Hall where lunch was served. Amongst those present were :—Fhe Mayor (Alderman Roberts), Sir Peter Walker, Bart., Colonel Wa'ter, Mr. Moore, Mr J Felton Smith, Mr Henry Greenall, Mr r May, Mr r J Ridgway, Aldermen Fairclough, Francomb, Sutton, Hutchinson, Heaton, Bolton, Councillors Baird, Bennett. Burton, Davies, Duncan, Ellisan, John Evans, James Evans, Forshaw, A Jolley, A J Jolley, Lee, Pet- cock, Plumpton, Jahn Roberts, Rogers, Shillcock, Smethurst, Tinnion, Wilkineon, elesers. J Lyon Whittle (town clerk). J Fairhurst (borough treasurer). T Longdin (borough surveyor). J Dees (water engineer)' Dr Gomel( (medical officer),W S eddock(gas engineer). F Tavlor(gas secretary), Charles Madeley (librarian). G F F White (deputy magistrates' cierk), Luke Talbot (chief ceneteble), Henry Thornton, El Ferguson, John Hesketh, T Locker, George Vernon, Bailie McNicoll and a representative of Messrs. William Micrarlane and Company. In the absence of the Mayor's Chaplain and the Rector through indisposition the Mayor called upon the youneest member of the Council—Councillor Fershaw—to say Grace. After an excellent repast, catered for by Mr. and Mrs. Powys, several toasts were proposed. Her Majesty the Queen." The MLYOa proposed the toast of" Her Majecey the Queen:" In doing so he said that yesterday they at Warrington in common with the rest of Her Majesty's subjects throughout the British Empire, celebrated the 61st birthday of Her Majesty the Queen. (Hear, hear.) For 63 years she had by the grace of God pre- sided over tare destinies of our empire and what a ,63 years they had ben f° Great as had been the extension of territory, greater still in ,his opinionehad been the improvements which tlaateZ years had done for the moral, material, political and social progress of her people. (Hear, hear.) They sIl fervently hoped and prayed that she might for many years in undimmed vigour preside over them. (Bear, hear.) That sentiment of loyalty which veers lege became almost a threadbare thing had in conse- quence of ter Majesty's conduct become engrained in the hearts of every true Britisher. (Applause.) The child- ren of to-day would recollect with very great pleasure the fact that they were permitted to witness such a ceremony as was yesterday ehown from one end of the country to the other and which they hoped might be for several years repeated. (Applause.) The toast was drunk with musical honours. The Prince and Princess of Wales and Members of the Royal Family. The MAYOR in proposing this toast alluded to the deep regard that the country had for the Prince of Wales He wasp, man sound at heart, deeply interested in the welfare of his people and in a recent speech in London he discussed the sanitary provisions for the very poor. Lie (the Mayor), was intensely grati- fied to find that the people on the Prinee's estate in Norfolk were an example to the rest of the country so far as regarded the interest of the landlord in their comfort and health. Whenever it should in the great Providence of God come abone that the Prince ehould succeed hia worthy Mother, heeivould prove an honour- ably and worthy successor in following the example which she had 60 nobly sot through an unparalleled life devoted to the interests of the country at large. (Ap- plause.) The toast was enthusiastically drunk. The Army, Navy and Reserve Forz:es. Speech by Colonel Walker. Alderman FRANCOMB proposed the toast of the Army, Navy and Reserve Forces. Referring to the army he said our soldiers had done wonders in South Afric t and it was no longer necessary in proposing this toast to refer to the brave deeels at Waterloo and elsewhere but they were able to speak of what they had seen in their own time In the present campaign in Africa their soliiers had tried to be as brave as any who had gene before. (Elear,hear,) Our generals were as capable or more capable than they had had before. As to their volunteers, the opinion had been largely held that they were simply citiz bre soldiers, and that some of them were merely playing at soldiers. (Laughter.) But they had preyed them qdvete quite equal to their brethren of the line and he thought the hat fee, months had shown the whole of Europe that we were a fighting nation when we were required to fight. We did not wilfully pick up quarrels, but if we were attacked even by stronger powers than the present enemy we should be able to bear ourselves well and come out victorious. (Hear. bear:) Referring to the naval forces, he said if it had not been for the naval brigade and their guns we should have heard of more disasters than we experienced in the early part of the campaign. That brave handful of men htd done wonders and the same remark auntie i to the reserve forces. (Hear, hear.) He believed we had</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>EVERY WOMAN</head>
        <p>EVERY WOMAN</p>
        <p>Should send two stamps for our 32 page Illustrated Book containing valuable information of how all Irregularities and Obstructions may be eutirely avoided or removed by simple means. Recommended by eminent Physicians as the only Safe, Sure and Genuine Remedy. Never faila. Thousands of genuine unsolicited restimonia from all over tho World.—P. BLANCIIARI Dilston-lan London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>ST. MARY'S AMATEUR DRAMATIC</head>
        <p>ST. MARY'S AMATEUR DRAMATIC</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Performances of "Grimaldi."</p>
        <p>With the commsndeble object of assisting the local Indian Famine Fund, the St. Mary's Amateur Dramatic Society have given three performances of " Grimaldi," in the Parr Hall. It was originally decided to give two but the demand for tickets was so great that a third per- formence was found necessary, and this was given on Tuesday evening. "Grimaldi "is a play in five acts and was first performed by the St. Mary's Society about a month ago with a considerable anseunt of success. Its repeltion he not only given delight to thousands of people, but has also been the means of augmenting the Indian Famine Fund. The principal characters in the piece are Mogi, of Grimaldi, an old actor, represented by Mr. W. Kilduff; and Violet (Grimaldi's adopted daughter), impersonated by Miss N Fieming. Mr. Kil- duff plays the character in a very happy manner, achieving great success. Miss Flem- ing had a difficult role to play as Violet and performed her exacting duties with con- siderable skill. In the tragic EC3IIO in the old manor house, some of the emotional opportunities were perhaps not fully realised, but on the whole Miss Fleming is to be coogra.tulated upon her performance. Miss Burrows acte4 with rare force and dignity as JuTia (an actress). Lord Arthur Shaftoa, Maltravers and Selwyn Dawdle are a trio of officers who are adequately represented by Messrs. J. M. Bate, S Ryan and H Roylence. It is no easy matter for an amateur to represent an experi- enced comedian, and in essaying the task which falls to the lot of Wopshot, Mr. G. Newton showed some of the qualities required of a cemedian. There was, however, ' something lacking in his perfoembnce. and :this was especially) noticeable in the scene with Violet, where he atempos to describe what love is, an episode which a comedian would certainly have made more of. The other ladies and gentlemen who contributed largely to the enjoyment are Mr C Newton (Duke of D--), Mr J N Vernon (Leech),Mr F V Eckenley (Hopkins),Mr G Dolan (waiter), Miss Byrne (Countess of Belem trig), Wee Hayes (Lady Belle Le.ttres), Miss Brown (Mrs Highly Preper) Miss Gleeson (Miss Stingwell), Miss S Mannioa (Mrs Hopkins) and Masters JMc Kay and A Kilduff (messengers). All the minor parts were satisfactorily sustained and the members are to be congratulated upon the exeellent performance. An effi ient orchestra under the able couductership of Mr. R. Eccles performed se'esttone during the intervals. Mr. W. Kilduff discharged the duties of hen. manager and Mr. M. G. ilearue, those of hon. secretary. Messrs W. and L. Barrows were the stage managers aad Mr G. Coggine was in charge of the mechanical effects. Toe ,Mayors, during an interval on Monday evening, stated that be had been asked to announce that owing to the large demand for seats which toe committee had been unable to satisfy it had been decided to hold anothsr performance on the following evening, (hp. plause ) Be also wished to take advantage of tee oppor- tunity afforded him of expressing,what he was sure they felt also, his extreme satisfaction at the very excellent performance of the St. Mary's Dramatic Society. (Hear. hear.) That was the first occasion on which he had had that pleasure but he felt zertain that if the society pur- sued its excellent work in other seasons they would take every opportunity of seeing their further progress. (Applause.) It also gave him great pleasure to express on behalf of the executive committee who were inter- ested in collecting money on behalf of the suffering people in India their grateful thanks for the motive which had prompted the St. Mary's Amateur Dramatic Society to voluntarily give their services for such a good cause. (Applause.) He knew that the feeling was spreading very much that it was the duty of the Government of the country to provide for the necessities which these re- curring famines were the cause of. It was a stupend- ous work, and whilst they perhaps thought the respon- sibility should be the Government's, at the same time they would be false to the principles which they pro. fessed and to their common humanity if they allowed their private benefactions to be warped because of opinions on the question of responsibility. Warrington was the only town in Lancashire which he believed was coming up to the standard of a few years ago in con- nection with the emount of subscriptions in aid of the sufferers in India. (Applause.) On the occasion of the last famine Warrington raised about £l,lOO and their preeent subscription list was about £l,OOO. (Applause.) It was perhaps only just to say that :a large propor• tion of that amount was the private gift of one firm in the town. (Hear, hear ) In many places throughout the kingdom time subscriptions had fallen short of what they amounted to a few years ago by at least 25 per cent, on the arersge. He thought they would agree with him that in this matter as in every other where a qaestion of common humanity came into play Warrington had done its duty and done it well. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>VOLUNTEER CHURCH PARADE.</head>
        <p>THE VOLUNTEER CHURCH PARADE.</p>
        <p>A portion of the local Volunteer Battalion paraded for Divine service at the Parish Church on Sunday morn- ing!, in accordance with a custom which has been adop- ted for many years on the Sunday before going away for the annual encampment. The meu, who were under the command of Colonel Ridgway, Mjer Buckton, Major Roorne (adjutant), Captains J. D. Fairelough, and B. Fairclough. &amp;world-Lieutenants Willcocks. Gough. Roberts and Williamson. and Lieutenant and Quarter- Master Potter, left the Drill Hall shortly after ten o'clock, the route to the Parish Church being, as usual, lined with spectators. The band of the regiment was in attendance, and furnished the aceernpaniments to the hymns. Mr. W. 8, Woods was at the organ. The preacher was the Rev. A. A. Markham (St. Jude's Liverpool). formerly senior curate of the Parish Church, who took for his text Hebrews ii. 6,8, and 9. In the course of his sermon he remarked that a few menthe ago they might have wondered wh•ther they were not forgetting the old virtues of loyalty, or whether there was any enthuslasmJeft at the end of the century, but what had recently happened had proved that it wks lie when it was said that we had no entlausiasne and wa r a phlegmatic nation, incapable of any eeneroos ernetion. During the last week tees() generous 'feelings of loyalty and enthusiasm seemed to have reached the highest pitch and to have found more adequate expreision. Our loyalty had found full expression in the celebration of the birthday of our venerable Queen. She had always been mar our hearts, but of late she appeared to be attached to us in a way she had never been before ;it was owing to her tender anxiety for the tv,lfere of her seldiors, her refusal to leave tho United Kingclein at this time, her generously-conceived visit to Ireland as a mark of gratitude to her Irish soldiers, her forgetfulness of self, her entiring devotion to duty in spite ot age and infirmity. All these good qualities had aroused he loyalty of the people to a Teter' which had eenkr ht•tio reached before, awl our enthusiasm had tee 'rely foiled adequate vent. Alluding to the siege of Mafeking, the preacher observed that we had for seve:•al months eagerly awaited news from the beleaguered garrison, had watceed with admiration the res mreetureese, the ingenuity, the skill; and endurance of the gallant men and him who comme.nded them, and whic,-. h !neje the word Mafeking for ever a household word in &amp;len land—e word which would never be forgotten as nine as there was a British heart left to thrill, arid to tell of British gallantry. But was all this to pass away end te be merely for a time ? It was a good thing to he :eyed and eethusiaetic, and to have warm and generous feelings, hut let them beware that they did not run ti waste. Let him encourage them to censeerete these qualities. wbieh had been doubtless displaced le Wer- ington—these virtues of loyalty and enteusi teen—to seinethine, even higher and more Impart ant, eta] to re- member that it was harder to be loyal to Christ than it was to the Queen. Who would have thoueencoreinued the rev gentleman, a year ago when they met on tee Sunday after Ascension that to-lay so large a ereporlion of the Werrington Volunteer regiment would be on active service in South Africa? Who wield have theught that so many would have had an opp-irtueity of benne fired with enthu,iasm for the country's cerise, ablr, to offer their services!, and willingly too, and ieedy !o jeopardize their lives ? Who would have Al:lug:at that all teat would have Come to pass, and that that day one of their number would be lying in a seleierea grave 6,U00 miles from Warrington ? It was a saleesr'e 'teen' although it was met in facing the grim ervene disease, equal to an soldier's death as if he hid faeentn stermine the trenches on the way to leadyken•th. To he a volunteer was no jesting matter, aid it seemed to him that the fact that so large a prop or' on of the eatteilen was then at the Front should make three, Weo were shortly going to cemp go in a more eericioe reerit, It wou'd make them feel, it they had nat already reeeeee nis-d it, that it was a matter of the yekitest impertance to be a soldier of the Q seen, aid induce thein to even themselves of every opportunity to improve thorn:l4mi and increase their efficiency foe national selfelefereese for which, althceigh simewh at tardily, the nation had dis- covered they had e.onsidereble aptitivie. 11 cencluaion the preacher urged them to become not (rely soldiers of the Queen but soldiers of J s rs Chrikt.</p>
      </div>
