      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>PROPOSED HOSPITAL AT SAN KEY.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED HOSPITAL AT SAN KEY.</p>
        <p>Ratepayers' Meeting.</p>
        <p>_ A meeting of ratepayers, residents, and owners of property was held in the Board Schools, Liverpool-road, Great Sankey, on Tuesday evening to discuss the question of the propoeed erectioa of a small-pox hospital near Linglcy Green by the Warringten •Corporation and to decide what steps should be taken in order to prevent the carrying out of the proposal. The chair was occupied by Mr T Halton (chairman of the Parish Coun- cil) and there was a large attendance which included Messrs J H Kilshaw, F Joynson. W Taylor, II Goedier, J H Smethurst, J Fulton-Smith. H Singleton, Newcombe Halton, Dennville, Joseph Wright, Thompson, John Houghton, R W Smith, W Bingham, T Kilshaw, A Buckley Mellor, W Furness, Joseph Furness, W D Jeans, T Perks, Jehn Johnson, J Robinson, and Mr J Smith (clerk of the Parish Council). The Clerk read letters of apology from several persons who were unable to attend the rneeting• Mr J Knowles wrote hoping that they would endeavour to ward off if pessible one of the most revere blows that ever had or over would be levelled against the health and prosperity of the township. A letter was real from Mr. J. C. Sutton (clerk of the Rural District Council) stating that at the last meeting of the Council it was decided that the Medical Officer, Surveyor and Inspector should visit rh . site and report as to its suitable isolation. &amp; The Council wbulel con- sider the matter at the next meeting. The Clerk also read a letter he had written to the Leca,l Government Board pointing out that the Corpor- ation proposed to erect the hospital in the parish,that the Parish Council considered the site to he altogether un- suitable, and hoped that the Cerperation would be re- quested to hold a iipublic inquiry at which property owners and ratepayera would tie allowed to state their objections before the scheme was officially sanctioned. The objecrions were numerous and of a serious character. It would be probably desirable to employ legal assistance. The expense would not exceed a penny in the pound,and they would be glad to know whether the Board agreed. If no pubic inquiry were held they asked to he allowed an opportunity of conveying their objection by other means. The Chairman said the meeting would agree with him that the erection of a smallpox hospital would be detri- mental to the district. In the first place he thought that the Parish Council should have been consulted by the Corporation of Warrington before it was ever attempted to purchase the land. Sankey wag a growing residential district and the hoepital would be very objectionable. He along with two colleagues on the Rural District Council, who were selected as a Roads Committee, visited the site a few days ago. His cal leagues aereed with him that it was unsuitable in many ways. The ratepayers could oppce,e the scheme on one ground because of the great amount of property in the immediate vicinity. Referring to the matter of sanitation he said the Corporation would be compelled by the Local Government Board to provide a sewage scheme but that would not secure the retepas era' position. If they con- structee filter beds for instance, there was property all round, and the germs could be carried fully three quarters of a mile. Wherever a hospital was erected it must be detrimental to those who resided near it. Mr: William Furuess said he was opposed to the scheme tooth and nail. He thought it was a most ob- jectionable site and the prevailing winds would carry the disease. The land was flat and marshy and there was no provision for drainage whatever. Even if the Cor- poration provided drainage in the shape of beds for pre- cipitation, a great quantity of the water must run into the Sankey Brook. That brook ran through a great part of the eountry and cattle drank from it. He thought their case should be a very strong one. Alderman Satton said at the last meeting of the Town Council, that there waa no fear of contagion. Ho differed from him acid had copied an extract made from a report which Dr. Gornall gave to the Health Commit- tee soon after the epidemic occurred in Warrington. Ira that report be said " I can call to your remembrance the importance of the separation of a small-pox hospital from any centre of population and remind you of the painful lesson that followed the ignoring of that principle in Warrington in 1892." The painful lesson was this. When the site was selected on the north east side of the town he (the speaker) believed there was only one isolated case of small-pox. Before the hospital had been in operation for more than a month,in Stamford-street there was only a single house in the whole street that was not effected by small-pox ; not to mention the fact that the epidemic on the east side was ten times worse than any other part of the town. Dr. Tnorne said that no site was suitable if within a radius of a quarter of a mile there was a population of 150. Sankey, however, could not claim to have a population of 150 within a quarter of a mile's radius of the site. But the doctor went on further to say that oven where these conditions were fulfilled, if there was a considerable population within a Half-mile radius it was not a suitable site. He (the speaker) considered that there was a considerable population on the marein of the half-mi!e radius and within five or six minutes' _</p>
        <p>walk of the site there was a large board school. In 1895 the Medicil Officer and Borough Surveyor made a pre- iiminary report with reference to a small-pox hospital and which stated thlt after visiting several spots in the</p>
        <p>neighbourhood of Waal ngton they had come to the conclusion that a portion of the Pear Tree F arm, near Houghton Green was a most suitab,e site. Acting on that report the Health Cornmitree rented the farm from the Waterworks Committee at the rate of £BO per annum. On March 3rd, 1896 the Town Council passed the minute approving the site. It seemed a very curious ning that the Warrington Corporation should now propose spending nearly £2,000 on the site in Sankey. He considered that the reason was that the Pear Tree Farm site was too expensive. He did not see why they should, for the sake of saving the Warrington Corporation a few thousand pounds, have fixed in their midst an objectionable hospital like a small-pox hospital ' would bo.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. H. Smethurst said that abont SIX months ago he was asked by the Borough Surveyor if he was inter- ested in any land in Great Sankey on which a small-pox hospital could be built. He told him that they had a lot of land but they should be very chary about offering it because they knew that the building of the hospital would be a ert,at disadvantage. He, bowever,suggested a portion of land but said that in the event of there being objections by the ratepayers they were willing to stand down and should not sell the land in face of objec- tions by the people. The land was inspected and he (Mr. Smethurst) did not hear anything else until he knew that the Corporation proposed to purchase Mrs. Hutton Wright's land, The Borough Surveyor told him that morning that the committee were quite prepared to receive a doputation.</p>
        <p>• The Chairman then proposed "That this meeting of ratepayers, residents, and owners of the property in Great 5,.nk,•57 unanimously agree that the site near Lingley Green selected by the Corporation of Warring- ton for the erection of a smallpox hospital is a most un- suitable one, and take this opportunity of recording a most emphatic protest against such proposal, and further that the following onjections amongst others are included I—(1) unsanitary, (2) places or sites t more elevated, (3) prevailing winds from present site selected are towards the town, (4) cases would largely be brought through the township, (5) there is a public school within a stone's throw almost of the site."</p>
        <p>Mr Thompson seconded the motion which ws unanim- cusly agreed to.</p>
        <p>%tr. Singleton proposed "that a committee composed of the members of the Parish Council, together with the following persons, namely, Mesqts Jeans. J Fdton Smith, Snoddy, Thompson, W Furness, and J H Smethurst; be and is hereby authorizsd to represent the parish on the qu^stion, and they are hereby authorized to employ such legal and other assistance as to them may seem proper, with power to add to auch committee." Mr. Houghton seconded, and it was unanimously carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Singletnn said he was under the impreFsion that the hospit.l W 3,3 to be built in the interest of three towns, Warrington, St. Helens, and Widnes.</p>
        <p>. , fhe Chlirman : That has fallen through, I under. stand.</p>
        <p>Mr. Singleton said he hui been infermed by gentlemen !lying in St. Helens and Wilnes that there never was any af,reement. They had their own hospital and did not neei AS sistance from any other borough. Mr. Furness: Have you had any communication from the Town Council or the Health Committee that has gi Vr n you the knowledge that the three townships united in the matter P</p>
        <p>['he Chairman : Not directly. Mr. Fume: s: lam rather inclined te think that n,thirg tolk place. _ _</p>
        <p>A vote of o'i-inks was unanimously recorded to Mr. Atton for ra%idint. _ _ _</p>
        <p>in src ,nding the-prpportion. Mr. Thompson thanked Mr. Ualoii ler the wanner in which he had taken up t)e qtiestiun. They all admired his determination and frclTl what thy read in the newspapers he had fought a' haul,. s well as he could single handed. Mr. Ha on brit fl, acknowledged the compliment and the mettrig termiaated.</p>
      </div>
