      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>RESULT OF</head>
        <p>RESULT OF</p>
        <p>SPRING ILJLEANING COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>We have received such a number of really good parodies on Kipling's celebrated verses that we should like very much to give the prize to several dozens of them. As that, how- ever, is impossible. we hive had to make a choice and have decided upon which we think is really the most appropriate. We publish this one, and also a selection of the others, net adjudging them the beat of those which hdive been sent in, but as a representative selection. We desire to thank all those who have taken the trouble to send us contributions towards this competition.</p>
        <p>X3was AWAIZDED to EDWARD H. DAVIS, Harborne, for the following verses :—BO Or</p>
        <p>ADVICE to HOUSEWIVES.</p>
        <p>When you're started with Spring Cleaning—and you want</p>
        <p>- to do it clean (And you know you cannot do it with your mouth)— Will you kindly buy a bottle of our Furnitawrer Cream,"</p>
        <p>WEich is used in -East and West and North and South. it's a powerful kind of compound, and It's very good to mix, And you and Jane can take it as you find it. When it's used in active service, cleaning stains from off the _ " sticks," You will find it wont leave many marks behind it. Boots slsop, Boots shop, shop of a hundred things, An enterprising Company, it's all the rage to-day, Everything sold for the lowest cash (as you can all see for yourselves), Then step in ladies, take your choice, and pay I pay I pay There are wives who do it secret—asking no permission to— For their husbands wouldn't give it if they did; But suppose you use oar " polish"—it will make things look</p>
        <p>like new—</p>
        <p>And you need not stop lila work "co" there's a kid. You could do your cleaning casual—wituout worry—when he's gone,</p>
        <p>For if the beggar absent never mind him. You might use our "Wills' Ammonia," which would take away the smell Of tobacco—which he always leaves behind him. B3ots shop, Boots shop, etc.</p>
        <p>You can manage so as later, when you look him in the face, You can tell him what he'll think is only fair, How that while he earned the wages you had tidied up the place And had polished up his special old arm chair. He's an absent-minded beggar, and he may forget it all, But you do not want his kiddies to remind him That you locked them in the coal house while you Gleaned his house and hall, Awl were bright'aing up the home he'd left behind him. Boots shop, Boots shop, etc.</p>
        <p>When you've started on your cleaning, when you're scour- ing all your rooms, Use " Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing," made by Wills, You can buy it for a shilling, and you'll find its pleasant fumes All the dirt and noxious germs quite quickly kills, Boots' an advertising beggar, but his stores are very great, And 1 and you must take him as we find him, He'd sooner have your cash than put it en a slate, And he's leaving all the other shops behind him. Cooks Boots, dukes Boots, Boots of a hundred kings, Boots of a market public en, he'll fit them all,we say; With Varnish, or Cream. and Paint, and thmsands of other things, Ask for them all for your cleaning's sake and pay, pay, pay. There are girls who make no secret,'cas they'vehto occas:on tea Boots Cream to polish furniture is good, That Gold Paint as made by Morgan is the finest thing that you Can gild upon old ormolu or wool. There are girls you talk to casual, will assure you that they Jove To polish up their brasses, and their tins, With Paste that's sold at Boots, so much better, and abore All others, covering multitudes of sins. Cooks' sins, dukes' sins, sins of a belted earl, Fifty thousand soldiers' sins, gone from them astray, Buttons and buckles and gun, will shine 'our khaki girl, Buy it quick for your polishing's sake, to-day, day, day. There are families by thousands using Bootses]eaken Stain To furbish up bare spots around their floors, It saves them yards ot carpet, for it will wash again When varistsed well to close the open pores. You can polish up the chairs with the Cream they sell Boots— You need not go to Africa to find bins, He has so many Isranchee, from scores of good old roots, He is mostly at the front, nor looks behind him! Dukes' Cream, cooks' Stain, Polish for metals, (MA Paint, These are the things you will ask for going to Boots to-day, With Ammonia, Cloudy Cleansing, to save you from a faint, Take yoar cash in your pocket and pay, pay,pay. Nom de plume -JACOB WILSON, Nottingham.</p>
        <p>If your slavey goes and leaves you when spring cleaning's coming ea, And you cannot get anoher maid that suits. I will tell you how to manage with little extra tail, Just walk off to the nearest shop of bOOTS. They are civil and obliging, you will very quickly find, Their goods are cheap and fresh and up to date. And you'll buy for say one shilling what most firms would sell for three, As they trade for cash and do not keep a elate. Pure Drugs, Gold Paint, Furniture Cream and Pills, Stuff for saving elbow grease, powders and various roots, Stains and Polish of every kind, things that will ours all ills, So cease to fret, but put on your shoas and just try 5001'S. If you think your chairs and tables get a little worse for wear, And the sideboard is not what it used to seem, Don't break your curs and saucers and say you wish you're dead. For 4d. only need be spent on Cream. With a rag apply it gently, any cbhd can put it on, It's as easy if you want to stain the floor. Spend another 40. on a pot of Varnish Stain, Then your home will look just as it did of yore. Soft Soap, Brunswick Black, Paste for a hundred tins, Oils and Paints of every kind, Cloudy Ammonia, too, Each of them certain todo its work, just as you're told It will, So keep home dean for your credit's sake and you'll not rue. When you've finished in the bedroom, and the parlour and the hall. Although you're well repaid don't think you're done, I•There's the pAs and pans and kettles want a little clean- ing out, So again to BOON you'll have to run; Get a tin of Metal Polish, just one penny does the triok, No stains or scratches you will ever trace, Then you'll sing about your housework and be glad you are alive, And in everything you'll see a smiling face, Cash Chemists, Good Chemists, things at the lowest rate, Everything superior for a price tic ,t always suits, 50,000 housewives sing praise of one accord, And never deal with another firm but Boor!, BOOTS, BOOTS, F, JOHNSON, 4, Victoria-rd., Peaketh, Emir Warrington.</p>
        <p>When the Springtime surreptitiously steals on us unawares With a sudden light of pale and sickly sun. Then the prudent British matron takes to polishing the stairs, And there's lots of other house-work to be done. So in order that the mansion may be thoroughly made bright' BOOTS VARNISH STAINS and other things are bought. And the hubby's cousolation is in thoughts of speedy flight, For he doesn't uaderstand things as he ought. Rub, rub, scrub, scrub, ain't it awful work. So the hubby thinketh, though he really doesn't know One little bit about it when he fancies terrors lurk, And the best thing he can do is to go, go, go. Oh. the saving in the eoal bills with thecoming of the Spring, When the black bacteria wobble in their flight, And the income tax collectors and the birds begin to sing, And the dirty smoky c•-ilings turn to white. If you view it as you ought to it's a most cougenial time. With UNRIVALLED METAL PoLISH to the fore. In burnishing door handles and removing all the grime From the metals while the women scrub the floor. Rub, rub, scrub, srub, making things look like new, FURNITURE RE am and Bnustawlces BLACK are the articles tuat pay. Buy all you want at Boots, you'll keep that well in view? And do your work when hubby's out of the way, way, way. When the work is nearly over there's a transformation scene, And you don't regret you've tekled it in time, As yom realize the fact that now your house is nice and clean, To have left it dirty would have been a crime. Oh! the glory of the carpets which Low look as good as new, Oh the brightness of the fenders and the chairs, It's surprising to the hubby as he takes a startled view. And the wife enjoys hie wonder as he stares. What;, what! what, what I done the whole lot in a day? (Heaven seems to open to the guilty man, who scoots) Wonderful! how did you do it? Tell me your secret pray, "I did it all with things I bought from Boots, Boots, Boots." C. .P. JONRS, Solicitor, 6, St. Andrews-street, Cambridge.</p>
