      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>Th THE rushing or" khaki" e Next General party is finding that there Election. are many matters to be c°ftsidered in connection with a General Election Which they have entirely overlooked. They have fired attention solely on the gain it would give to the party in office, regardless of the disturbance it Would occasion in the country, and its effect upon Parliament and the composition of the Unionist Party. A General Election in July or August Would be a serious disturbance to the business of the country. It would injure trade, disturb agri- culture, interfere with holidays, and lead to heated controversy upon many matters as yet imperfect Dr unready for clear presentation. An election nleans that the new Parliament would be sum- inoned at a given date, six weeks or so ahead. 'Ant is, in September or October the new Perna- till.ent would have to assemble, and some Into must elapse before it could be fully established and ready for business. In other Words, three months would be occupied by nilsettling politics at a time when the harvest and the holidays usually occupy attention, to the exclusion of nearly everything else. The effect this election upon the inner politics of the rolliSt party would be considerable. Some l'iheral Unionists would be attacked, and probably iose their seats. The power of Conservatism would e increased, and the reactionary party, of which, 1.4 social politics, at any rate, Lord Salisbury is the Intellectual leader, wculd be increased. Some es, trangement would naturally follow. Questions 1° which Liberal Unionists were keeoly interested I,!_nuld recede,and the party would be discontented. What other gains would there be? It is doubtful Whether the nominal majority of the party at resent in office would be increased. If it were it would have lost in the process. If it were, would be purely a war majority, and its compet- :ea could be denied on every other question. Of tB. general policy there could be no sort of cer- wailltlY. But it is usually said that it tgive the Government power to sattle 3ub African question on the lines In nutted to the country, and that no Govern- .r.3 11t has a right to annex without that authority. 'RD ROBERTS has annexed and renamed the' Urange Free State without any such authority, and so half the matter is already :settled. The ilatention to annex the Transvaal ia part of the 'overnment's present policy, and it looks as if it Would be complete before even a Dissolution or a General Election can come. If the annexation can thus be settled, what other questions of vital coo- are ther, for submission to the couatry ? n indemnity is already accepted in principle. .oiilitary rule for a time is absolutely certain. Are the new States to be Crown Colonies or what? Is tilt at the question which the country has to assist Government in deciding? It strikes us as the tr,ltst saisfactory matter to be left open. The rvernmeut must have a policy, based on annexa- vi°l3, and that means loss of independence. Every- qing else is simplicity itself. When peace is 4i.ranged the terms may be submitted to Parlia- ;lent in an autumn Session, and agreed to. Tha °Vernment will then have seen the thing through the appeal to the country can be arranged for lat the least inconvenient time. The verdict will 4°,t be one upon our military operations in the :4, but on the Imperial situation and the do. %tic situation gPnerally. Army reform, tam- r:ellnee reform, social reform, cannot be wisely tihiled up with our successes in South Africa. We 4411 want to attend to our affairs, and Lord e aIIERTs, Lord KITCHENER, and General BULLER hatleet help us very much in getting therr(linto a vetter condition. In fine, the next General Elec- 111°4 has to settle policy for the years to come, and 13t for the few weeks ahead of it.</p>
        <p>, TiE policy of the country Ministr of Educatioyn. upon elementary education is not always the policy of the 4inlster of Education for the time being. is left to his decision, so much depends upon tbis initiative, he is; o apt to be biassed by pressure Zia Way and that, and he is so often himself in- Posed to be impartial, that abuses arise and vances are left untouched, and angry feelings Ltise that impede the wishes of the country. The t"lieY of the present Minister of Education, as 4t4rtied out by Sir JOHN GORST, has been con- tly and objected to by a considerable ifltt, 3' in the House of Commons and the country. i_446 not always been clear,energetic and resolute. parents have demanded the protection 4 the conscience clause and they have been ft,'4All°lllleed by the rector of the parish them the pulpit it is surely the duty of dee Education Board to remonstrate. A like itllliunciation from the altar in a Catholic church 40t.reland would arouse the Irish G3vernment to sz;°. When the Flint case was brought befere to- OR CroasT he thought it a sufficient answer bi flay that he was not the clerical gentleman's g:lf.°l3! Need we wonder that feelings of dis- are aroused? It is the duty of the Minister fair.'"ducation to administer the law with strict %paness as between contending parties, and not to tojtie tebukes that would be effective when erratic 1% 0 Iflet et this kind is brought to his notice. It bY such means that the present arrange- can he maintained. The question of train- asl:3llsges is another point,and it is not as simple timit If the State gives grants it has some %Ate t° see that the doctrines taught to the stud- -I,4s.ehallnot be subversive of the national religion. egtee mentioned in the House of Commons on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>Monday, in which an ecclesiastical manual was in use, teaching that the Protestant faith was an un- mixed evil, is one that could not have been al- lowed to exist under a strictly impartial Minister. Other cases were referred to in which Papist prac- tices were constant and notorious, and nothing was done until the ARCHBISHOP asked the clergy- man to retire. Sir JOHN GORST is very adroit in dealing with complaints of this kind. He demand:, particulars, he investigates, and he submits the defence to a much more favourable interpretation than he does the attack. In nine cases out of ten, he never discovers anything in the attack, and he is always eager to put the most plausible inter- pretations on the defence. This is not the true province of a State official. He should be just and fair all round. A few words from him would often remedy an evil at once, but they are seldom penned. The assumption invariably is that the Nonconformist c mnplainant has no case, and that if he has a case the best thing to do with it is to gloss it over with a joke about the Bishop or the politics behind it. The recent change in the way of specially favouring the voluntary schools ought to have been attended with extra caution in meet- ing the complaints of Nonconformists. More fre- quently, the contrary spirit has been shown. The House of Commons as at present constituted is strongly biassed in favour of the Voluntary schools, in favour of allowing clerical managers the utmost liberty in the matter of Ritualistic teaching, and it usually supports the Government in any diffi- culty, even where the principles of Protestantism' are involved. g The Education Board cannot escape the feeling that it need not be very particular under such circumstances, and particular it Certainly is not. The partiality it has recently displayed has made a strong impression on the minds of Nonconformists and of National Protestants. We shall hear more of the subject when the country is consulted, and when votes are asked for in support of the policy recently pursued in taxing the patience of Nonconformists beyond endurance, and treating all their complaints as evidence of amazing credulity.</p>
        <p>THE election camplign wrkicii is in progress in the United States has at least produced some ot the finest specimens of spread-eagleism with which orators have ever attempted to astonish the world. Though there may be still Some things which are better dine in this " effete Monarchy " than in the land of freedom across the Atlantic, there is no doubt that for campaign oratory the palm must be given to our cousins. Thus at a recent meeting one of the candidates was described as "a states- man of ripe experience, a philosopher and orator without a peer on this or any other continent," "clearly the greatest American citizen of the age," "this magnificent man, this matchless statesman, hero, and orator." On our side of the ocean such remarks by the candidate's chairman should lead to bloodshed. Then in Kansas City we have the declaration that the citizens " are ready to give -- our votes again; yea, more, we are ready to worship at his shrine, and give to him the hot blossoms of a noble surrender as the only hope, the only salvation of this nation." And who in this country would have the assurance to compete with the Populist Senator from North Carolina in his glowing affirmation that" there are times when the human tongue can grow eloquent in painting the sterling growth of some great character whose virtues are not fully known to the world; but when the duty is devolved upon any man to attempt to express with human tongue anything to add to the greatness, the sterling worth of such a man as William J. Bryan the human tongue is not equal to the task 9"</p>
        <p>The inventors of " anti-ethylene," the serum by the inoculation of which the liking for alcoholia liquor is to be overcome, are not disheart-ned by the ridicule which was poured upon the discovery on its first announcement. It appears that fifty- seven cases of drunkenness have since been treated with serum drawn from intoxicated horses; there have been thirty-two complete cures and eight partial recoveries, the remaining seventeen cases being admitte4 failures. A complete cure, one assumes, is when a drunkard is changed by treatment into an enthusiastic teetotaler, while a partial recovery leaves him a moderate drinker with a strong leaning to the side of respectability. In the seventeen cases o! failure there were, we learn, pathological complications which interfered with the action of the cure, a tendency to neuro- sis, in particular, being fatal to the patient's chances of recovery. All this is interesting enough, but it is noticeable that there is no attempt whatever to meet the main criticism directed against this treatment, the charge that,at its best, it only affords a temporary cure by hyp- notic suggestion. It is hardly necessary even to bring hypnotism on the scene, some of the effect being doubtless due to the goodwill of the patient, who knows what is expected of him after inocula- tion, and does his best out of mere kindness of heart to f ulfil the wishes of the experimenters. A well-know similar case occurred early in the cen- tury, when Mesmer was working wonders with huge magnets which produced certain psychical phenomena in subjects who plac-KI themselves between the poles, the magnetic theory of these results being, however, upset when it was dis- covered that, provided the subject knew nothing of the change, wooden magnets realistically painted possessed equal powers. One would like to ascer- tain the effects of pure water on the human system when introduced with the formalities which doubt- less accompany inoculation with "anti-ethylene."</p>
        <p>The Queen will never have another 81st birth- day and probably there will never be another relief of Blafeking, while it is still more unlikely that two such events will again happen in one week. It is perhaps fortunate that such is the case, for a repetition of auspicious occasions of this kind would greatly endanger the mental equilibrium of the Anglo-Saxon race. Already,we fear,the nation has lost something of its reputation for decorum, i-nnassivity, and self-command. The British have ...coo a people gone mad during the past week, but as the madness all proceeds from joy it cannot do much harm. Our neighbours across the channel will, no doubt, see in it further signs of the weakening of our national character, and if they find any consolation in the idea they are welcome t) it. The national character will stall i much more than brief attacks of midsummer madness. After all there is cause enough for all this jubila- tion. No sovereign was ever more beloved than is our gracious Queen, and her people are unfeign- edly and rightfully happy in commemorating her entry on another year oF sovereignty, and re- joicing at the same time in the safety of the little band of heroes who have so long upheld the flag in the face of the foe.</p>
        <p>At last it has come ! Our dear Parisian friends have not let pass the opportunity of sneering at England's recent joyful manifestations. Hear what the " Libre Parole " says :-" I would ask what would London do when in a future war the siege of Portsmouth or Plymouth is raised? And this is English joy! Well, their splenetic sadness is preferable to this orgie,which reveals the depths of their barbarous, brutal, and ferocious souls." We do not know tho writer of these charming lines and sweet expressions, and we feel sure the loss is ours. He must be a nice man, and possessed of a soul neither barbar- ous, brutal, nor ferocious or ho would not be so amiable. Perhaps if he came to see what London would do under the remarkable circumstances mentioned he also might find cause for a display of splenetic sadness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>FIB TO RHRUNIATIO IND PARALYTIC</head>
