      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE l, 1900. MATTERS OF MOMENT. ;RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE l, 1900. MATTERS OF MOMENT. ;RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter. Whitsuntide, now close upon us, is something, mare than a political holiday. It is a pause in tte world of fashion. as well as of politic-, aid cora- rn-rce. Givers of entertainments are supposed to , hare worked hard since Easter, hard enough to make a little rest now net unwelroaa a It comes like a tortnight's frcet in midwinter to the hunting world alter an open season, with a succession of hard rune, in November and December. The law- yers, too, take their holiday at this period of the year, and probably leave the courts behind them without any of that lingering regret which had been attributed to certain members of Parliament when they take a farewell glance of the House of Cocarnone, though it be but for a few days. It would be difficult to say which of the three prefee- sloes has worked the hardest, that of politics, the law, or of society, but members of all three like unto m re common or or inary mortals, are glad to doff their armour by the end of May and take their 81.+0 in their tents for a time. All will reassemble when Whitsuntide is over? Though it is said that when Parliament, adjourned this week, it was for a longer holiday than ever before England can scarcely be looking other than lovely at the begin- ning of June. Everything is now at its greenest. Field, meadow, and hedge-row have by this time donned their full summer dress, unsmirched as yet by the dusts of July and August. There is, indeed, no time of the year when England is seen to so much advantage as just on the brink of the hay harvest. Just before the scythe begins its ravage, the elnglise meadow is in all its glory. It is so now; and for all of us. old or young, rich or poor, greet or small, the Whits Intide holiday,when it falls in the raonen of June, should offer tempting °ppm tunities for rubbing off something of the every-day dust of toil, and refreshing ourselves with a few draughts of that rural beauty and country air which we need not leave old England to find and which, in soma of its aspects, we can find nowhere else. Pos .ibly on the unierstanding that "what every- body says must be true " the impression that after the declaration of another victory, of a decisive character, on the part of the advancing Brigs') Army in South Africa, an appeal to the country will he made as speedily as possible, appears to have become fixed in the public mind, and there is much talk of what will be done in the way of political organization—after Whitsuntide. About six weeks since there was the same kind of feeling as to what was to be done, after Easter; and possibly the result may prove much the same in each instance. But, be this as it may. an earnest hope is expressed, on both sides and everywhere, that the introduction ) of certain reforms relating to the Army may not be , delayed, since, if the appeal is to come soon, the ' electors will require to be satisfied that the service is to be placed upon a more satisfactory footing than it is now found to be, as regards both offi ems and men. It appears that recruiting during the war, particularly in the provinces, has been enor- mous. During the first four months of last year the number of recruits was fourteen thousand, while the four monthr, of this year brought in twenty-two thousand. It was not expected that this increase would continue, and the reforms which were indi- cated have ben well received in Parliament, though they do not exhaust the needs of the Army, which it is believed can be met without any very great increase of expenditure. There is scarcely a district in the provincee,town or urban council, that has not at one time or another had its difficulty as to the disposal of sewage. The most prevalent system is known to be largely responsible for the pollution of rivers, and other evils.and there are special reasons why ex- periments in the bacteriological treatment of sewage should everywhere be watched and noted with the keenest interest, Bence attention is here directed to a report of Dr, Clowes and Dr, li ous' on, which has been presented to the London County Council, and to a report by Mr. Baldwin Latham. Dr. Frankland,and Dr. Perkin on the trial of the system which has been made at Manchester. The substance of the former document, is that the experi- ments at the Crossness Sewage Works, where three coke filter-beds have been in operation,have proved eery satisfactory as regards its bacteriological com- posirion. It is, of course, important to have the assurance that the effluent is quite sweet and free from smell, that it will not cause any deposit on the river bed, and will in no way interfere with fish life in the stream. It is claimed for the new system that it requiries no chemicals,produce,s no offensive sludge, that the whole of the suspended m irter is removed and fifty-one per cent of the oxidizable (putrescible) matter is removed. The result of the ianchester experiment, confirmed by inquiries in other parts of the country, is, that the bacteriolo- gical method is the only one likely to yield satis- factory results with Manchester sewage. The experts who have reported upon the subject recom- mend tanks and beds to be at once erected, which is certainly a " large order" for a population like that of qanchester. The bacterial system is far less costly that the ordinary, and may or may not justify all the hopes that have been formed. This has yet to be ascertained and proved. The Board of Guardians which will please every- body has yet to be 'invented." There are, of course, two sides to the question of the method on the part of these Boards of receiving and accepting contracts—a tradesman's and a ratepayer's. Bristol grocers have just now a grievance against the Bristol Board which other tradespeople in other towns and districts have against their Boards re- spectively. Outsiders, they consider, are favoured too much. It is urged by the local Grocers' ARSo. cietion that the members of that body contribute largely to the poor rates,that at one time it was the practice of the Guardians to allow"only local firms" to tender for the supply of goods,and that hope is in- (Niger' that such custom will again be allowed ti Prevail. As yet, the ratepayers' point of view has not been submitted, Indeed.the whole question has yet to be thrashed out, and when and wherever it is, will affozd a fine opportunity for" Little Eng- landism " controvery on a reduced scale. Eleven thousand pounds in prizes for the forth- coming shooting at Bisley should prove a tempting offer to expert "shots" among the Volunteers. As ! regards Volunteers,their employers,and the encamp • meats, some difficulty presents itself in the agricul- tural districts. Employers have been asked by Volunteer Officers to allow such of their men as are Volunteers to attend camp from the first to some other week in August, and as this represents the busiest time of harvest, there is a reluctance to comply. garly or mid-July has been suggested in some districts, and there seems to be nothing for it but that each lecality shall have its own arrange- ment, to suit officers. men, and employers, as far as it can be made practicable. The farmers, as a dare), appear willing to oblige the other parties to the een,ract or understanding,and that counts for much in trying to settle the difficulty. In consequence of the concessions respecting the formation of rifle clubs recently granted by the War Office, the attention of the men likely to join is drawn to the meagre supply of Lee-Enfields to b - served out, namely, one to ten men. The fact of one rifle being handed round between ten men will mean a loss of time, both to the men and their in- structors, as well as being in other respects abso- lutely absurd. In a newspaper correspondence the question has been raised whether the prayer for the High Court of Parliament ought to he read when there is a Par- liamentary holiday as at present. The point is new to most peopleincluding even the clergy;since, on a previous occasion when it was mentioned,atEaster, ie appears to have led to the prayer being omit- ted in one or two instances during a pertion of the itecees. Uniformity of service in the church is nGsurelly deeirable, as well as conformity to the Prayerebook direction.</p>
        <p>WIDNES CYCLE PARA.DE. On Thursday morning the result of the cycle parade and ladies' house-to•house collection at Widnes in aid of the local war relief and the Indian famine funds was made known. The ladies co,- lecLed £lOB 15s 7d, the stre,t collection amounted to £73 1:3 7d, and a ball and other effy,•',.., wili bring the total to abut £230. - MILTON P.S. A.., WIDNES. Thera was a good attendance at the open meeting of the above on Sunday, vf hen Mr G Lacy presided. Miss Parr of St. Helens, gave two excellent rendi- tions of the solos " Daddy " and" The Children's Home." Mr Councillor Evans, of Warrington, de- livered a capital address ou the social side cf Caris'aanity, which greatly impressed his hearers. SENT TO THE WORKHOITAE. John Thomas Phillips, aged 17 years,was brou;',ht up at Runcorn Police Court on Wedne•sday charged with lodging in an outbuilding in Mill-street and having no visible means of subsistence. P.C. Wik- son deposed to finding the prisoner in a trap be- hnging to Mr. Percival, butcher, The prisoner bad previously been in similar trouble, and he was ordered to be detained in the Workhouse for seven days.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A WIDNES JOINER.</p>
        <p>On Friday, a joiner named William Twist, resid- ing at 14, Victoria-road, Widnes, met with a serious accident. He was working on a scaffold about 16 feet big . a'.; the foundry, when he fell, sustaicing injuries ta his head and lower part ot the body. The ambulance was obtained, and he was removed to the Accident Hospital. Dr. Hutchinson attended him, and found hi i injuries to consist of a scalp wound and a serious wound on the left hip. I 1 THE CHARGE OF STEALING FOWL AT WIDNES. ,</p>
        <p>Oct Tuesday, at Liverpool County Sessions.doseph Bright (24), labourer, and Samuel Shone (22). labourer, were indicted on a charge of having at Widnes on the 28th April, feloniously stolen one drake and one duck, the property of Pater flodgkinson, Widnes. Shone pleaded guilty, and Bright not guilty. It was said that the two prisoners were together close to prosecutor's pond, on which were the birds, and they bad a gun with them. Shoni sho:-, a duck,and the drake was carried away by the prisoners. Both prisoners were ordered to be imprisoned for one dab. A PUGILIST CHARGED WITH NEGLECTING HIS CHILD-aEN.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Paice Court., on Monday morning, James Tolley, a travelling boxer, was charged with unlawfully negtecting his two young children— Henry, aged four years,and David,aged four mouths —in a manner likely to cau,,e them unnecessary suffering. The evidence showed that the children were found in a show van on the fairground in an emaciated condition,and were thoroughly neglected. Pri4oner had ample means to properly look after his children. He decamped with all the takings, amounting to £4, on April 28th, and was appre- hended on Friday last at Mountain Ash, South Wales. His wife was sent to gaol for one month at this court a week ago —Sergeant Ratcliffe said he paid special attention to the van. With the in- spector he entered the van,and there saw the child- ren. The van was dirty, and the woman was under the inflaence of drink. He fotin4 the young child lying on a bed, and in a neglected state. It was very thin and it ill-uourished. The children were taken to the workhouse,and had gained both weight and strength rapidly.—The defence was that the mother was to blame, and Tolley was discharged. ROBBERY FROM RUNCORN LLWN TENNIS CLUB.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday before Mr G L Davies and Mr Frei J Norman, two boys Joseph Spencer (13), and Gilbert Williamson (11), were charged with stealing two pairs of tennis shoes from the pavilion of the Lawn Tennis Club, Moughland-lane. Edgar S Lea, a nimmber of the club, said he had received numerous complaints of tennis balls and ladies' tennis shoes having been taken from the pavilion and in consequence he gave information to the police. The tennis shoes pro- duced were those which had been missed.—Dete?,-</p>
        <p>_ tive-Sergeant Gregory deposed that in consequence of information he had received he on Tuesday visited Mr. ffailwood's shop at the end or Byron-street and ssw the boy Spencer who admitted that he and Williamson had taken three tennis balls and a pair of ladies' tennis shoes each. Subsequently the Sergeant received the shoes frora tilt mothers of the prisoners.—The prisoners pleaded guilty.—Mrs. Spencer said her son told her he had found the shoes in Mr. Lake's empty house.—Mr. Davies said Mrs. Spencer was as:much to blame as the boys for receiving the shoes —The boys were bound over to come up judgment when called upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>A PUBLIC HOUSE ON QUICK9AND•—At the Borough Police Court, on Wednesday before Mr Birchall (in he rhai: and Councillor Smethurst, Mr Bro ne, on behalf of Meesrs Greenall, Whitley and Cmpany, Limited, applied for permission to alter the plans for the re-build- ing of the King and Queen Inn, Padgate-!ane. He said that at the annual licensing,* meeting permission was grante4 to Messrs Greenall and Whitley to re-build the Inn. Plans were drawn up for cellars to be built for the purpose of keeping beer. underneath the bar, tap room and kitchen. New, when they came to build the cellars they found to their astonishment there was quicksand, it was impossible to have the cellars there. It was propos.d to keep the beer in the yard, and he applied for perm i ssion to make the alterations.—The application was granted.</p>
        <p>TO THS DRAF.—A. gentleman who cured himself ef Deafness and Noises in the Heal after fourteen 5 ears suffering will gladly send full particulars of the remedy, on receipt of stamped addressed envelope.—P. CLIFTON. Ambe:rls House, Strand, London, W.o. Advt.</p>
        <p>ASk or CUNNINGHAM HIGH-CLASS; MINERAL WATERS—Advt. UNDER THE CLOCK FOB GOODPOOTS.—Advt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>WHITSUNTIDE HOLIDAYS</head>
        <p>THE WHITSUNTIDE HOLIDAYS</p>
        <p>On Whit-llonday there will be a children's car- nival at Runcorn. similar to that of the days gone by,and in the afternoon all will congregate and par- ade the town. In the morning the " Dukes Club" (ye olde club) will parade headed by tie Weston Church Band. Their first destina, ion will be the Parish Church, where it is announced the Rev Canon Wood (vicar) will preach. The " Rose of Snaron " and " Lily of the Valley" Lodges of the 0.U.0.0., will also parade headed by the Widnes Subcription and Gossageis Bands respectively. '1 heir rendezvous will be at the junction of High-street and Church- street, at a quarter to ten, from whence they will proceed to Halton, returning in time to join the children's pageant for the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ACCIDENT ON RUNCORN FAIR GROUNI9.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT ON RUNCORN FAIR GROUNI9.</p>
        <p>A serious accident took place on thti Runcorn Pair Ground on Thursday afternoon. Peter Hushin the two year old son of Nl.r. IV''Ham flusqm, of the" Rose and Shamrock" Runcorn, was playing with a number of other lads, and clambering up 801110 swing-boats piled on one side of the ground. when the boats upset and the children were thrown violently to the ground. Toe boy Ilus'iin fell upon piece of old scrap iron and sustained a deep cut no,,n his forehead and other bruises which Dr. Tbbinson who attended him described as dangerous. The little child now lies in a precarious condit;ion</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY -SRBSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY -SRBSIONS.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions last Monday, there were present on the Bench llessre. J. Handley (pre- siding), FJ Norman, A R Norman, and W Handley. The business transacted was very E ..,alkeld Lee, was granted a renewal of his license for the performance of stage plays at the Public ARIL—John Woodfine, Cooper •street, was fined .54 with an alternative of 7 days for drunkenness ; and James Denning, Brunswick-street; Henry Big,s, Shaw-street, and William Cavanagh, Cover-street, were each fined 61, and 43 61 costs, for neglecting the education of their children. BEGGING FROM A RUNCORN MAGISTRATE.</p>
        <p>James Cobbow, describing himself as a eailor,was brought before Messrs. F J Norman,and W Timmins at Runcorn Police Court yesterday (Thursday), charged with begging in High-street, on the pre- vious evening. From the evidence of P S Gregory appeared that the prisoner had only completod qeven days hard labour on Saturiay for a imiar offence and since he was liberated he had continued to practice although warned of the consequences. He had used very bad language, Mr Norman said the prisoner had stopped him in Alcock-street on Tuesday and asked for a penny for a stam D.—The prisoner was sentenced to a month's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Boy's MARVELLOUS ESCAPE.</head>
        <p>A Boy's MARVELLOUS ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>Last Thursday evening a boy named Thomas Mc Donald, residing at No. 7, Hartloy-str-et, was pro- ceeding to the railway station to fetch newspapers when, as he was passing over the crossing leading into Shaw-street he was accidently knocked down by a horse and cart, driven by Mr.fteub.3n Peacock, farmer, Higher Rugeora The who+el of the cart passel over the boy's thigh and back,but,strange to say,when subsequently examined by Dr McDougall, it was found that beye!id a few bruises he was none the worse for the mishap. No Mame is attached to the driver of the cart. CLAIM AGAINST A WIDNES BUILDER.</p>
        <p>Before bis Honour Judge C-illier, at the Widnes County Court, on Friday, Mr. El Sircom, timber merchant, Manchester, sued Mr. Charles Heming- way, builder, Widnes, for the RUM of £lB 171 2d. Mr. W E Farrington represented the platntiff and %ft J W Knowles defended. The case for the plain- tiff was that in October last year, the defendant ordered from plaintiff's traveller 20 lengths of spouting 6 by 4 o.g.,and 20 lengths of headed spout- ing. The goods had been sent, but the defendant had refused to accept them. Mr. Knowles submitted that only 401engths were ordered, and the plaintiff forwarded 57 His Honour pointed out that the amount of goods sent was not in accordance with the order, and he should give judgment for the de- fendant with Costs. NARROW ESCAPE FROM DROWNING AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Friday morning,a man—n—timel John Kirkham, blacksmith, No. 9,llowarth-street. narrowly escaped being drowned in the Bridgewater Canal, above Doctor's Bridge. It appears that the man was tak- ing a short cut home along the canal bank and staggered into the water. Thomas Robinson, No 5. Mason-street, captain of the Robert and the 41111te. went to his assistance,and immediately after- wards P.C. Edwards came upon the scene, and the two succeeded in getting Kirkham on the bank again. He was able to walk home with the assist- ance of Leonard Stubbs, of Norman-road, and Thomas Curzon, Vine-street. P.C. Elwards accom- ranie4 the man to his home, took off his wet clothes, and put him to bed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPRRANCE.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SONS OF TEMPRRANCE.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the above order nn Saturday evening in the Greenway-road Rebonl, Mr. Thomas R, Whyte in the chair, Mr; Bisbrown said :—They would all be pleased to hear that in their official 'ran, the "Son of Temperance" for June. there was a portrait and sketch of their friend Mr Thomas Fieesom. This was a distinction which they all appreciated. He felt that Mr. Heesom was worthy of the honour. He was one of the first workers when the cause was introduced in the town in the year PM. Hs had never for a moment swerved from his fidelity to the cause that lay so near his heart, and he had dons all that in him lay to advance the interests and preserve unsullied the repu'ation of the Sons of Temper,nce. Messrs Thomas Cooke, A. Noden, Thomas J Holt, and the Chairman (Mr Whyte) spoke in a similar strain, expressing the hops that their friend would be long spared to co- operate with them in temperance work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SCHOLA.RSUIPS FOR RUNCORN BOYS.</head>
        <p>SCHOLA.RSUIPS FOR RUNCORN BOYS.</p>
        <p>The results of the exe-Min- a-tions—fOi. the 36 scholar- .zhips or,)vided by Cheshire County Council for com- petition amongst the elementary schools in the County have lost been made public, and it is grati- fying to record that seven of the scholarships have been won in Runcorn district. One has been eecured by Halton Grammar School,one by Efelsby National School, two by Runcorn National School and three by Victoria-road Board (Boys) School. Of these suc- cessful scholars Richard Newport of Main street, Halton takes pride of place; being ninth on the list with 372 marks. The Burd School scholars are re- spectively 12th, 24th, and 26th on the list, their names being Jame.; Allan. 20 Greenwav-road, 361 marks Edwin Kilcross, 27, York-street 331 mark; John James Lunt, 50. lrwell-lane, 324 marks. The National School boys are 19th and 23rd on the list, their names being William Rowe, 28. Okell-street, 338 mark., and Charles 0 Faolkner,7, Parker-street, 334 marks. Elward Percy Hill, of 1, Elm Mount, Helsby. son of a schoolmaster, is last on the list, with 308 marks, and qualifies for a scholar- ship under the rule which allots twelve scholarahiss to students from rural schools only. The others are placsd in order of merit. About 400 boys and girls eat at the examinations, and 52 received over 70 per cent, of the 440 possible marks rendering them eligible for a scholarship, but t4ere were only 36 places and 16 were thus disappointed. It is worthy of note that the list is headed by a girl, Jennie Kenyon.of British School, Altrinchatn, with 418 marks, and the second is also of the same sex,Mabel Ryan. of Winnington Park School, who has 400 marks. The scholarships in Runcorn district will be tenable for two years at Runcorn Technical Institute. Two Runcorn boys, who received over seventy per cent. ot marks, but who are unfortunately not high enough on the list to obtain scholarships, are Gcorge Albert Warbur- ton, Norman-read (Runcorn National), 315 marks and Edwin F Williams. 17. Lowlands-road (Victoria- road Board School), 314 marks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[By A CORRESPONDENT] . The nerves of Runcornians have been highly strung for the last few weeks, awl they have had to put up with the screechings of sirens and hooters in- numerable. Their last taste of the "steam torture" was on Tuesday night when th 3 news of the occu- pation of Johannesburg by French was sufficient provocation for a "buzz." and large crowds occupied the streets up to a very late hour. Naturally the man-in-the street asked where the band was? and said" what a pity they could not turn out, and livsn things up ? ' My answer to that is—the man- in-the-street should treat the band better when it does turn out, especially if he wants it to appear again.</p>
        <p>The way in which Mr. Outhwaite and the Volun- teer Band have worked for ittuncorn's credit is praiseworthy. Their efforts on behalf of the War Fund and Charity, and in enlivening the departing of their " comrades " have been looked upon by them as a duty,and they have discharged it fittingly and well. But the perpetual " turn outs" on every occasion of rumoured success are toe great a strain upon even a bandsrnan'a "loyal patriotism," and when added to that there is precious little encour- agement behind it, it is not a matter of wonder- ment that the Band is sometimes conspicuous by its absence when the townfolk are shouting their heads (ff.</p>
        <p>The viewing of bodies, is a duty to which every man, who is called up to be a juryman, has a de- cided objection The law, however, says the body must be viewed, and the juryman conscientiously dis3harges his duty when he does view it. Run- corn jurymen under the late Hr. Worsley were granted the privilege of viewing the b3dy at their leisure. They were generally summoned an hour or two before required, and during the interval took the opportunity of Rotting their unpleasant task accomplished. Ten when the Coroner arrived they were solemnly: sworn that they had viewed the body and would return a verdict according to their conscience, and the melancholy proceedings of the Court at once commenced.</p>
        <p>Under the new dispensation of Mr. Dobson, whose administration of the law is far from being in accord with modern idea., the jury are summoned to the Court for a fixed hour, an I have sometimes to spend a quarter of an hour or twenty minutes waiting for the Coroner's appearance. Then comes the ceremony of swearing in,and the Coroner leads the twelve true and independent custodians of British justice like "sheep to the slaughter''in order to view the body under his personal surveillance, the man-in-the-street meanwhile wondering what all this "flummery" is about. By this antiquated procedure usually half an hour to threequarters is wasted, and though the jurymen, who are men of business, have seldom voiced their complaint in public, in private their words of condemnation are loud and deep. And who will not say that they and not the Coroner have reason on their side?</p>
        <p>The housing of the poor and w)rking classes is a question which our large towns have been forced to take into serious consideration, and the object lessons they hold out to small towns and communi- ties are such that no economic boly could well aff ird to ignore. Runcorn will one day grow into a big town, hence the Urban District Council should set about purifying and cleansing their district. There are many places where detestible hovels exist. True,most ot them are uninhabired,but their condition is none the less a menace to public health; indeed, some are veritable pent-houses oF filth and disease, and steps for their immediate demolition should be taken, or as an alternative their owners should be called upon to put their houses in order,</p>
        <p>I had a stroll round the cemetery one day this week, and although the cround is now well covered with memorials of those translated hence, these hid no effect of sadness upon me, and I enjoyed myself. With all due respect to the Parks and Commons Committee the Cemetery at the present time is the park of the town and many years must elapse be- fore the Common can hope to rival it in wealth of foliage. Not only are most of the graves in the Cemetery well kept, but there are numerous beds of brightly blooming flowers and the trees are in their best spring garb, while all the walks are neatly trim. The contlition of the Cemetery at the present time is indeed a credit to its energetic and painstaking custodian, Mr Ford.</p>
        <p>Warrington A .F.C. At the General meeting of the Lancashire Alliance held at Wigan on Monday evening last Mr. T. P. Bradshaw the secretary of the Warrington A 11.0. was unanimously elected vice-chairmaa of the Alliance for next. season</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Aquarium in the Park</head>
        <p>The Aquarium in the Park</p>
        <p>Fish were put hit() the slate tanks neat the aviary of the park on Sundav,and attracted a large number of spectators. The tanks, which are conapo,ed of slate, contain two ;oils of water each. One is stocked with Salm() Iridens (rairhow trou),and the other with Salmo Lovenen•is (Loch Leven trout). Pieces of rock and aquatic plants have been placed in the vnter,and the effect produced is very pretty. Inside the long shed adjoining, thousands of young trout, which have been hatched in the gardens, are to be 8.81).</p>
