      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>RUNCORN F.C. The Club at Cast Solvent.</head>
        <p>THE RUNCORN F.C. The Club at Cast Solvent.</p>
        <p>A Brilliant Season.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the Runcorn Foot- ball Clao was held at the Bethesda Schoolroom, on Tuesday evening last, when there was a good attendance of members present.—Mr. J. Ellison (secrenaty) read a letter from Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., (the presiden-,) deploring his inability to be pre- eent, and wishing the Club every success.-11r. Walter Jones (chairman of Committees) was then voted to the chair. THE SECRETARY'S REPORT. Mr. J. Ellison (secretary) submitted the following re- port :—" After the most glorious season in the history of the club he regretted to have to open his report with a tone of melancholy but elle record would net be com- plete without a refe.rence to the lamentable cierh of their esteemed late President Mr Map Speakmae, J.P., and County A'derm an, which occurred a few days after their last'annual meet Mg. Through fair weateer and through foul for many peers Mr Speakman had stood firm and s es.dfast his faith in the team and the club; no one was more proud than he of thedeeds accomplisnee and the good name the Weyer&amp; had achieved on flails of conqu:st far and wide. T.) their imagination it was, however, left to try and picture how he would have re- joiced at the high altitude the club had attained during ttoe past season, and he was sure he only voic fd the opinion of (Jeer member %vim he stated that Mr l..,peakrrian't timely and unexpected decease in the plenitude of his remarkably suceessful career, plunged all in grief. "Tee King is dead, long live the King!" was as much a truism that day as ever. In Mr. S. Taylor they had as a suc- cessor to Mr. Speakman, a president filled with enthusi- asm for the ancient game ; animated by lofty ideals of manly conduct, and a desire to help forward the club in every way ha possibly could ; and they hoped he would live for many years, in prosperity and happiness to icciipy the position in which they were delighted to see him. (Applause.) Corning to the work of the season just eneed,he felt hoeoured in being the mouthpiece te sound forth achievements so ably striven for and so brilliantly carried out. They commenced operations at Wigan and success crowned the efforts of the team by three tries to nil. Seven victories followed in succession. Then came the well-romembered collapse at Stoekoort, when their Cheshire r.vals snatched two points at Runcorn's ex- penso, with a score of ene goal 2 tries to ell. Circum- stances there were which in fact explaimed the defeat, but he disdained to iiesceed to eecuses. That it was a passing wave was shown by the fact that for four months, by splendid combination, skilful tactics, and high-spirited determination the team carrie I everything before them, only one draw that with Leigh, at Leigh sullyine the record. It was not until February 24th et Se Helens that the colours of the " Wearers of the Green " were agaia lowered, and that in a very lucky fashion—c insidering that Runcorn had decisive's' beaten B'. He'ene on the previous Saturday, and had scored a haedione run ov:r Salford, in mid-week after a stub- born contest in which the skilful play of the Runcorn men won the admiration of the Manchester «reties. It was no surprise that the men were a little stale and did not do themselves justice at St. Helens. The only other reverse, if such it may be called, was the draw of five points each at Widnes, on March 3rd. That left Run corn who headed the Lancashire Senior Competition- throughout the whole season—not once did tney lose pride of place—only one point ahead of their great Lan- cashire oppenents (Oldham), and the great battle for the championship teak place at Runcorn CO tee Saturday following, November 10:h. He felt safe in saying that no event hid ar iused such interest in football circles in Lanoishire and Cueshire for many long years past.lu an- ! ticipation of the great demand upon the accommodation , of toe Irwell-lane eround, the committee (pined neither labour, nor money in order to accommodate all present with a view. Ato iautifulty fine day favoured the fix- ture,and it was estimated that fully 15,000 persons were Present. Never in the history of Runcorn, either in football matches or other functions, had so many peeple been in.is•ed together at one time; and, they were pri- v Deed to witness one of the most splendia struggles in tee annals of Rugby football. Prom start to finish the match was full of life, strategy and vigour, and in the end Oldhem yielded the laurel wreath of victory to the " Caeshire lads," gracefully actenowledging that the honour had been worthily won. (Applause.) Thus ended the Laneaseire Senior Compotition in a blaZ9 of triumph. Five years only had the Northern Union been in existence, and yet twice head Runcorn won the cup and gold ineeats. They had moreover created a record in the Northern Uaien—(applause)—a record indeed— (continued applause)—far ahead of anything yet accem- relished. (Cheers.) The financial result he addel, by way of parenthesis, had equalled the deeds performed upon the field. As the foliowing fieures testified :-26 matches were played ; 22 were won; 2 drawn and 2 lost, and the points scor-d were Runcorn 35 goals, 54 tries. or 232 °lints ; to their opponents 6 goals, 7 tries, or 33 Deina; an average of 703 points to one. (Applause) Tie merits of the team were finally demon- strated when on April 21st Rancorn met a strong team selected from the rest of the clubs of th Lancashire Senior Competition and won by 10 points to nothing. Feeliags of jubilation had not subsided over that remark- able achievement, bet ore they became engaged in the Northern Union Can Ties. After Birstall and Maryport hal received their quietus, then began a series of three ' games with the Yorkshire champions Bradford, two being drawn before Runcorn demonstrated their superiority by 6 points to 2. A draw with Leeds Parish Churcn after an exciting contest followed at Leeds, and at Runcorn on the Wednesday afterwards the "Churchmen" snatched the victory in the eleveuth hour in a most flucky fashion, and Run- corn's hopes for any further conquesit its tnat competition were dashed to the ground. In the space of twelve days the team had played five ties which will ever memor- able in the history of the Northern Union. Then followed he newle resuscitated West Lancashire Cup Competition and it was Runcorn's feet tune to meet St Helens in the firaal at Widnes, and they did not yield the palm until three men had been disabled and left the field and extra time had been Mared. (epplause.) That was a brief review of the past season and never did Runcorn rank higher in the football world than it did to-day and it would be a wish, achieveel by all" that long may the club maintain its pre-eminent position." (Applause.) Tne Lencashire Cup was placed in the hands of the Captain, S Houehton, at the match at Runcorn v. Rest of the Competition and subsequently the president entertained the members of both first and " A " teams to dinner, a hospitality which was much appreciated and at the seine time he banded to the following players the shandsome geld medals they had so worthily won : 8 Elcughton, T Wilkinson, T Warder. J Butterworth, D Jenes, 0 Buteraeorn. J Faulkner, J Jotley, S Walker,ll Farmer, A Taylor, J Langley, R Reece, F Darlington, 0 Hughes, F Gayter, William Lightfoot and J Tomlinson.</p>
        <p>(applause.) The matches vuire as follow :—L,ague match-is : Plavel 26, won 22, low; 2, drawn 2, with a 6f:or,' of 35 54 tries (232 points) for, against 6 goals, 7 trios, (33 point.). Friendly' matches : Played 3, won 3 ; scoring 8 goals, 17 tries (67 paints) agaiiast ons Et'o4l (2 points). Northern Union Cup Ties: Played 7, won 3, drawn 3, list on', with a ,W,or,, ,-)f 7 goals 20 tries (74 points) Against 3 goals, 4 tries (18 points). West Lancasnire Cup (es : Played 3, won one, lost ono, drawn ,ha-k; scores 4 g,oals,B tries (32 poin's),against 2 ties (6 points). In 40 matcoes wero played of which 30 were won, 6 drawn anl 4 lost the total score being 56 coals, 101 tries (415 points) against 10 goals 13 tries (59 point), leaving a magnitlc nt ha!acca of 356 paints in the Club's favour. (Appian .e.) The scor,r6 wero a folly, :—Tri.s J Butterworth, 23 ; C Bu!terworth, 15 ; D Jones, 14 ; J Faulkner, 10 ; Ward,',, 7; r Wilkinson. 6 William Liz,htfoot, 4 ; S Vialkt-T, 4 ; J Richar:ison, 3 ; F Gayter, 2 : J Jolley, 2 ; F,rm-r, S Floughton, R Reece, J Tomlinson, W G Isil,,writ.!er, one each. Grabs from tries weris placed by H Farmer, 16 : S 1 Houghton, 6 ; J Jdlry, 4. Dr 'Toped goals : J Joller, 12 ; J Batterworth, 4 ; T Warder, 2; J Faulkner, S Houghton, and H Farmer, one each.</p>
        <p>• PonaltFgas wro Owed by 11 Farmer, 3 ; J Jolley, 3 ; S Houghto,l, 2 ; whilt H Farmer placed oxe goal from a mark The feature of the season has been the</p>
        <p>ma7nifincnt defence.</p>
        <p>Last year in the L'saztie Comphtition we finished in a vf‘ry cr ditab e posltion Twenty,ix games were played of which 15 were won, 9 !oat rind 2 drawn. Thirty-two</p>
        <p>, - ffoals, 43 tries were soz-e,l (193 points), against 19 goals, 25 tries (113 points). Thirty games in all were played. in whin 43 n'ool, 69 tries (293 points) were ,cored as!sinst our opponents' score of 24 goals. 27 tries (129 points, leiving a b3lanc' o 164 points in the club's favour.</p>
        <p>SEQOICD TEAM REPORT. During the toast s-a4on the second team has made gr?at impro,einent on previous years, and for a time looke,l lil winrunz the second team championship. A difference with seve*al players, however. weakened the tenn an.l of ter b,atioe all their opponents up to .Novemlyar 18, tbey went under to Sill ,rd " A " throuph not having liii teatn,playing only 13 men. From th s, very consis'ent fo,ao was maintained and the team fr.ishod up third on tho list with a score of 20 points ; sevcral points being lost by the narrowest marein. In compit4) matches the result is as follows : Played 13, woo 9, 'ost 4, and two points were awarded in lieu of playing S-olcoort and Salford return fixtures which were caneel'el through frost. The full return for all matcl.es was, Waved 20, wen 15. lest 5 ; vore for. 17 goak 46 trio or 172 p late, against 8 goals 18 tries or 70 points ; being a nalance in the club's favour of 102 t_oints. Th,, scoring was done by the following players : 'fries : J 1‘1!ck;on 8, J Richardson 8. D Jones 4, P Stnneley, T Wiffamson, C Butterworth, W Gayter, J weron, and E D übytiliire 3 each, Albert Able 2, J Dyer, Peters, nd E fornlinson 1 each. Goals from tries were kicked by J Nickson 4, J Richardson, P Stoney and J Dyer 2 each ; Albert Able and F Peters 1 each. Drepped goals by .1 Nickson and 3 Richardson 1 each</p>
