        <p>I Ile C. Pswaell (treasurer) suetnitted tha balance- r sheet from cviC twa. seen tee reseipts, including a balence of £ll 123 7 1 frern th orscedine aeasou, total up to the handsome fi.;ares ef £1,916 6, 91. The prin- cipel seurces of inc ene •vere—ssioscriptiens from 279 honorary mem or;, £B3 17s 4 1; 7e stand members, £l9 16s; gate recsipei tr 'el 21 L etc isoire senior competition matcees.£9lB 191 6; sta-Li receipts,223 lOs 31; receipts from elsecuera Ualoe cup ties (seven mesiones), £793 7s 7.1 ; from works' cornpetitiou, £4O 101 9i. Toe prin- cipal items on the expenditure side were—Parr's Bank, Limited, oveldreft Isst season, £53 43 91 ; insut- mice of players, L.S.O matches, £59 7s 61 ; sub- scriptions to N.U., C.F.U., L. SC, and £9 14s; field expeees. £53 8s 9.1 ; donation to the War Fund. £5 ; tree medals, Lancashire senior com- petition, £l5 ; erent o H Myers, £5 ; resits, rites, and V ixes. £e3 Is 101; prietine, stationer; and advertising £36 13, 41; gsternan sod reeve, £33 113 21 ; referees and toucu jud2es, £26 13s ; travelline expenses, Linea- sure Senior C impetiteon and friendly mitchse, £lOO 18; 111; 01/Iham F)otbdl Ciub in lieu of sfc)nd team fixtures. £l5 ; lit time, L,,ncsshire Senior Competition and friendly instcees, £352 13, 81; refreshments, £35 16s 2,1 ; pestiges and teegrame, £l5 7.4 21 ; doctor's fees embrocstion, &amp;e, £27 7s : fentieels, jerseys, pants,beots and washing jerseys, £76 19; 2d ; lalaries—secretary treasurer, asEiscant-so.o,qars, and store-keeper, £2l 19s. The expsnses on the Northern Union cup ties were-2506 31 81, the chief items being, lost time, £2522; 7d ; travelling expenses, £6O 3; 41 ; visitors' share of gate receipt., £135 3, 10,1 ; estemen and checkers, £2O 151 2 retresrow;,ts, £l6 43 31 ; referees and touch judges, £8 133 1J ; printing and posting, £lO 10.; The expenses of the West Lancashire cup tee; were £34 2s 3d, and of the inerw nks cempetition £4l 3; 61. There are no liabeitice, aid the club's assets are made up of cash in treasurer's henis, £2 104 51; loan, £4 19s 91 lean, Cheshire C )unty, £l3 11, 3 1 ; cese in Parr'; Bank, £314 11s 31 ; stand, £62 19, 31 ; (less 20 per cent. de- oreciation, £l2 11; 10 e, £507; 51; footbeards, tents, turnstiles, &amp;e., £45 15. 1 1 ; added this year. £2O, £65 15; id (less 20 per con,.: lepreeiatien,£l3 3,).£52 12s id; pintejerseys,begeboots, stockings, bath firtiegsesc, £27 18; 6d; t0te1,£466 10. 8.1. Cositinuleg Mr. Pownall added the first item of note was the satisfactory increase in the number of subscribers. the number this year was 270, an increase of 85 ; stand members, sit increase of 31 ; season tickets ireneaee 16 The total number of sub- scrilwrs this year was 305 last year 205, increase 100. (Applause.) The csinmit ee welcomed this increase as it showed that more people were taking a deeper interest in the game, and ware vEiPing to take the responsibility of mempershire Several old members who left when the club jeineu the Northern Uneen had returned to the fold ,and the committee were pleased to have them back. (Applause.) The seine Eeoeipts showed an increase of £l3 3; 61. He was sore if they had a better stand it woule be filled on speeial occasions. It formed an Ex- cellent investment. The old stand cast something les £l2O 133 and it had pill for itself lone since. Th-y were now reaping a eeatter of £45 per per for the use of it. He biped this question would be takes in hand by the new coeunictee. the gate meney showed an increase:of £1,164 15s.Tee large increase in the Lance shire Competition gat' e no doubt could be put down- to the very interesting s' arse of the Competition at the end of the season when Runcorn met Oldham in the final before the largest crowd ever seen on the Runcorn ground—when their pleyera won the match—captured the cup and 15 gold medals ; and he (as treasurer) sscured as booty £362 in hard cash. (Applause) Inde- pandeot of this, however, all the gates in the first team matches with tho excep'jon of three had shown sub- stantial increase. There was a loss of 123 911 on the Works' Competition, Wile.'ol.B last season they had made a profit of £29 16, 111. Tnis was accounted for by the great interest taizen in the Nnehern Union Cup Ties,and the number of draws Trey beaan the last season by owing the bank £54 4s 9 and if they would take that little item off th t ,ind add it to the creJit balance they made £214 11; 31 on the season's work. This stetson they had peed £7 15m 61 more in insurance than previously. This was an item they could not avoid, but they believed in trsaArig the players properly when suffering from injuries. The amount spent on the field showed an increase et- £3O m )re than the previous sea- son. Under this head sne item of £2O in new footboards was incurred for tne Oldham match, and if tlac Com- mitte could have foreseen hiving such a fine dsy they might have been inclined to spend five times £29‘ and it wocld have ben rep4l iii ot e day. Referring to the salaries of the officials, be said they were almost like the laws of the Modes and Persians, which could not be altered. They were perfectly satisfied with what they were receiving, but considering the very successful season and the increasel amount of work entailed, he thought they were entitled to a little extra consideration. (Leud applause.) The Chairman observel all present could hear from the reports jest submitted that the past season had indeed been the most sacessful that the club, had ever undertaken. They had not only won the cup for the second time, hut the prerent conernitttee were going out of nffise feeling more satisfied with themselves than ever any previous ccnarnitteo had done. (Applause ) Mr. F. Hancock proposed and Mr. J. F. Moulton seconded the adoptior of the Secretary's and Treasurer's report.</p>
        <p>ALTERXTIONS OF RETLIS</p>
        <p>Mr. Moulton moved ma'', a new rule be inserted ink the club rule book : " That the offices of treasurer and secretary and assistant secretary shall not coma up for annual election, and that those officials should hold office in the same way aq officers of public boAies." La doing so he ennrended that the result of the rule would ba that their officials would be free from. the attacks of any particular section who might come forward to oust an official they did:not lika,snd could consequently serve the intere,ti of the club better.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Brawn secon,l,al the addition of the, new rule. Mr. W. B. Brimelow advocated that it would be better to go on in the old way aid rs serve to themselves the right of electing their officers annually. It was, he felt sure, better for the offic ills to feel a revival of confidence by their annual ; and when they could no loner command that confidence it would be thaseenough to dispose of them. Upon a vote being taken only four voted for Mr Moulton's resc.lution which was consequrntli rejected. Mr. Li,snry Wright propoied that the words " The captains and vice-c ipt -sins of the first andsecond teams' be struck oat of Rule 2 aid that the words " NJ profes- sional shall he aaowed to serve on the committee" be added to the rule.</p>
        <p>This was unartimiutllv 3Rreed te). THE ELECTION OP OFFICEIZS.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., Ryd,l Mount, Runcorn, was unanimously re-elected p:esijent of the club on th pro.. pysition of Mr. W. B. Brimelow, seconded by Mr. H. Grounds.</p>
        <p>Tne other appointmelts were :—S-scretary, Mr J Elli- son ; treasurer, Mr C Po Nnall ; assistant secretary, Mr A Abel ; auditors, Me,srs C Cooper and E Lunt ; captain (first team), Mr S amghton ; deputy-captain, Mr J Butterworth ; captain (second team), Mr J Nieks3n ; deputy-eaotqin, Mr J Richardson: committee, Messrs W p. De tkin. H Cawley, G Robinson, S Abrams, W Faulkner, H Wrif:ht, A Lightburn, A Abel,T Robinson,R Garnett, J F Mculten.</p>
