      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>THEFT OF TOOLS FROM DALLAM. At the Warriegton Police Court on S tturday More Messrs. T. Birchen (in the chair), R T Fairclotieh and J W Hay war d, James McAreaney, was charted with steal- ing from a moulding shep in Dallem Forge betwree six and seven n'clock on To sdey the 15- h hist a number or moulders' tools, value 4s 81, the property of three men named Ball. Hackney and Gibson.—He pleaded guilty— Mr. Taylor (Messrs. Greenall and Burkton) prosecuted, and sad that prisoner was until the elart of the 14th Inst. a moulder, in the employ of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co , Limited, and he was di•=- charged. Among e number of men employed in the same shop were the nen Ball, Hackney, and Gibson. They used a number of tools on Monday 14,,h inst. and on Wednesday the 16th the tools were missed. Three out of the four tools stolnn were disposed of by the prisoner at the shop of Mrs. Leslie, ?inners Brow, and the fourth was found in his possession when arrested. The value of the gooda was small, hut unfnrtunately for some time past these petty thefts had been c,n- stantly occurring. A month ago a case was hearl at that Court of stealing a quantity et scrap from tho premises of the arm. He was instructed to ask the Bench to mark their appreciation of the seriousness of the thefts by imr3oBing such a penalty as they migist think necessary to impress upon the men that they must not commit that land of larceny.—Prisrner was committed to gaol for a month with hard lahour.</p>
        <p>- THEFT BY A LODGER. On Saturday at the IA arrington Police Curt, Thomas Price, 23. Forster-street was charged with staling from the dwellinghouse of Samuel Lear, 6, Milner-stet-et en Friday the 20th April a gentlemao'i elver lever watch, white metal alhert and medal value £2 101., the property of William Ikin. He pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable briefly stated the facts of the case from which it appeared that prisoner and Ikin lodged at the house of Lear in Milner-street. On the 17th of April prosecuter took his watch and chain and pendant off and put them in a box but did not lock the box. On tit. 20th April he found the articles were missing. 'Uhe prisoner also disappeared from the town and did no-, return until the previous day (Friday), when he was ap- prehended by Superintendent Monaghan and Detective Foster. He was charged with stealing the articles and admitted that be had dare so. They had not recovered the property as yet but prisoner gave the name of a man in Dolgelly to whom he said he had sold it and he (Nto Talbot) thought they would be able to recover th pro- perty. Prieoner bad not been before the Court previously. Ho had been discharged from the Army.—He was com- mitted to gaol for two months with hard labour. SEQUEL TO THE MAFEEING REJOICINGS.—CASE OF FURIOUS DRIVING. At the Warrington POile, Court on Monday before Mr. F. Monks and other magistrates. Henry Whitlow, a groom, of Appleton.was summoned for furiously driviru: a horse and cart in Bridge-street on the 19th of May ta the danger of paesemgers.--He pleaded guilty.—Mr. Pigot, mueic dealer, Bridge-street stated that at 8 45 on Saturday the 19th of May he was standing at his shop door and saw a man (he could not say whether it was the defendant) driving a horse and trap down Bridge- street at a terrific speed. There were two ladies and a child in the vehicle. The streets were crowded at the time and there were a number of children beating tin kettles etc and that might have made the horse bolt. The driver did not appear to be 'ming it on.—The Chief Constable said he thought there was every reason to be- lieve that it was a ease of bolting. If any man could drive a horse it was Mr. Whitlow but when the cane was reported to him (Mr. Talbot) it was not for him to constitute himtelf a judge, but to bring it before the maeistrates. He thought the horse was frightened by the banging of tin cans and waving of flags and it bolted.—The defendant said the horse was a young Me and boltsd.—P.C. Corbett was called, and raid the street was crowded at th 3 time, especially with small children who were having procesaions; Witness saw the defendant driving the horse and trap. The horse was galloping. He shouted to defendant to be more careful, as he expected to see him knock someone dawn.' He could not say whether the horse bolted or not, but the I defendant did not seem ta b° endeavouring to hold it in. —P.C. Turner said he saw Whitlow driving the horse at Bridge Feet. Knewitie him to be a good driver be was surprised to see him going at such a pace. He followed him over the bridge, and at Wederspeol crossings the horse stopped, as the gates were closed. Defendant told him that the horse had bolted, and that it was impose be to pull up.—The Chief Constable said that if the defend- ant paid the costs he would withdraw the SUMIII3OB. He etas afraid that the Mafeking rejoicings were respon- sible for a lot of things.—The Chairman : The cane is withdrawn if you pay the witnesses their costs (5)., hut don't come into town with a young horse when Pretoria is taken. (Laughter). WARRINGTON MAN'S STRANGE ADVENTURES. Richard Geering a native of Wartiugton, appeared at the Salford Police Court on Monday (-barged with loiter- ing at the rear of Roslyn Villa, Garald-road. Broughton with intent to commit a felony. Early on Sunday morning the i:rieoner. who is a sickly looking man, was found by the police at the rear of the villa. A jemmy was also found in the garden. and the prisoner admitted that it belonged to him,having got it to commit a bur- glary- He futther confessed to the police that ha had served a year's imprisonment for stealing at Warrington and had been three years in a reformatory. Inquiries made by the police, however. showed that Goering 'w- hinged to a most respectable family, and that he bore an excellent character. He had never been in trouble at all. Of late h- has shown signs of insanity, and the stipendiary, therefore, ordered him to be handed back to his friends.</p>
        <p>A NIGHT DRIvIt T 3 WARRINGT3N.—S3IART CAPUIRE. At the Manchester Police Court, on Tuesday, three young men, named George Quinn, Joseph Kenny, and George Jaakson were charged with stealing a pony and trap belonging to Simnn Super Hop:, a greengrocer. Clar- ence.street, Cheetham Hill-road, and valued at £27. Toe prisoner Quinn was employed by the prosecutor for the purpose of !irking after the pony, which was kept in a stable on a piece cf waste land at the back of Strange- ways Prison. Late on Sunday night the pony and trap were taken away. and the following morning they were entered by the prisonfra Kenny and Jackson for sale by auction at the Norton Arms Hotel, Warrington. The sum of £l5 wail offered for the animal and trap and was accepted, but befcv the bargain was actually completed the Warrington poiice, who had received information of the affair, interfered and took the three men into cus- tody, Quinn having Joined the other two during the day. —The Stipendiary (Mr Headlam) said there was no evidence, technically, that the prisoner Quinn had really been concerned in the theft, and he would be discharged. The others would, however, have to go to the sessions or trial.</p>
        <p>HOW TO STOP A COUGH, How to Cure Weak Lungs, Bronchitis and Asthma.</p>
        <p>Take VENG'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. The most success- ful remedy in England. It is used in many hospitals and token by doctors themselves because they recognise in it the most successful remedy that science has ever been able to compound. Vastly superior to ordinary cough mixtures or any of the Emulsions. Ask for VENG'S LIGHTIqING Cocoa CURE and see yon get it. Price Is Id. and 2s. 9.1. field by Boots. Ltd. ; Knowles, Warrington; Peake Harleatown and Newton; Weston and Co., Runcorn; !mitt, Widnes; and all Chemists.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL TREXTMENT OF A WIFE—EXEMpLARY SENTENCE. At the Warrington P ilice Court on Wednesday before Mr. T. Rirchall (presiding) and Councillor Smethurst, Isaac Smith, Glasshou=e-row, Bank Quay was charged witn assaulting his wife, Esther Smith, on the 26:h of My.—de pleaded guilty.—Complainant stated that on the date in question she was in the house when prisoner ram--! home and"gave it her right an] left."He thumped her, banged her head and then kic4cd her till she could I not stand.—Complainant' sister spoke to examining complainant's legs. They were black. Complainant had been living at her house since Sunday.—Superin- tendent Monaghan sail he bad known the prisoner for a great many years. The complainant had been illused by him many times and had had to leave her borne; Witness apprehended the prisoner in a public house yesterday. The previous night he had escaped from the police. fie was a poacher and a thief, and did very little work.—The Chief Constable said that was the prisoner's 18th appearance. He had been before the Court for almost eVery offence short of manslaughter.— The Chairman sail the prisoner was like a wild beast, and was not fit to be at large. Tbe Bench had had to deal with so many cases of women being abused and treated worse than footballs, he would not say by men, but by creatures in the garb of men, that they were going to make an example in that case. Prisoner would be sent to gaol for six months with hard labour.—Com- plainant then applied for a separation order. She said she was afraid to live with her husband.--Phe order was granted, prisoner to pay 8s our week towards the main- enance of his wife and children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MINOR CASES. Warrington Borough Court. Fizins.Y.—Before the Mayor (in _the. chair), Councillor</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. Warrington Borough Court. Fizins.Y.—Before the Mayor (in _the. chair), Councillor</p>
        <p>- - Smethurst and Mr. Hepherd. . FIGHTING. —Nter Scully, Percival's-fold and Patrick Burke. 63, Oliver-street. were charged with committing a breach of •he peace by fighting on Pinners Brow and in Horsemarket-Street on the 12th and 13th May.—P.C. Barnett gave evidence —The Chief Constable stated that Burke had been before the court 35 times —Defendants were bound over to keep the peace and ordere i to pay the costs or in default a month's imprisonment. FoWL-KEEPING IN' I BACK YARD. —Patrick Moffatt was summoned for keeping fowls at. 58 and 60. Napier-street so as to be a nuisance and injurious to health —lnspector Norman said that in consequeece of a complaint he received about the filthy condition of defendant's premises he visited them. He found there was an ordinary yard about 239 fee square. Of this space 103 feet wereloccupied by a shed where there were 42 head of fowl. The yard was in a filthy con- dition. Defendant was served with notice to abate the nuisance but he failed to comply with it. —The magistrates , made an order for defendant to abate the nuisance within I seven days and pay the costs which amounted to Bs. SAVAGE ASSAULT.—Ann Floyd, 234, Winwick-road, was summoned for assaulting Mary Pownall, 23, Jockey-street, on the 18th May. —Complainant stated that the defendant struck her in the face with a key, greatly discolouring it, and also bruised the back of her head. —A fine of 20s was imposed. A BOY ASSAULTED.—WiIIiam Morrison 277. Knutsford- road. was summoned for assaulting , illiam Henry Tongue, a boy, residing at 279. Knutsford-road, on the 21st inst.—Mr. Browne prosecuted, and said the complainant was coming horse from school when he met the def-rndant, who without any 'provocation struck him on:the side of the face.—Defend- ant was bound over to keap ths peace and ordered to pay the costs. SATURDLY.—Befire Messrs. Birchall (in the chair), R. T. Fairclough and Hayward. ScritooL ATTENDA.Neg Casks —The folowin2 parents were fined for neglecting to send their children regularly to school :—Thomas Griffin 2, Old Gar lens. 29 6d; Jolan Dicken- son. 68, Scott-street 5s ; Sarah Williamson 8, Ail,-lane. 2s ; Thomas Turner, 62, Scott-street, 2s 3d; Henry Newton, Lythgoes-lane, 53 ; liaph Alcock, 10, Appleton-street. 2s 6d ; Thomas Farrington, 27, Glasshouse-row. 2s 61 ; Pater Clemo 3. Wakefield-stveet, 2s 6d ; Thomas Nunnerlay 120. Pierpoint- -1 street. 2s 6ii ; John Dunn, lb, Howard-street, 2s 6d ; Isaac Ryder, 8, Baxter-street 23 6d. The son of Joseph Bibby, 132. nehool-brow was sent to Hightown Truant School.—Mr. William Jones. school Attendance officer. prosecuted. i MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair). !Councillor</p>
        <p>Smethurst, and Messrs. Birchall and S Wright. DRUNICLRDS RICUOES. —lsaac Rickley, 7. Roger-street was charged with being drunk and disorderly in the Market Placr, at 6.40 on Saturday night.-- Asked if he hat anything to say, prisoner said he met a lot of friends and th.y drank Baden-Powell's health.—The Chief Constable skid the man was a perfect wastrel and would make anything an excuse. Pined 5s and costs.—Thomks McLoughlin was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-str,et at 8.45 the previous night. —Re pleaded guilty but said ha had baen drinking slaw last Thursday (the Quaen's birthday).—Fined 5s and costs.</p>
        <p>TUE3DAY—Beforo Alderman Sutton (in the chair) and Mr. Birehall TTIFFT FRoM A CART.—Mathias Wood, was charge I with stealing from a eart standing in Winwick-roal at noon the previoas dav a bettle of hoo-bitters vane 21. the property of Owen and Company.—The Chief Constable said that the cart, laden with boxes of hop-bitters was standing in Winwiek-road outside the Red Lion public houae. P.C. Cooper (20) was on cl Ley in plain clothes in the neighbourhood and saw the prisoner.vvha was under the influence of drink. put his hand in one of the boxes. abstract a bottle, put it in his pocket and walk away evidently in the hope that it was bitter beer. It however, turned out to be a milder beverage, hop-bitters. The offioer arrested the ram and brought him to the Police Stat'on. Prisoner had been before the magis- trates t-n times. Thefts from carts in the neighbourhood of Winwiek-road were becoming a prevalent thing—Prisoner was committed to gaol for 14 days with hard labour. VIoLENT ASESLULT.—John James was charged on a war- rant with assaulting Agnes Varley on the 10th of March.— Complainant stated that on the date in question prisoner came into her house and conrriencd to use filthy language. She requested him to go out an.l he refused and struck her three times in the face. He also knocked her down on the 'stairs. He was ejected from the house but he kicked the door open and threatened to murder her.—Prisouer was fined 10s a d costs. NEGLRCr OF FAMILY.—Walter Huxley was charged with neglfcting to maintain his wife aid family whereby they became chargeable to the common fund of the Union on the 18th December, 1899.—He pleade I guilty. —Mr 13 dhearer briefly stated the facts of th s ease and prisoner was corn mitted to gaol for a month with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TUESDLY.—B4fore Megers, Lou! On the chair), Neiid, Talbot and Colonel Ridgway.</p>
        <p>SWINE FEVER CASE9.—GREATLY ON THE INCREAaM IN THE: Contery.—Riuhard Percival, farmer, was chargel with neg- lecting to report three cases of swine fever oecurriag on his premises at Antroous on the 4th May. Ser.esant Oarter proved the case, and stated that he found three animals suffering from the disease. Defendant's son told witness that two pigs also suffering from the (Hawse had been buried. When asked why he had not reported the case, the defend- ant repled that he did not believe they were cases of swine fever. The matter had been reported to the B tar 1 of Avicul- ture. Defendant now expresse 1 his ignorance that the animals were affected. It had been hiS intention to report the matter to the police. The animals had " eaten their meat as usual, and did not break wit with blotches, which he thought was usual in cases of swine fever,"—Superintend- ent Pearson said it was nommen know! dge teat out- b-eaks of swine fever had to be reported to the police. 'Nap were now eight cases reported in the district. People wofilo not report these oases, and no wonder that the disease spread. Swine fever was very much on the increase throughout the county. The Chairman said the Bench felt that the defendant was vary blameable in the matter. Having lost two pigs he ought to have been put on his guard at once. The Act in question was passed in 1894, sad it required all farmers to report outbreaks of the disease.—A fine of 20s and costs was infileted.—Ann Wright. Higher Whitley, was summoned for a similar off .nce on the 14th May. She pleaded not guilty. Defen-lant's son said he and his mother had no idea the animals w-'re affected. They thought their condition was owing to overfeeding The morning the animals were slaughtered they had had their breakfast as usual.— Fined 20s. including costs. —Themes Miller,butcher,School-lane,Higher Whitlev,was also similarly charged and pleaded not guilty —Sergeant Carter otated the facts.—Superintendent Pearson observed nat it WAS snot _men as these who were spreading the disease throughout the county.—Defendant's plea was that the animals haa their food as usual. —Defendant was fined 20s and ce.ts 'PRE RELIEF OF MAFE.KING.—A FALSE REPORT. —Robert W Hunter and Charles Jones, young men, were chagel with having on the 7th May, in 01 London-road, Stockton 'death. made use of profane and obscene language P.C. Tarte proved the Cases. One of the defendants stated that they were excited because it was reported that Aafekin.; had been relieved,but it subsequently proved to oss a false report. They were " only having a bit of fun." Bach was fiuel ss. an i costs.—Wright Barrow.a, labourer, charge i with a similar offence at Dutton. on the Northwich-road on the 21st April, as proved by P.C. Ridgway, was filed 53 and costs Scnoor. ClASES.—Joserda Howerth, of Stoeatoa Heath WAS charged with failing to send his child re t schcol. The facts of the case were state I by `lr. R Wilkinson, seholl attendance officer. Tbe case was a ijeurned 'or a mottle. and It, WRS intimated to the defendant that if the child went regularly to school in the meantime no further steps would be taken in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Newton Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Newton Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TEURSDLY.—H.fore Mr. T. J. Gillespie (ha the Col.</p>
        <p>Wood find lir- W. Coi,ingwqoi</p>
        <p>_ SCHOOL ATTEn7•LiwEic C S 3 —On the evuienen of Mr. T. MartinOale. school attendance officer, James Gregson, Hobert Blackgard. Jon n Broster. Henry Pilling, Thoinas 'Morris, George Gerrard and William Speake w-re eaah fined be for neglesting to send their children to scho'd regtalarly. OBSTRUCTING THE FOoTPA.TH.—WiI jam Jackson, Will:am Gee, Alfred Monks, Albert Keeps and Henry tie -ps were summoned for obstructing the foollytth at tray,tocic by stand- ing thereon and preventing passed. 'tars from na,siii4os Vie 13th of May.—gr 13 Smith of Wigan.appeared on behalf of th. d•fendants who pleaded not guilty.-4 constable stated that, at 12 50 on the date in question he saw the lefendatits in company with several others obstrueting the fo tpttli oßoosi uff-Bcensed house at the end of Keeper's-lane. He watched them twenty minutes and saw then drinkin r beer omit of bottles. Several passengers had to leave the footpath. He took defendants names and aldre.oes and told them that they would be reported. He WAS on duty there spec ally on account of cases of that description.—By Mr Smith : Keener's-lane was about six or seven yards wide It was nit 25 minutes to one when he SAW the defenda,tts. The house from whick they obtained the beer openel at 12.30. Witness was standing in a far.n yard about a dozen yards from where the men were standing. The defendants did not remain on one spot but went on %oth Penny-lane and Keepers lane footpaths They were on Penny-lane footpath when he took their names and addresses.—Mr. Smith said his case was that the men were in Keepers-lane between the gable end of the farm house and a cottage. It might be at the time the constable took their names they were in Penny-lane. the effieer came from the farm and naturally defendants would move into the other lane. The 7 were,according to the constable's statement passing from one place to the °tiler and therefore passengers found no difficulty in passing through them Where, therefore was the obstruction ? If that was an obstruction there were infinitely worse obstruclons in the main streets of the town. The public had a right to stop in the streets for a reasonable time to talk with their friends. Ile</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET</p>
        <p>The Warrington team at times shown the need of a first--ilass howler. This was the 0*46 last Saturday when Cbeetham had little difficulty in scoring 120 for the loss of only five wickets. Toe batsmen lid not snem to have any trouble in dealing with the balls sent down, the only Warrington bowl.r to obtain a couple of wickets being E Gibson. The Warrington score of 112 was easily passed and the leca.l team have vet to win their first match this season. The services of E Connor were again missed but a capable Oarr was found in James Briggs, brother of the Lancas,hire County player.</p>
        <p>Warring,ton went to the wicket first, B Silcock and Thewlis batting to the deliveri,s of Parker and Mien. Both bowlers opened with maidens, but eventually the professionsl got Parker away to boundary twice in one over. At 16 Allen sent back Si!cock, making the way (dear for J. Callon. T. Sinclair relieved Parker,but both batmen treated the new bowler with scant respect, whilst Allen maintained an excellent length With the score at 37 Naylor relieved Sinclair, but Allen was the distinctive agent, getting Thewlis caught at extra cover for a dashing 26. James Briggs joined Callon and ten runs were added when the latter returned a simple one to Naylor, who had relieved Sinclair.</p>
        <p>Dr: Smith soon got to work, and caused the 50 to be hoisted with a splendid la'e cut, but at 56 a couple of disasters befel the locals, Briggs playing late as Naylor was bowled: whilst Clirke played the third ball he re- ceived. At 61 .1 W Bibby was 'eat back, and six wickets ere down for the poor acorn of 61. W Fl Pocimore played steadily, and some vivoreus hitting by Dr. Smith caused the score to steadily rise and a double change in the attack was tried, Parker relieving Allen, who crossed over. Runs continu,d to come freely from Dr. Smith's bat, and the " century" was reached amidst cheers, but with the addition of a single Podmore V74q dismissed with a grand ball from Allen, whilst at 108 Pirker met with his first success when he got Dr. Smith caught at mid-on for a valuable coatribation of 29. The innings closed for 112.</p>
        <p>The hroti-iers Sinclair were the first to bat for the vial- tars, Thewlie and Dr. Smith sharing the bowlinze and from the first one rent down the visitors appeared to -make light of the task set them. The twenty was hoisted speedily, hut sev:‘n runs later a fine ball from the amateur sent back T. H. Sinclair, whilst three runs later Thewlis broke throuriti Parker's defence. A confi- dent appeal for a c itn:i at the nilket was answered in W R Sinelair's favour, whilst a Fimilar verdict awaited an appeal for Sharp infringin7 rule 24 Runs Came ctAadilv, and at 42 Gibson relieved Dr Smith, whilst at 62 J W Bibby went on in place of Thewlis. The separation was effecteo by Gibson, who got Sinclair lbw. and six runs later boa'el Clllinge with a fine ball. P Mier ear joined Sharp. who mononnlized the whole of the scoring, this wicket yielding 17 runs, when Mercer was caught at the wicket. Vigorous hitting by Sharp and Tinker placsd the issue in safe keeping for the visitors, and the War- rington total was passed withnat further loss.</p>
        <p>The second team gained an easy victory over Client- ham 2nd. Uatting first they ran up a total of 161 for five wickets when the innings wAs declared. On Cweet- hem going to the winket they renlied with 95 runs. For Warrington Hawtin rattled up 48 and Adamson scored 44.</p>
        <p>S‘nicev Wirl Mills elAily dia.ce 1 of Cheshire Lines Enein.prs. Battine first they knocked up the respectab'e total of 122 for the I,qs nf five wickets,when the innings w-,As cieclar'cl. The Engineers reTnnded with 60 runs, the Wire Mills thus esti ninr.r a victory by 62 runs. For the winners Lavell, J Evans, Chen and It 03ok batted in fine style.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe defeated Crosfi ld's team hy the smnll mai-mita of hiV rting, the senrea being 70 and 68 reeperltively. G Sharp and S Wood 4 did good work for the winners, knocking un 21 and 22 respectively. T Whitfield made the best eff3rt for Crosfield's placing 18 runs to his crelit.</p>
        <p>Choshire Linos Enginoors 2nl sufferod defeat at the hands of Sankey Wire Mills 2nd. The Wire Mills seorei 61, whilst the Ens,,ineers total was 46.</p>
        <p>An ex3itinz match wag witoPmei betwren Arnley and Moore, ArplPy runninl out winniTs by the majority of to runs. Raiford batted in god form for Arplay, knockinz up 13 Banner contributed 15 runs for More.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists met Ryland' Bro. on the ground of the later and FRICIgnd,A in dpfeeing thorn by 19 rung. For the victors Woodiag scored 11,Barnes rattling up 14 for Rylands's.</p>
        <p>CYCLING. Th mAmhers nf thA Ovhid Ovelin?. Club. Thelira'</p>
        <p>met at Cuerden Hall f-Ar the second run this season. Miss Naylor presentei each of the cyclists with a beautiful fhwer and also ac3ompanierl them (driving). Mr. R A Naylor. the nresident, and the captain, Mr. G Lambert, were as usual in charge. Their destination was Whirley Grove, Macc'esfiAll, a most delightful place, beyond Alderler Eigs Tao 'sad taken lay throulh Knutsford and Chelford. The cyc lite were entertained to tea by Miss Navlor, who vet, kindly presided. After taa the r,ture j )urney was cmrnncel. and taken through Alderley Edge, Ashley, Rostherne. Hon Green. Home was safely reach3d a.frar a most enjoyable run.</p>
