      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Gospel Hall.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the preacher at the Gospel Hall was Mr. J Cowan of Gateacre who spoke from the story in John 6, 1, 58, Christ fedinv„ 5,000 besides women and children with five loaves and a few fishes. After r•uch a wonderful act it is no wonder they wanted to make him King. He was just the man f &gt;r ic as He could supply their every need. They wanted Him for what they coula get out of Him. Selfishness was their motive and the Lord would not gratify them but pliyted them to their spiritual need ; their souls were dying with hunger and He wss the Bread from Heaven to give and sustain their life. His body He gave for the life of the world, and if any eat of Him he shall live for ever. What did Christ mean? Adam ia Eden ate of the forbidden fruit and to prevent him eating of the tree of life and living in sin for ever God barred his way by a flaraing sword. Christ Came and by His life and death He atoned for sin and opened up the way to life again and to eat Him is to simply appropriate the benefits of His life and death to oneself by accepting Him, becoming His follower.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday School Anniversary,</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Sunday School Anniversary,</p>
        <p>Sunday school anniversary services were held in Bold street Wesleyan chapel on Sunday and passed off most successfully. There were good congregatiens, the preacher being the Rev. B. Weaver, of Stockport. In the morning the rev. gentleman based his remsrks on the words " Take heed therefore hoe ye hear" and im- pressed upon his hearers the necessity of listening attentively to the sermon ani thus help on the work ot the Church of God. They must not blame the minister if they were not benefited by the services they attonded. Ministers were blamed enough, but the fault for the most part lay with the people. They must he more enthusiastic over the church's progress. The " 're Deum " was readered to Oakeley's set'ing, and Mr. J. Parkinson effectively contributed a solo. The cinging of the scholars was very creditable. In the afternoon a mesical service was held, Mr. J. N. Hartley presiding. Mr. Hartley, in opening the proceedings, gave a few words of advice to the scholars and friends. Miss Brough feelingly rendered the solo "Entreat me not to leave thee " (Geunod). Mr. Hassel sang " Deeper and deeper still" awl " Waft her, 1 Angels" (Handel.) A trombone solo " Arm, arm, ye brave" by Mr. E. Smith was an interesting feature. Miss Christian had been announced to sing " Lead, kindly Light," but was unable t ) appear, and Mr. F. Hickman (the organist) gave an organ Polo instead. Mr W. Silcock followed with an instructive address, the sub- ject of which was" While the children are about us" He spoke first to the ptrents and friends, and then to the children, pointing out how they might devote their lives to Christ. The church was well filled in the even- ing, when Mr. Weaver nreached from 11. Samuel xviii., 32,—" And the king said unto Cushi Is the 3 oune man Absaloin safe ?" The choir rendered the anthem "Leave us not " (Stainer) with good effeet. Mrs. R. H. Pear- son was heard to advantage in "0 Divine Redeemer" (Gounod), with violin obligate by Mr Pearson. The collections, which were in aid of the Sued ty school funds, amounted to £4B Os 9d—about in excess ot the pre- vious year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
