      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Fatal Fall Downstairs.</p>
        <p>An inquest was conducted at the Police Station on Monday by Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, rela- tive to the death of Mary Roberts (69),48,Winwiek- street, who died at the Infirmary the previous Thursday morning from an injury received by fall- ing downstairs on the evening of May 13th.—John Chantrey, greengrocer, said the deceased was his mother-in-law, and resided with him at 48, Win- wick-street. About half-past nine on Sunday night (May 13th) he left the house for a walk,leaving the deceased upstairs with his wife, who was ill. He returned shortly before ten, and found deceased lying at the bottom of the stairs. He questioned her, and she said: "I was coming down, and when I got near the bottom I fell." He bad her removed at once to the Infirmaiy, where it was found she was suffering from a fractured thigh and shock. lie saw her dead in the Infirmary on the 25th.— Catherine Bowyer, a nurse,also gave evidence,anct a I verdict of "Accidental death," was returned. Presentation to Mr. Thomas Wallington. Air. Thomas Wallington, who for a period of six years has teen the secretary of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society, having resigned from that position, the members determined to mark their appreciation of his services, by a presentation. The gift took the form of a handsome easy chair, and at the meeting of the society on sunday afternoon,the presentation was made.—The President (Rev. J.Yonge) in asking Mr, Wallington to accept the gift said they had that afternoon to fulfil a very pleasant duty,ftlthough like a great many other duties it had its side which was not altogether so happy. They were giving a token of regard to one who found it no longer pos- sible to render them service as be had done tor some years past. Mr Wallington was the first secre- tary of Wycliffe P.S.A. Society and a great deal of hard work had fallen upon his shoulders. The burden of the initiation of the society was borne very largely by Mr Thomas Wallington. He proved himself to be almost an ideal secret ary ;very painetak- inanand devoted and had put his whole heart into the work. The committee felt that they could not allow him to cease office without showing in a practical form that they were thankful to him for his services, and the members now asked him to accept the gift as a token of brotherly regard from the P.S,A. It was not to be supposed that because they were presenting him with an easy chair that they were going to let him sit down in it for ever and ever. (Laughter., He was not to take it as a general sign that his work was finished. It was presented with the affectionate regard and esteem of the members with their beat wishes for his domestic and family happiness.---Mr. Whetnall added hie testimony to the valuable work done by Mr. allington.—Mr. Hawthorn said the present was only a small token for the services Air. Walling- ton had rendered as secretary and he, hoped the society would have the benefit of Mr. Wellineton's experience and help in years to come—Mr. Term said as the secretary of the society upon whom had devolved the duties which had been so faithfully dia- charged by Mr. Wallington in the paanbe had plea- sure in adding his testimony to the useful services of Mr.Wallington. If at any future time Mr.W ailing- ton was in a position to take up the work agairnhe (Mr. Farrar) would be glad to make way for him. —Mr. Hickman stated that in a quiet, unosten- tatious way, Mr Wallington had done a great deal of work in connection with the initiation and growth of the society,—ln replying. Mr.Wallingten thanked the members for the gift and for the hearti- ness with which they had given it. The present would serve to bring back to him, the many happy moments he had spent in connection with the P.S.A. Society. No letter gave him greater pain to write than that containing his resignation, but his duties were now entirely out of town and he was , unable to continue the work conneeted with the secretaryship. He hoped tiee society would grow stronger and that its usefulness would be still greater. As far as ho had ability and tie he would I always be willing and ready to do what be could for the advancement of the Society.</p>
      </div>
