      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>TH E WAR.</head>
        <p>TH E WAR.</p>
        <p>LATEST TELEGRAMS.</p>
        <p>FALL OF PRETORIA.</p>
        <p>Flight of President Kruger.</p>
        <p>The London `• Daily Mail" has received the following despatch from its special correspondent, the Earl Roselyn :</p>
        <p>PRETORIA, WEDNESDAY, 11 30 a.m. Pretoria will ba occupied in abut two hours' time tc-day, the 30. h of May without resistance. The President has gone to Waterval Boven.</p>
        <p>BurgomaAer de SDUZI is authorized to receive the British. He, with an influential committee of citizens, including Ciaief Justice Gregorowski,Mr. Samuel Marks, and Mr. Loveday, haw been appointed to preserve life and property during the interregnum.</p>
        <p>Everything is quiet, bur, crowds are waiting expec- tantly in the Church Square for the arrival of the British.</p>
        <p>Fearing possible disturbances and bloodshed among the prisoners of war at Water val, Mr. Hay the American Consul, and Mr. Leigh Wood insisted upon 20 officirs being liberated on parole to go to the men. Their action cannot be too highly praised. 1 was permitted to accom- pany the officers.</p>
        <p>Everything was quiet. I have just ratuined.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Messages from Lord Roberts. Heavy Fighting Outside Johannes-</head>
        <p>Messages from Lord Roberts. Heavy Fighting Outside Johannes-</p>
        <p>burg</p>
        <p>The War Office on Thursday morning issued the fol- lowing telegram from Lord Roberts : Lord Roberts telegraph, Germiston, Wednesday, 9.5 p.m. The brunt of the fighting yesterday fell on lan Hamil ton's column. I had sent him, as already' mentioned,to work round to the wrst of Johannesburg in support of French's cavalry, which was directed to go to the north, near the road leading so Pretoria. I have not heard from Fretch yet, but Hamilton, in a report which has just reached me, states that about one o'clock in the after- noon he found his way blccked by the enemy, etrongly posted on some kopjes and ridges three miles south of the rand. They had two heavy guns, several field guns,and pom-poms. Hamilton at once attacked. The right was led by the Gordons, who, after capturing one extremity of the ridge, wheeled round and worked along it until after dark, clearing the enemy, who fought most obstinately. The City Imperial Volunteers led on to their flank, and would not be denied, but the chief share in the action, as in the casualties fell to the Gordons,wbose gallant advance excited the admiration of all.</p>
        <p>Hamilton is now at Florida, due west of Johannes- burg, and French a few miles further to the north-east. The Gordons, cavalry, mounted infantry, and the Seventh Division are holding the heights north of the town. The Eleventh Division, with H, Q, and heavy artillery are to the south of Johannesburg. Hamilton speaks in high terms of praise of the manner in which Bruce, Hamilton, and Spans, of the Shropshire Light Infantry, handled the men under Smith Dorrien's direction.</p>
        <p>Lord Roberts, telegraphing at 4.50 p.m., 30th May, says : —ln answer to the flag of truce I sent to Johannes- burg this morninz,the commandant came to see me. He begged me to defer entering the town for 24 hours, as there were many armed burghers still inside. I agreed to this, as lam most anxious to avoid the possibility of anything like a disturbance inside the town, and as bodies of the enemy are still holding hills in the immediate neighbourhood from which they will have to be cleared off beforehand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Johannesburg Handed_ Over.</head>
        <p>Johannesburg Handed_ Over.</p>
        <p>" Daily Mail" this afternoon received the following cable from Pretoria, dated May 30, 1.15 p.m.</p>
        <p>Johannesburg has bt en handed over to Brit'sh.</p>
        <p>The Late Li euten ant-Colonel O'Leary</p>
        <p>Lane-C.rooral O'Neill. of E Company, IQt South Lancashire Regiment (a Runcorn man), send home the following eulogium upon the late I.4eutJnan`-Colonel W. McCarthy O'L.ary which appeared in a South African Journal :—" One of the good old stamp of regimental) officers was Lieutenant-Colonel W. McCarthy O'Leary, of the Ist South Lancashire Regiment, otherwise the 'Prince of Wales's Volunteers.' who was killed at Nelthorpe, in his fifty-first year. He joined the regiment so long ago as 1863, and had served through all the corn missixred grades, till he became Lieutenant-Colonel, some four years ago. El was a man of suporb physique indeed, one of the finest men in the British army—and, though the present Boer war was his first experience of actual fighting, he IVA S an excellent officer, and much beloved by all ranks of the old 40th. As his 'lame implies, he was a member of the gallant sister nation, lyting in fact a J.P. of County Cork. Co!onel O'Leary leaves a widow and large family to lament his loss."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Relieflof Mafeking.—A Pancake</head>
        <p>The Relieflof Mafeking.—A Pancake</p>
        <p>Feast.</p>
        <p>In honour of the relief of Mareking,Mr. R. A. and Mi&amp;s Naylor. invited the members of their Temperance Hall, Thelwall, to a pancake fees.; on Monday evening. Ort the plaiforgi TrAs Illahged a longtable on which gas je's Telo fitted. Round this table were a dozen or more young ladies frying pancakes, while down below willing helpers distributed cups of coffee, cakes, buns, the pan- cak,!s and oranges. Before commencing the feast Miss Naylor read a hymn composed by Mr. R. A. Naylor, which was afterwards sung by all present with fervour. Patriotic songs were sung and cheers were given for General Baden-Powell and Mr and Miss Naylor, and after singing the National Anthem the people slowly diverged. There was also a beautiful show of 133wer5 and plants arranged by Mr. Clarke, the gardener. "Buller Had Some Hard Work."</p>
        <p>Private G. Johnson. D Company, 2nd Devon R,gi- meat, Ladysmith. Natal, writes to his brother (Mr. Alexander Johnson) at Runcorn, under date April 26th. 1900 :—" I have recovered from my wound, and joint d my regiment again. I was wounded on the sth February in the battle of Vaal Krantz. lam sorry to say we have buried three of our volunteers already. I suppose you were surprised to hear of me being out here in the Devon Regiment. We had been out on outpost duty on the 9th, and we had just come in to our camp on the morning of the 10th,and was preparing our mess tins for breakfast:, when the Boers began to shell our camp, killing one o 2 the East Surreys, and two of the Blue Jackets, and I cannot say how many were wounded. I cannot get over this, a. our scouts were out at the time. It occurred at vlandslaagte, and it must be bad manage- ment somewhere, but it is a miracle that no more were killed. I have not received the paper yet, but it will come on. I can assure you that Buller has had same hard work on his side. If people could only see the positions the Boers held, it would open the eyes of the public. lam always glad to get a paper, and I shall be glad when it is all over, for it would surprise you to be here. We are sometimes ten days without a wash, and fancy being served out with water to drink. Another thing is that we have plenty of lodgers in our shirts, which it is impossible te keep clean. I hope I shall live to see you again, when I shall tell you more about this war. but I must say that there is a lot to be done yet. I am sending you a bit called' Kruger's dinner party,' hoping you will like it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Warrington Policeman Having a</head>
        <p>A Warrington Policeman Having a</p>
        <p>Rough Time,</p>
        <p>Writing to P.C. Lewis, of the Warrington PoTic Force, Private S. Howard, a meml”r of the force who i serving at the front with the 3rd Grenadier Guard says :—" We have had a rough time of it since we took Bloemfontein. We have tramped nearly all over the Orange Free State. We have been marching day and night and having a fight with the Boers now and again but they won't stand their ground with us. We came into Bloemfontein yesterday after chasing the Boers for seven days. We drove them up to the borders of Basu- toland thinking that we would get them into a trap but they were too knowing for us. " Once bitten, twice shy." They retired on Ladvbrand and we followed up and drove them away from there. The Orange Free Staters are thoroughly beaten. We are losing a lot of men from sickness. A great many die from fever and pneumonia. I still keep jogging along in the best of health. Perhaps my turn will come in my old days if I rub through without being knocked over. Many times we have stood about the lines all night in the teeming rain with mud over our toot tops. We have been with- out tents for about three months. This is bound to tell upon us some day. I hear that our 2nd Battalion is out here, but I have not seen any of them. I have been waiting very anxiously for them to see some of the old faces. I expect to see Gibbs and Bradley out here.. Now is my only chance to write to you because to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EXAM F'•- EP FRIDAY,C. JUNE 1.. 1900.</head>
        <p>THE EXAM F'•- EP FRIDAY,C. JUNE 1.. 1900.</p>
        <p>morrow we join Lord Roberts again and make an advacc. on Pretoria. I don't think that they (the Boers) will make such a big stand as they did at the beginning of the wari am longing to RPe the Boers crwhed as they are a bid, dirty, idle tot. lam longingl for the.war to come to a close so that I can be with you all in the old town—P.S. Next time I write I hope to be in Pretoria. We are now at BrAndfort ; our camp is outside the town I went into town this mornin-4 to buy some bread when an officer saw me and made me a prisoner for leasing the lines. That is how you ate treated out here. 'Nate is many a good man leaves the army with a bad charac- t!r."</p>
        <p>elect 40 persons to sit on the Earlestown Liberal Coun- cd. This number of names was submitted and duly ap- proved, and the tallowin: wereappointea re?resentAtives on the ex-cutive :—Councillor Evkns, Revs F E Mtlier,E Bocock, Dr Lathatn, Messrs Dibson, B Harrison, J H Profit, J Webster, W Davies, 8 Harvey, and G W R, wh ns.</p>
        <p>After a voi-e of thinks to the Chairman, the meeting was brought to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Nicholson's Nek.</head>
        <p>Nicholson's Nek.</p>
        <p>The last cartridge has been fired. Still the deadly hail continues. Father Matthew, best of padres, goes round amongst the dead and dying, binding up wounds, administering last words of hope and consolation. The Boers are creeping up. There airs no ;more cartridges. May these mules broil in the hottest pit in Tophet ! What shall we to ? " Faugh-a-Ballaghs I Fix bayo- nets! and die like men !" The sleepy, weary, lion- hearted boys jump to their feet. From their parched throats bursts the wild cheer that prefaa-s semi deed of daring hy the lads who clear the way. Die eke men ! Ay, that will we. We will die to a man ; but the Beers shall not live to boast of it. Fx bayonets! A flask; and the ready steel leaps into the scabbard. " Faugh-a-Ballagh !" They are not tired now. For they are no longer to sit still and lie down and be shot like rabbits. Their ceel shall bite deep in this last-- What's that? 'the "Cease fire ?" A look of increchi bus amazement breaks over their faces. "Surrender?' " White flag ?" With an execration so fierce that it almost frightened their officers,the Irishmen swore that, white flag or no white flag, they would never surrender. But it was true enough. A junior officer of the Glouces- ters had ordered the white flag to be displayed on per- ceiving the Boers about to open with artillery. And, though the emblem of surrender had been displayed without the order of the officer commanding, .there was no honourable course open but to keep faith and yield themselves. The scene amongst the Fusiliers was heart- breaking. At first, some scores would net lay down their arms. "Let me kill one of ,em, and Oi'll doi happy." The officers and the goon pri st went amongst them and besought them, and even used force, to make them put their rifles on the ground. "Father I'd rather have been shot than this!" "Oh! What'll the rift/lint think av us.? ' And they wept, and dashed down their arms, and took their belts off and threw them about, and cursed like very fiends of hell, because they had been preventei from dying like men. The black shame of it. We, the, Faugh-e- Ballaghe. We! !! co surrender—and to surrender to these fellows. What will tho regiment think of us ? What will Ireland say of us ? flow shall we ever hold up our heads ag.in ? How shk I fnr sham-'s sake, be ab!e to carry the motto" -Baileeh ?" Lion- hearted,! 'Tait Country shall speak of .eu, hut not with ,e,en. For you have not tarnisLei the glory of your nam-, ner caused to fPcl, one leat of your NutelS.— Frone• Cassell s History of the Boer War" for Jane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Our Naval Plan of Campaign.</head>
        <p>Our Naval Plan of Campaign.</p>
        <p>In the Juno number of " Ca.sell's Alazazine " Mr. D. T. Timing, writing on "Our N tvy " in the first of a series of papers on •.` National Defence," says :—" It seems pretty generally agreed by experts that the next naval war will be conducted ace -irding to what is known as Lord S. Vincent's method.' The principle he lays down is • that the Channel Fleet should, supposing the enemy to be a great maritime Power, be of sufficient force to blockade the fleets of such Power in their ports or to bring them to immediate action should they put to sm, and that there should always be an effective reserve.' The obvious strategic value of this policy is that as long as we are able to carry .it taut we are absolutely safeguarded against invasion, whilst our sea-borne trade is secure from molestation. To give due effect to this plan of naval campaign it has been decided that our battleships, as compared with those of the nemy, should be at least in the proportion of five to three. Of cruisers, at least two should be attached to every battleship to act as scouts. A further number of cruisers should also be retained to protect our merchant shipping, which in time of war would certainly sail only in convoys, for it would be very unlikely that the entire fleet of an enemy would be in borne waters at the time of the outbreak of hostilities. Seeing that our trade is worth nearly 1.000 millions yearly, as against a com- bined total of 360 millions which France and Ruslia would be called upon to defend, it will be readily seen that the provision of an adequate number of cruisers is a very vital matter."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>MILITARY NOTES.</head>
        <p>MILITARY NOTES.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel J. C. Ridgway, commanding the Ist Volunteer Battalion South Lancashire Regi- ment at Warrington, has been granted the honorary rank of Colonel in the Volunteer Force. He had over tberqualifying period of 20 years' service, for he joined the corps in January, 1880; became Cap- tain in 1882; Major in 1893, and was promoted Lieutenant-Colonel in command of the battalion in January, 1895, in succession to Colonel Greenall, who had ben nearly 30 years in command of the battalion. Major Molyneux Seel, who has of late been serving at Warrington in command of the Depot Companies of the Liverpool Regiment, has been appointed 600044 in command of the Ist Battalion Liverpool Regiment.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Frank Hud—son of the Ist Battalion Ox- fordshire Light Infantry, has been given a com- mission as Second Lieutenant in the South Lanca- shire Regiment as a reward for his services in the Boer war and he will join the Ist Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, now in South Africa. In addition Sergeant Cecil Irwin, of the Liverpool Regiment Volunteers, who served with the Ist Bat- talion which took part in the defence of Ladysmith has also been promoted to a Commission becoming Second Lieutenant in the lit Battalion Suffolk Regi- ment now in South Africa.</p>
        <p>Surgeon Lieutenant Colonel Davidson of the sth Volunteer Battulion Cheshire Regiment, has retired from the service. He was appointed Acting Surgeon in the corps in 1876 and became Surgeon Lieutenant Colonel in 1891</p>
        <p>Second Lieutenant Cary, ist 'Volunteer battalion South Laneashire Regiment at Warrington who was lately appointed to do duty with the active service company of Volunteers attached to the Ist battalion South Lancashire Regiment,has now been given the temporary rank of Lieutenant in the Army and will be seconded from his Velunteer battalion. He joined the corps in September 1898; is a very capable officer holding a tactics certiicate. There are now four officers ot the local Volunteer battalion serving in South Africa including Captain Appleton who commands the Volunteer company. Lieutenant IVitherington of the sth Battalion Liverpool Regiment with headquarters at Warring- ton has been appointed to the Ist Battalion Liver- pool Regiment in South Africa as an attached militia officer. Lieutenant Gellibrand of the Ist Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, has been specially promoted to the rank of captain in the Manchester Regiment Captain Gellibrand joined the South Lancashire Regiment as second lieutenant in October 1893,and was promoted lieutenant in 1895. He served with the let Battalion throughout the operations in Natal during the present war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>ENCAMPMENT OF THE 2nd (EARL OF CHESTER'S) V B.C.R.</head>
        <p>THE ENCAMPMENT OF THE 2nd (EARL OF CHESTER'S) V B.C.R.</p>
        <p>Battalion Drill at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The 2nd (Earl of Chester's) V.B.C.R. paraded at Run- corn on Saturday afternoon for battalion drill, under the command of Colonel Smith. The battalion was made up as follows :—Chester six companies, Runcorn two, Weaverhana and Hartford two, and Frodsham one, The visiting companies arrived about 340 p.m.. and were joined at the railway station by the Runcorn detach met Besides Colonel Smith there were present :—Majors Gamma and Harrison. Captains Thompson, Evans, Bromley, and Ashton ; Lives. Nicholson, B urton , D a vid- son. Hodge, Dickson, Smith, Linaker. and Clarke. The strength f the battalion was about 600 non-commissioned officers and men. When the respective companies had been arranged in marching order, they proceeded by Lowlands-road, High-street, Bridge-street, and Irwell-lane to the Norton Marsh. the band of the regiment, under the direction of Bandmaster Oathwaite, Runcorn, leading the way with patriotic music. The arrival of the vo:unteers was witnessed by a large concourse of people, who accom- panied them to the marsh to witness battalion drill. At the close of the manceuvres Colonel Smith presented Sergeant Williams and Sergeant Sullivan (Chester), and Sergeant T Holmes and Corporal J Marsh CRuncorn), with long service medals. Refreshments were served out, and the battalion at length returned to the station, the visiting companies entraining for their respective destinations, while the Runcorn detachment proceeded to their headquarters, where they were dismissed. The strength of the Runcorn detachment was about 160, in- cluding band and buglers. On Saturday next the battalioa go under canvas for the annual encampment. This year the camp will he at Salisbury Plain and will extend for a month it being compulsory for every unit to put in 14 days' training. The Runcorn Detachment will parade at 1 45 on Saturday, and subsequently depart from Rut earn by the 2.27,train. En route they will be joined by,the Froiaham Companies who parade at 1.50. and join the train at Frodshana Station at 2.36 Whilst in camp the men will receive Army pay and their wives is id a day with an allow- ance of 2cl per day for each child. The men will also receive an ostra is a day from the battalion funds for every day after the first two weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EAR LESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES. At the Earlestown Police station on Monday. be- fore Mr J. W. Emmett, Henry White,collier, Newton Common, was charged with being drunk on Satur- day night and was fined 2s 61 and costs. THE PROPOSED NEW PAVILION. The subscriptions for the prapoled new pavilion on the Newton-le- Willows Recreation Ground now amount in the aggregats to £379 Si. Among the additional subscriptions since the last were pub- lished Colonel R Pilkingtou has promised £5 54; Mr W Bradshaw, £5 ; Rev Canon Pearhyn, £5; Sankey Sugar Company, £5; and tile Vulcan Fouudry Cnoe- pany, Limited, £5. ROMAN CATHOLIC SUNDAY SCHOOL. The annual school sermons in connection with Ft. Mary's and St. John's Roman Catholic Church, Newton-le-Willows, were preached by the Rev. Father Buckley, of Liverpool, in the morning, and the Rev. Father Morgan, of Buttonwood, in this evening. In the morning Gounod's Mass was ren- dered, and the music at the evening service was specially composed by Mr.S. Iligham,of Earlestown. VULCAN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL The usual annual services in aid of the above school were held on Sunday afternoon and evening. Tnere were very fair congregations and a couple of interesting sermons were preached by the Rev. D. Jones of St. Helens. Special hymns were sung by the children and Miss Rebecca Mills sang one hymn as a solo. At the svening service Mr, Twemlow sang 1* The Holy City," and Mr. James Plett gave in excellent voice " Glory to thee," and Miss tiettie Ada Taylor sang very nicely the hymn "I belong to Jesus." Mr. W. B. Grundy presided at the har- monium. ibe collecteons during the day amounted to £37s. Od. EARLESTOWN BAPTIST SUNDAY SCHOOL. The annual special services in ski of the above schond were held in the chapel on Sunday. The day was beautifully fine,and there were large congrega- tions at each of the services. The pulpit was occu- pied morning and evening by the Rev. Vaugean Thomas, who preached eloquent and instructive sermons. Special anthems and hymns were sung by the choir which was augmented by a number of scholars specially trained. In the afternoon a sacred cantata entitled "The Galilean" was given by the choir,and was much enjoyed by the large congrega- tion present. The collections throughout the day amounted to over £46. GOOD TEIMPL &amp;HY. The Hope of Earle4rown bodge meeting was held in the Co-operative Hall, Wargrave-road, on Monday evening, Bro Cutler, C.T., presiding. One member was restored to membership. Bro Woods read notice of Convention to he held at Aehton. The pro- gramme being a mu .icel iiih, Bro Willis respon- sible, the Lodge was opened to visitors •' the pro- gramme being opened with a piano solo by Bro H Reberts, entitled "A Summer Ride " ; Sis Thomas sang with good taste "Oh say not, that love is bought " ; Master C ilazeldine; in a manner that promises well for the future, gave a cornet solo "The Red, White and Blue," havine to respond to. an encore; song," Juanita," Sis A Newman ; piano solo, Bro Willis; song, "Come and join the Temper- ance Army," Bro G Clarke; song," There is beauty all around, Sis. Thomas; song, "Genevieve," Sis Newman; cornet solo "Stand like the brave," Master C Hazeldine ; Master Harold Roberts sang in a pleasant manner, though somewhat nervous, the sacred song " He wipes the tear from every eye"; song, "Cheer boys, Cheer," Sis Clark; hamorous song, "The Coon Brigwie Mr Huddleston. DETERMINED SUICIDE AT EARLESToWN. At the Town Hell, Earlestown, on Monday after- noon, Mr S Brighouse, coroner, held an enquiry into the circumstances attending the death of James Holland Lea, 33 years of age, a fitter. of 190 Com- mon-road, ,Earlestown. From the police statement it appeared that the deceased had suffered from in- flammation of the lungs since the previous Monday and had been attended by Dr. Valentine's locum tenens About three o'clock on Friday afternoon last he be- came much worse.,and appeared to be in very great pain.At 4.20 he asked a person named Ellen Chubb, who bad assisted in attending upon him, to go for a doctor. Sha left for that purpose and he then turned to his wife and asked her to get him a drink of water. Immediately afterwards he got out of bed and went towards a dresser. His mother, who was in the room, inquired of him what it was he wanted, to which be replied that it was his pipe.—She did not believe him and forced him back upon the bed. He became violent, and rushed past her, secured a penknife from the dresser and deliberately cut his throat, from the effects of which he died within five minutes. His mother in endeavouring to prevent him, was badly cut about the hands.—Mre. Annie Lea, mother of deceived, corroborated the police evidence and said she had no idea that he had any intention of taking his life.— A verdict of "Suicide whilst temporarily insane" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>--..-- ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>--..-- ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED HIGHWAY ROBBERY.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Police Court on Monday, before Coun- cillor Leach and Mr. J. Cotton, Patrick Kelly, of 133, Freierick-stmet, was charged with highway robbery with violence.—James Hurst, of 67, Cowley-street, a clerk at Messrs. Pidrington's,said that while going home about ten o'clock on Saturday night, by way of Birchley- street and the open space behind Mr. Swift's bakthouse, he was accosted by tour men, one of whom assaulted him. He was left on the ground dazed. When he got home he was covered with blood, and found that 14s. had bcen tak In from his pockets.—Prisoner said he :1-.ruck Hurst atter he lascl dared him to do so, but he did not tako the money. Hurst was drunk, and lost the rnonec.---The magistrates said they could not prc,?ed on the evidence, and pris )ner was discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>LIBERAL MEETING AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETING AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>A LTheral organisation meeting in connection with the Newton Pelting Division of the Liberal Association was held in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, Earlestown, on Monday evening. The Rev F. E. Miller preaided,and he was supported by the Rev E C Bocoek, H B Shores, and C R Aldrick of Hinton. The Chairman said they were met for a two fold purpose, one was to continue the organisation of the Literal forces in this neighbourheod and the other to listen to a lecture upon an important subject. Teey had to congratulate themselves, upon their condition and upon being able to hold that meeting. It was not so large a meeting as they woeld wish, but in considering its size they had to consider the difficult circumstances under which a meeting was held in Eirleetown on a week night, and especially early in the ' week. The effort to bring the party into line was an important one, and they must consider it was not a spasmodic effort, but an earnest concerted attempt to put the party into the state of organisation in which i; ought to be. Tney had a most capable organiser in Mr. Shores and there had been a great improvement in the party since Mr. Shove took the reins. They moat rest assured that what had been done was only a beginning and those veto had the matter in hand would persevere wite their wok, and whether they felled encourage- meet or not, they would continue, and Liberals would be able to give a good account of themselver when the nesce, election was held. (Hear, hear.) When the election would be, depeaded upon tbe speed with which Lord Rob-rts would carry the flag to Pretoria, mid there seined to be an impression that the Government would snatch the popularity which would come to teem, and continue to misgovern the coentry for sonic yieirs len !et. "Ihe militeryisuccess:might mean success all aim the line, and they might be able to get such a majerity in the House of Commons, as would help tt ell to maintain office for tsolll9 years longer. But the election when it did come would be more sweeping and striking and all the more lasting, and when the cou try i- al to submit to such a Government as we had to submit to f&amp;r the last few years, the country could be relied upon to put in Ministers who had at heirt the greatest good to the greatest number. (Hear, hear.) They might during the excitement of the war forget matters which sleould reed attention. NI h ttever might be the advan- tages obtained in the long run, it was worth while to consider the price which was being paid for it, and if they had had a Government :more gifted, with more sagacity, more commonsense, and not content to take toe mere Ptandpoint of the,man in the street,and states- men to deal with the question not quite so rash, so wi ful and so strong headed as Mr. Chamberlain it might have been possible to secure the end by peaceful means. (Hear, hear.) People would begin to realise that what- ever the advantagelof the Government policy of the war might be it would be for them to come hack to domestic questions. An address was then given by Mr. C. R Aldrick on the " Housing of the Poor," which was listened to with great interest. 0 e the motion of the Rev E. Becock, seconded by Mr. W. Dsviee a vote of thanks was accorded Mr. Aldrick.</p>
        <p>Mr H. B. Shore said the condition of the party in the Newton Division had undergone censiderable change since the last rvistration. The facts were patent on the Thl result of thsir registration work was very carefully tabulated, and he had presented to his Com- mitt.e a truthful statement. Mr Holland-Owen, on behalf of the Conservatives, had published an exaggera- ted statement, and he challenged Mr Owen'e figures, but be declined the challenge; It was now necessary to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>DOUBLE DROWNING CASE AT ST.</head>
        <p>DOUBLE DROWNING CASE AT ST.</p>
        <p>HELENS.</p>
        <p>Distressing Scene at the Inquest. A Self-Accused Mother.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held an adjourned in- (pest at till Luc )motive Inn, Sutton, St. delfts, on. Woleteada.y. oil the bones of Lily Welton, aged seven pars, iind Agnes May Walton. aged tnre3 year 4, the ceilitren of Jotin Walton, of 15, Sutton Moss, who were tiro gned in the reservoir near the Sutton Rolling Mills on Thursday, the 17th inst., under sad cir- cums!ances. The inquiry • was op need on the following Tuesday morning and adjourned un- til yesterday oa account of the condition of Mrs. I'Vaitou, who was suffering from her tomer- Biota. Dr. H. B. Bates spoke to making a post-mortem examination of the bodies. Death was due to drowning. —Archibald Thompson said that on the 'Tours lay night be beard cries corning from the reservoir in Hulling Mill- lane about twenty rn•nu:es past eight o'clock. He went to the water and saw Mrs Walton splashing about in the water with the elder child in her arm about half a dozen yards from the side. There were a number of men .on the bank, but thy did not go into the, water to teieue mean Witness jumped into the shallow part and caught Mrs. Walton by her shawl and dragged her out of the , deep water into the shallow, and, witn the aid of Ed ward Williams, lifted her out. He thought the eider child was alive, but did not see Agnes May.—Further evidence Was given by Edward William, John Forster, Mary Jane Thompson, Mrs. Arkwright, and Mrs. A 4r:in.—Thomas Heirhorn, 6, Berry',lane., sail Mrs. Walton went to his house who a coal ticket. See was carrying one of the children in a shawl. There was nothing strange about her.—Mrs. Walton was next called, and appeared in a very dazed candition. The Coroner asked her how she canee to get in the wateryand quite a sensation WAS caused when Mrs. Walton said in a wandering a airier "It is no use me saying. I have done the deed." Toe Coroner: What do you mean ? Mrs. Walton : I mean I have done it.—Aiter a conver- sation with Dr. Bates, the Coroner said it wou'd be very unfair te Mrs. Walton, Seeing the condi ioa she WA in, if they proceeded with the inquest. He th )u2lst the proper course to take would be to adjourn the inquiry for three weeks or a mouth. He could not ask them to return an open verdict in the face of the statement Mrs. Walton had made or to return a verdict of accidental death. —Mrs. Walton here asked to be taken out, and intimated to too coroner and Dr. Bates that she wished to speak to them. Mrs Walton was assisted into another room, where she made a state- ment —The Otirouer afterwards came back to the jury, and said he was going to edjourn tie ioquiry for a month. It was only fair to tell them that when Mrs. Walton had made that statement her mind might be unhinged. They had ajourned the inquiry for a month in order that she naigh'a recover. He galled Mr. Walton, and advised him to seek the services of a solicitor to defend his wife at the adjourned inquest. She had voluntarily made a statement todDr. Bates and himself, but he was not going to say what it was at present. Mrs. Waitron's extraordinary confession produced a great sensation among the jury and police officials Pre- sent at the inquest. It also created the wildest excitement throughout the district of Sutton, where the peculiar circumstances connected with the drowning of the two children have been much discussed. Immed- iately on the closing of the inquest the woman was put in acab and taken to the police office at the Town Hall where she will be det:itted. The Coroner and Chief Constable iteerviewed the woman and her husband, and Dr. Reid, the police-surgeon, was called to exemine her as to her mental condition. It is expected that she will be brought before the magistrates in due course on the charge, on her own confession, of causing the deaths of the two children.</p>
        <p>Dr. Bates' who has attended Mrs. Walton for pleurisy and shock since the sad ccatirrence, expresses the opinion that she is at present non compos mentis. The drowning of the two children aroused suspicion at the time, but when the whole of the circumstances were gone closely into everything seEmed reasonably correct. She stated that after b?ing at Mr. Hentnorne's shop with the coal ticket, she was going back home when the little girl began to cry. She turned back and bought some toffee. Both children were given some. Oa the way back the girl of seven was walking behind her sucking toffee when she fell in the water. She laid the little girl down by the water and went in to rescue the big girl, when the younger one rolled in. Dr. Bates says he was thunderstruck when Mrs. Walton made her statement at the inquest ;in fact, everybody was. She made it without being pressed at all. The coroner was very gentle indeed. He simply asked her how she came to go home by the reservoir,when she immediately cried out," It Is no use going any further ; I did the deed." Previous to this Mrs Walton, in her conversations with the doctor, the neighbours, and the police-sergeant had never hinted at anything of the sort, and Dr. Bites regards this sudden and awful confession as the act ef a mad person. He thinks it would be exceedingly difficult for the woman to have got a girl of seven far enough into the water to drown her. He considers that the hospital is the proper place for Mrs.- Walton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>FATALITY AT WESTON POINT. ---- A Dock-weigher Killed. THE JURY MAKES A RECOMMENDATION.</head>
        <p>FATALITY AT WESTON POINT. ---- A Dock-weigher Killed. THE JURY MAKES A RECOMMENDATION.</p>
        <p>Coroner R. Dobson held ar... inque3t at the Runcorn Police Court, on Mcnday afternoon, touching the death of Alfred John Lawton, aged 52,a weigher in toe employ of the Weaver Navigatim Trustees, who was iild by a large piece of china-stone falling upon him whilst h#3 was following his employment at Weston Point Dock, on Friday laat.—Mr. Joseph Burton, solicitor, Runcorn, re- presented the deceased's relatives ; Mr. Louis Rosenberg the Weaver Trust Ere ; Mr. Eirnt (Liverpool), the H.M. Factories Inspector; and Mr. T. P. Derbyshire (claims inspector) the Ocean, Accident and Guarantee Cc-.;pera- tion, Limited.</p>
        <p>Evidence of identification was given by Mr. William Henry Lawton, 6. Clarence-street. Deceased was his father and was 52 years of age. He had been in the employ of the Weaver Trustees as a weigher for nine or ten years, and was a man of good hearing. Thomas Dewson, 6, Cross-street, and John Griffin, 5, Granville-street, both labcurers, also employed by the Weaver Trustees testified to being engaged in the hold of the as. " Deerhound " which vessel they were dis- charging of china stone, on Friday last. Deceased was engaged on the dock side weighing. They were engaged by the trustees upon the tonnage system, and were paid by results. It was true they sometimes loaded the tubs " up, but the particular tub out of which the stone fell that caused the deceased's death, was in their opinion loaded quite safely. It was as mueh t) their interest as anyone else's to load them safely. Neither witness could see what occurred after the" tub" wa3 wound out of the hold and swung over the quay.— Griffin added, on hearing a shout he got out of the held of the vessel and came te the deceased whom he found lying on the quay. The stone was lying behind the stend on the weighing machine. _</p>
        <p>Henry Stubbs, Weston Point, said he was 16 years of age, and had been employed as a crane driver by the Weaver trustees for the past 18 months. H. had never had anything drop out ot his tubs before. He sa w the particular tub, which ha lifted with the hydraulic crane about 19 feet from the quay. %ben clear of the rigging of the vesel he swung the cram over to the weighing machine ; and, as he was about to lower, the stone fell off. He saw the stone, and in his opinion it was net safe. Shortly after the stone fell he heard a cry, hut could not tell what had happened until he came out of the crane box. Ths particular tub out of which the stone fell had a "spin" upon it,but as it did not catch in any- thing he could not tell why it was spinning. The weight of the s-",one would be about lAcwt.—Mr. Rosenberg said the stone had been weighed, and was lot. 2 quarters, 221bs.</p>
        <p>Dr. John Rebineen,Ruecern, said he had seen the holy of the deceased!whom he had known for the last 30 years. He saw him a little before nine o'clo k on Friday morn- ing at his home. He was then alive and breathing, but unconscious. Deceased was paralysed, cold, and unconscious, and did not recover, in fact t e -vas dying. He had that morning made an external examination. He found the right side of the chest—the right shoulder and lower portion of the chest—had upon it marks of a tremendous blow. In the lower mart of the chest rib 3 were broken, and the chest itself was in a pulpy cond.tion. The right cellar bore was broken, as also was the left thigh, and there was a gash three inches long on the left leg lellew the knee. On the right fore arm there was an external dis- colouration ,ad the knuckles of both hands were abrased. 'There was an abrasien on the kneecap of the left knee. In his opinion the injuries would be clused by a stone falling upon the deceased in the manner described by the evidence. .Probably it struck the deceased on the right tile with such force as to break his left thigh. Mr. T. Dean. foreman of the jury, intimated after a brief retirement, that the jury held the unanimous ver- dict "tint the deceased met hie death accidentally." and were of the opinion that the accident occurred through the Nue stone being placed on the top of the tub ; they rec-immended that in future large stones should be put at the bottom or middle of the tub and not on the t)p. Mr. Rosenberg said he would take note of any recom- mendation, and would place the matter before the directors of the Weaver Trustees.</p>
        <p>LADIBS BRST GLUM BOOTS AND SNOBS UNDER TITS CLOCK, BRIDGE-ST/MT.—Advt.</p>
