      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>NEW WORKHOUSE INFIRMARY,</head>
        <p>THE NEW WORKHOUSE INFIRMARY,</p>
        <p>Formal Opening,</p>
        <p>The impoAng blo:11{ of new buildings at the rear of the Warrington Workhouse and which are to be used as an infirmary were formally opened on Thursday afternoon by the Right Hon. Sir J. T. Elibbert, K.C.B. (president of the North-Western Poor Law Conference and Chairman of the Lanca- shire County Council). The day was beautifully fine and the interesting ceremony was witnessed by a large number of represer.tative public man. The visitor could noz; fail to be impressed with the massiveness of the structure which has been erected, and in this instance, at. least, the Guardians cannot be charged with not having provided for the future wants of the Union. From the description prepared by Messrs. William and Segar Owen, the architects, we learn that the infirmary stands upon 52 acres of land, apart from the land belonging to the workhouss premises. Three blocks of buildings are now complete, some 70 feet apart,but connected on each floor with open air corridors. The central block is for administrative purposes and stands three stereya high, and the two outer blocks are ward pavilions,two storeys high. Under the whole of the buildinge and corridors there is a basement for access to the heating and water pipes, and for special ventilation and the securing of dryness in the rooms.,</p>
        <p>The administrative block gives accominolation on the uround fl3or for the Aledicat Officer and the Lady S''uperintendent, with dispensary, waiting room, nurses' sitting and dining room, linen and other stores, and on the first and second floors a large number of bedrooms and other apartments far the housing of the nursing staff, with both lava- tories and closets. From this administrative build- ing access is obtained by the open air corridors into the male and female pavilions. The nurses will re- main in the pavilions during the time they are on duty, and will return to the administrative block when not on duty. Access can also be obtained from the administrative block to the different airing yards where convalescents cam sit out or take exercise.</p>
        <p>The two side blocks, or war,l pavilions, are each similar in arrangem3nt and each gives accommoda- tion for 92 beds, a total of 184. In each block there are two wards of 24 beds, two wards of 12 b3ds,t wo wards of four beds, and six wards of two beds each, and each sat of wards has its separate arrangement of bath, lavatories and closets. There are in addi- tion ward kitchens,linen rooms, separation lobbies, and lifts for patients. The wards are hosted by open fireplaces in the case of the smaller ones, and by central open fire Musgrave stoves in the large ones; but in all cases the heating is angmented by hot-water pipes or radiators.</p>
        <p>The floors throughout are fire-proof of Messrs, Homan and Rodgers construction, and although the building is well equipped with stone stairdases, there are also outer alternative stairs of iron from each pavilion, giving choice of two staircases in case of fire.</p>
        <p>When further accommodation is required, it will be obtained by erecting other pavilions. the present buildings being so placed to admit of same being erected on either side, and .which will be again reached by open air corridors in continuance of the present line, and the total accommodation of the Infirmary, with the two additional pavilions, will be 320 beds. The administrative block is built already of such a size that it will accommodate the nursing staff for the present and future pavilions. The general cooking for the Infirmary will at pre- sent be carried-out at the Workhouse kitchens.</p>
        <p>The premises are lighted by electricity through- out, generated by boilers, engines and dynamos in a separate building on the premises, which, are not yet complete. The electric lighting and plant has been carri6d out from the specifications, and under the superintendence of Mr. T L. Miller, E.. 6 ,of Tower Buildings, Liverpool. by the Warrington and District Electric Light and Power Company, and Messrs Tinker's Limited, and Messrs Billick and Cochrane of Liverpool. The general contract for the whole building, and for the boiler and engine house have been carried out by Mr C. W. Davenport, of Stockton Heath,with the assistance of the following sub-contractors : Mr William Winetanley, plumbing, Mr Osborne, plastering, Messrs C W Williams, tiling; Messrs Williams and Clay,grates; Messrs Twyford and Com- pany, sanitary fittings; Messrs Homan and Rodgers fireproof floors; Messrs Killick and Cochrane, heat- ing and hot water, and Messrs Clarke, Bunnett and Company, lifts, the work throughout being from the designs and erected under the superintendence of Messrs William and Segar Owen, architects. The infirmary has been furnished on the most im- proved principles and everything has been aone to secure tee comfort of the inmates. The total cost is about £40,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WIDNES FOOTBALL CLUB Annual Meeting.</head>
        <p>WIDNES FOOTBALL CLUB Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Widnes KC. was held in the Conservative Club Assembly-rooms on Wedneclay evening. There was a goad attendance. The minutes of last season's general meeting were confirmed. The balance sheet shower" that the gate money totalled £764 83 4.'; athletic club,£ss; works' competition gate money. £lOB 93 61; members' subscriptions and donations,£l34 16s; making a total, with several minor sums, of £l,llO 03 9. Thl chief items of expersa were :—Field rx- pecses, £BO 193 71 ; wages and lost time. £396 us 61; railway and hotel expenses, £143 53 10d ; players' teas, &amp;c., £7O ; clothes, £73; insurance, £6l 123—•otal ex- penditure,£l,26B is 41, showing a deficit of £l5B on the season's working.—Mr H Wane Deacon was elected pre- sident. The voting for the secretary resulted in Mr E M`Naughten's election by 133 votes to 37. First team captain, F Hammon ; A team captain, T ; hon. treasurer, F W Wright ; financial secretary, J C Evans. —A notice of motion by Mr F W Wright, seconded by Mr E M`Nau,ghton, that the annual meeting be held in June, instead of May, was pieced unanimously.—The hoticn proposed by D Brennan, seconded by A T Kyle, that the Widnes Football Club have full control of the works competition matches was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>KINGSLEY.</head>
        <p>KINGSLEY.</p>
        <p>A VILLAGE REJOICES AT THE FALL OF JOHANNE3BERG Oa behalf of the fall of Johannesberg the on evening of Tuesday last almost the whole of the village inhabitants congregated and marched in preeossion to the National Schools. On arrival there they torew atones at the bell as an attempt to ring it,but th,s was improved uwn by a, happy suggestion to ring up the schoolmaster and get the hell properly tolled. This success caused them to continue their march, and the Parish Clerk was next visited and had his repose abruptly di turbed, nor was he allowed any peace until he got up and undertook to ring the Parish Church boll. Having got the two bells ringing, the crowd then wound up the proceedings in parading the village singing patriotic songs and " God savo ti;:e Quren."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-06-01_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>WHIT-MINDAY.—As is usual,at Fro isharn,the Friendly Societies will parade thE village during the rnorning,and attend divine servic,.</p>
        <p>INQUEST.—On Thursday Mr. T. M. Dutton, deputy coroner,beld an inquest on the body of James Ainsworth, 62 years of age. labourer, of Main-street, Frodsham, whou sudden demise we reported last week.—A verdict of death from heart disease was returned. OVERTON BILLS.—In the beautiful weather that is now prevailing the Overton Hills present as great an attraction as they ever did, and hundreds of visitors daily resort there to enjoy the bracing air and magnifi- c-nt scenery. Mrs. Parker ['lose with her usual fore- thonght for the comfort and enjoyment of those who patronise the hills, has arranged for Mr. Barton's War- rington Baud to play there for dancing on Monday, Wednesday and Saturday in Wait week.</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>We understand that the---(4;1"-een has conferred the Volunteer Officers' decoration upon Lieutenant- Colonel John Clare Ridgway, commanding the Ist Volunteer Battalion. Prince of Wales's Volunteers (South Lancashire Regiment). MessrF. Henry Thornton and Sons head the local Indian Famine List with a donation of £2OO.</p>
        <p>A CHANGE FOR THE BETTER is the change you want when the blood gets out of condition, and Dr. JACOB TOWNSEvD'S SARSAPARILLA performs the needful with splendidly pleasing efficacy and comfort. Tar IT. Whinges couidn't say nom—Advt.</p>
      </div>
