        <p>about London, the local consumption would, the Directors believe, amount to at least four times the present output,and it is therefore proposed to increase the plant to deal with an output of 20 millions per ennum. For the purposes of this Company the value of the clay on the property is calculated only to the depth of 40 feet, and at this depth it vtill yield about 25 million bricks per acre, these figures being the recognised quantities at this depth. The Directors are advised that, with the large area at disposal, the clay can be conveniently worked to a depth of 80 feet, or even deeper. Taking the foregoing valuation of Mr. Alexander, together with the Working Capital provided by the present issue, there is available as security for the Debenture issue : Value of Estate Working Capital provided by this issue 5,000 Total security The price payable by the Company for the property,includ- ing all fixtures, plant, and rolling stock (Stock, Stores and Goodwill), is £45.W°. of which the Vendors (who are the Promoters of the Company, and sell the properties to it at a profit) have stipulated that the Company shall allot to them 1b,0(..0 Ordinary shares in part payment of the purchase money, and the balance in cash. 'the whole of the expenses of the formation and registration of the Company up to the first allotment will be borne by the Vendors, except stamp duties and the costs of conveysuce, which will be paid by the Company. The ameunt reserved out of the present issue for Working Capital will be £5,000, which will be sufficient to fully equip the works to cope with the anticipated additional output,and to carry on the business to its full capacity. The following Contracts have been entered into Agreement, dated dated the 20th day of February. 1900, lotween Herbert Carpenter of the one part, and the Industrial and Concessions Corporation, Limited, of the other part. Agreement, dated the 21st day ef February, 1930, between the said Industrial acid Concessions Co,poration. Limited, of the one part, and Frederick Charles Vernon. as Trustee for the Company, of the other part. Mr. Herbert Carpenter has consented to stet as Managing Director for a period of not less than five years at a remunera- tion of £5OO per annum. The business will be taken over subject to all existing Contracts. These are of the ordinary trade character, and necessary for the working of the concern ; but including, as they do, Contracts with the employees,customers, and others, they cannot, in the interests of the Company, be specified, There are also other Contracts relating to the formation of the Company,and subscriptions to the Capital and otherwise, which may technically fall within Section 38 of 'S The Com- panies Act, 1867," or otherwise, Applicants will be held to have had notice of all Contracts, and to have waived all rights to be supplied with particulars of such Contracts, and to have *reel with the Company, as Trustees for the Directors and other persons liable, not to make any claim whatsoever,or to take any proceedings under the said section ot otherwise in respect of any non•compliance therewith,and allotments will only be made on that footing. Applications for Debenture Stock should be made on the forms enclosed, and forwarded to the Bankers of the Com- pany, at their Head Office or Branches, with the amount of the depo.it. If no allotment is made, the deposit will be returned in full ; and where the amount of Debenture Stock allotted is less than the amount applied for, the balance will be applied towards payment due on allotment, and any excess will be returned to the applicant. The Company reserves the right to from day to day allot the Debentute Stock, either in the order of the applications or otherwise. Failure to pay any instalment on Debenture Stock will render the previous payments liable to forfeiture. Copies of the Prospectus, with forms of Application for Debenture Stock, can be obtained at the Offices of the Com- pany, or from their Bankers. Brokers, or Solicitors. The Memorandum and Articles of Association, the Con- tracts and Report specified above, together with plans of the property, can be seen at the offices of the Company. London, May 18th, 1900.</p>
