        <p>himself had stood with six or seven friends for more than 20 minutes and had never been Interfered with by the police. It was palpable on the face of it that the men were brought up not because they were obstructing, but simply because they were drinking the beer. —The defen- dants gave eviderce.—A.lfred (Jeeps said that Keepers-lane was a cart road and they were standing there about five minutes. People could pass along the road.—Bv Superinten- dent Brassington : He knew there was a notice saying that the road was a public footpath. Other defendants gave similar evidence, but in eross-extmin ttion it was admitted that on the offiler coming up all the defendants with the exception of Gee and Jackson. went away.— The Bench coesiderel that an obstruction was caused and fined the defendants is and costs each. BREVITIES.—The usual batch of inebriates were dealt with —John Jenes was on the evidence of P.O. Dagger fined Is and costs for riding a bicycle on the footpath in Penny-lane, Haydock on the 17th o! slay;! Miehael Colony was fined is and costs for discharging missiles from a catapult In Millfield- lane. Haydock on the 17th of May. A COMPROMISE.—Richard Fearns was summoned for persistent cruelty to his wife Emily Fearns on the 25th May and on previous dates.—He pleaded guilty.—Mr. Littler, of Leigh appeared on behalf of complainant and Mr. Riley, of St Helens represented defendant.—Mr. Littler said that the parties had come to an agreement that there should be a separation order with an allowance of 12s a week and defendant should pay the costs of the case.—Mr. Riley on behalf of the defendant thought the best plan was to submit to the order in the hope that perhaps after a period of refl-ction the parties might live together again.—lhe magistrates agreed to this course.</p>
